var e={notice:`The data here is/may be copyrighted and NOT included in the GPLv3 license. Converted from MegaMek's unit files (https://github.com/MegaMek/megamek): MegaMek Data (C) 2025-2026 by The MegaMek Team, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).`,shared:[{engine:`InterComBust 180 ICE`,armor:`BAR 6`},{$k:[`tag`,`parameter`],$v:[[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[`quirk-fast-reload`,null],[`quirk-obsolete`,2580],[`quirk-narrow-low-profile`,null],[`quirk-poor-performance`,null]]},{front:22,left:22,right:22,rear:12,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`feedsWeaponTag`],{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`fusion`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:1,chassisModifications:[],bar:6,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`advanced`},[`seating-standard`,`#0`],[`seating-standard`,`#1`],[`seating-standard`,`#2`],[`seating-standard`,`#3`],[`seating-standard`,`#4`],[`seating-standard`,`#5`],[`seating-standard`,`#6`],{manufacturer:`Rebel Industrial Technologies Inc.`,primaryFactory:`Mars`,targeting:`Advanced Fire Control`},[`quirk-improved-communications`,null],[`small-laser`,`front`,`#0`,null],[{tag:`cargo`,amount:.5,doors:1}],[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`],[`communications-equipment`,`#0`,null],`remote-sensor-dispenser`,[`hi-res-imager`,`#2`,`front`],{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`fusion`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:.5,chassisModifications:[],bar:5,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`none`},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`halfTon`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`machine-gun`,`turret`,`#0`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#2`,!0,`machine-gun`],[`quirk-hard-to-pilot`,null],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`,null],[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null],[`lrm-5`,`turret`,`#2`,null],`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,[{tag:`cargo`,amount:7,doors:1}],[{tag:`cargo`,amount:3,doors:1}],{front:1,left:1,right:1,rear:1,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[{tag:`cargo`,amount:.019,doors:1}],{manufacturer:`Pacific-Euro Consolidated Aerospace Systems`,primaryFactory:`Terra`},`The Cobra VTOL Transport traces its origins to the formative years of the Terran Hegemony's military organization, commissioned from Pacific-Euro Consolidated Aerospace Systems on Terra in 2317. The design emerged from Director-General James McKenna's sweeping initiative to replace the fragmented procurement practices of the Alliance Global Militia with a standardized, purpose-built military equipment base. McKenna's vision demanded a light transport capable of operating across the full range of Hegemony operations, from battlefield resupply runs to rear-area logistics, and the tilt-rotor Cobra answered that specification with a design that proved both practical and adaptable. Capable of delivering troops, supplies, and light vehicles to contested forward positions and evacuating casualties on the return, the Cobra defined Hegemony military transport operations for most of the twenty-fourth century and established the tilt-rotor transport template that would echo through centuries of successor designs.`,`The Cobra VTOL Transport uses an internal combustion engine driving a tilt-rotor assembly for propulsion, achieving a cruise speed of approximately 130 kilometers per hour. While this performance lagged behind faster VTOL designs of the period, the Cobra's strength lay in its carrying capacity rather than raw speed. The spacious internal hold accommodates nine tons of cargo and is fitted with three access doors, right, left, and rear, allowing rapid loading and offloading in the field. The cargo bay accepts interchangeable kit packages, allowing units to reconfigure the vehicle for troop transport, medical evacuation, reconnaissance, airborne command post, or VIP duties without dedicated maintenance facilities.

The vehicle also supports a slung load beneath the airframe, extending its utility to delivery of light artillery pieces, small vehicles, or supplemental bulk cargo alongside its internal carry. For self-defense, four machine guns cover multiple arcs and are supported by an advanced fire control system to improve crew targeting effectiveness under operational conditions. BAR-6 composite armor provides adequate protection for a transport operating in contested airspace, though the Cobra relies primarily on speed and escort for survivability in active combat environments. A three-person crew handles flight, vehicle operations, and gunnery during missions.`,`The Cobra VTOL Transport's development came at a defining moment in Terran Hegemony history. Director-General James McKenna's rise to power inaugurated a new era of military standardization across what had been the loosely organized Alliance Global Militia. Prior to McKenna's reforms, equipment procurement had been inconsistent at best, with contracts regularly awarded for designs already rejected by the military hierarchy. The Cobra was among the earliest products of the new mandate requiring uniform standards, with Pacific-Euro Consolidated Aerospace Systems receiving the production contract and beginning manufacture in 2317.

The design proved an immediate success and remained the standard Hegemony VTOL transport for most of the twenty-fourth century. Field conversion kits developed in parallel with the original model allowed individual Cobras to be rapidly reconfigured for medical evacuation, airborne command posts, VIP transport, and reconnaissance missions without factory support. The design's adaptability encouraged a continuous stream of specialized configurations, and hundreds of examples entered service across all branches of Hegemony forces. Many decommissioned Cobras eventually found their way into civilian hands, serving government agencies, commercial operators, and the civilian aftermarket as workhorses for nearly every conceivable transport purpose.

By the latter decades of the twenty-fourth century, advancing aerospace technology and the introduction of more capable designs had begun displacing the original Cobra in front-line service. The final military production orders, fulfilled in 2489, incorporated a fusion power plant that improved performance margins and extended the design's useful service life before the original line was discontinued entirely. The Cobra VTOL Transport did not survive to witness the founding of the Star League, but its influence persisted in Hegemony operational records and engineering documentation that directly informed Aldis Industries' development of the Star League-era Cobra Transport VTOL. The tilt-rotor transport concept the Cobra pioneered would prove remarkably enduring, with echoes of its basic design philosophy visible in transport VTOLs produced centuries later across the Inner Sphere.`,{$k:[`tag`,`parameter`],$v:[[`quirk-vtol-rotor-arrangement`,`dual`],[`quirk-obsolete`,2470],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`,null]]},{front:10,left:8,right:8,rear:6,turret:0,rotor:2,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`halfTon`,`feedsWeaponTag`],$v:[[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#1`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`right`,`#2`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`left`,`#3`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`rear`,`#4`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#5`,!0,`machine-gun`]]},{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`ice`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:0,chassisModifications:[],bar:6,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`advanced`},{manufacturer:`Michaelson Aircraft`,primaryFactory:`Ruchbah`},{chassis:`Michaelson Heavy Industries`,engine:`ICE`,armor:`Standard (BAR 6)`,jumpJets:`N/A N/A`},`The Cormorant Medium Cargo Craft is a seventy-ton Wing-in-Ground-Effect transport manufactured by the Michaelson Aircraft division of Michaelson Heavy Industries on Ruchbah since 2690. Developed for the ocean worlds of the Federated Suns, the Cormorant addresses a persistent logistical gap in commercial shipping: while air transport offers speed, its cost makes bulk cargo movement prohibitive over long oceanic distances, and conventional surface shipping lacks the speed and flexibility operators need. The Cormorant occupies the middle ground, skimming ocean surfaces faster than conventional freighters while remaining economical enough for routine cargo operations. Fully amphibious and capable of operating without a prepared airstrip, the craft proves equally suited to established port facilities and remote frontier destinations where infrastructure is minimal. Its rear cargo hatch and large hold have made it one of the most common medium WiGEs in the Federated Suns.`,`The Cormorant relies on an internal combustion engine for propulsion, giving it a cruise speed suitable for extended ocean crossings while keeping operating costs manageable compared to fusion-powered alternatives. Its rear cargo hatch allows rapid loading and unloading without specialized port equipment, and cargo secured with parachutes and flotation devices can be jettisoned in flight if circumstances demand. The amphibious chassis modification lets the Cormorant operate on water surfaces as well as in ground-effect flight, extending its reach to destinations where dedicated landing facilities are absent.

For protection, the Cormorant carries a pair of machine guns in wing-mounted positions, providing limited deterrence against wildlife and light pirate threats. Its BAR 6 armor offers adequate protection for a commercial platform but leaves the craft vulnerable to organized military opposition. The Cormorant's fundamental weakness is its civilian heritage: designed around cargo volume and economical operation rather than survivability, it performs best when escorts or military assets handle serious threats. Crews operating in contested or wilderness environments have been known to remove the weapons entirely to increase cargo capacity, a trade-off that reflects the tension between commercial efficiency and the realities of operating on worlds where not all dangers are human in origin.`,`The Terran Hegemony's experimentation with Wing-in-Ground-Effect vehicles during the twenty-fourth century established the technological foundation for designs like the Cormorant, though early Hegemony WiGEs such as the Thorizer proved unsuitable for military applications and saw limited adoption. Michaelson Aircraft, better known for its military aerospace designs, recognized the civilian transport potential of WiGE technology and began developing a purpose-built cargo variant optimized for the ocean worlds becoming increasingly important to the Federated Suns economy. The Cormorant entered production in 2690 at Michaelson's Ruchbah facility and quickly established itself as the workhorse of oceanic cargo logistics throughout the Federated Suns.

Ulysses became the craft's most prominent operational theater, its vast oceans offering both the reach the Cormorant was designed to exploit and hazards its crews had to manage. Large predators, including the Ulysses Tiger Raptor, presented a constant threat to both flight operations and surface cargo handling, giving a distinctly non-military dimension to crew safety procedures. Cormorant crews developed informal tactics for deterring wildlife, and the machine gun installations standard on commercial versions reflected practical necessity as much as anti-piracy concerns.

The Ruchbah facility changed hands repeatedly through the Succession Wars, passing from Terran Hegemony control through an independent period and into Capellan Confederation ownership before the Federated Suns recaptured the system. The Cormorant's fundamental utility kept production largely continuous despite these transitions, and the design accumulated a substantial operational record across multiple political eras. The FedCom Civil War brought intensified piracy to Ulyssean shipping lanes, creating demand for a dedicated military variant that ultimately led Michaelson to develop the Fulmar patrol derivative.`,`Cormorant Medium Cargo Craft`,{front:10,left:10,right:10,rear:9,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`machine-gun`,`right`,`#0`,null,null],[`machine-gun`,`left`,`#1`,null,null],{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`fusion`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:4,chassisModifications:[`amphibious`],bar:6,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`none`},{engine:`Fusion (unspecified)`,armor:`Unknown (BAR 10, 3.5 tons)`,jumpJets:`N/A N/A`},`Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar`,{front:16,left:13,right:13,rear:12,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`ice`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:0,chassisModifications:[`armored`],bar:10,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`none`},[`tag`,`uuid`,`location`,`feedsWeaponTag`],[`communications-equipment`,`#0`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,`#6`,null,`machine-gun`],[`seating-standard`,`#7`,null,null],{chassis:`Michaelson Heavy Industries`,engine:`ICE`,armor:`Advanced Composite (BAR 8)`,jumpJets:`N/A N/A`},`The Fulmar Patrol Craft is a seventy-ton military derivative of the Cormorant, introduced by Michaelson Aircraft in 3056 to meet growing demand for dedicated maritime patrol and convoy escort capability in the Federated Suns. Outwardly similar to its commercial parent, the Fulmar incorporates advanced composite materials that simultaneously improve armor protection and increase top speed, making it noticeably faster and more durable than the cargo craft it was designed to protect. Where the Cormorant prioritizes cargo volume and economy, the Fulmar trades payload space for military systems suited to anti-piracy operations, coastal patrol, and the paramilitary transport of infantry forces. Its introduction coincided with an escalating piracy crisis on Ulysses that would ultimately define the design's early operational reputation.`,`The Fulmar's advanced composite construction yields armor rated at BAR 8, a significant improvement over the Cormorant's commercial-grade protection, while reducing hull weight enough to accommodate a more powerful drive configuration that raises cruise and flank speeds substantially. This combination of better protection and higher speed makes the Fulmar considerably more capable as an escort and patrol platform than any modified Cormorant hull could achieve.

Armament centers on a pair of light machine guns in wing-mounted positions, supplemented by an Advanced Fire Control system that improves accuracy against the small, fast targets typical of piracy operations. Four mine dispensers mounted to the rear provide area-denial capability useful for blocking coastal approaches and discouraging pursuit by surface vessels. The reduced cargo bay retains sufficient capacity to transport a full company of marines with their equipment, giving the Fulmar practical utility as a paramilitary transport in addition to its patrol role.

The ICE powerplant keeps operating costs manageable for sustained patrol duty. The Fulmar's primary limitation is firepower: the light machine guns and mine dispensers are effective against unarmored or lightly armored smuggler craft but provide limited capability against opponents with meaningful armor protection. The design performs best as a deterrent and pursuit platform against civilian-grade threats rather than a combat vessel expected to engage organized military opposition directly.`,`Michaelson Aircraft developed the Fulmar in direct response to conditions on Ulysses, where the FedCom Civil War created an environment that coastal pirates and smugglers exploited aggressively. With the Ulyssean Navy's resources stretched by competing demands, shipping losses in the planet's shallow coastal seas reached epidemic proportions as organized pirate groups used their own WiGEs to prey on unescorted cargo craft. The Cormorant's commercial-grade systems were inadequate for self-defense against coordinated attacks, and the scale of losses threatened the planetary economy.

Rather than designing a purpose-built military WiGE from scratch, Michaelson adapted the proven Cormorant hull with advanced composite materials, a strengthened drive system, and military-grade electronics. The Fulmar entered service in 3056 with standard machine gun armament closely resembling its cargo parent, but an updated configuration introduced in 3065 replaced those weapons with light machine guns, added an Advanced Fire Control system, and incorporated rear-mounted mine dispensers for area denial. This configuration proved decisive in addressing the Ulyssean piracy problem.

The Ulysses Navy deployed Fulmars in convoy escort, establishing protective formations around commercial Cormorant flights and gradually forcing pirates to seek softer targets elsewhere. Intelligence gathered through sustained patrol operations eventually identified the primary pirate base on the island of Penelope. A Fulmar squadron transported a full battalion of marines directly to the island, and the resulting assault eliminated the threat. The operation became something of a showcase for the design's dual utility as both patrol platform and military transport. The Fulmar subsequently attracted attention from smugglers themselves, who recognized that a fast, amphibious WiGE with military-grade systems served their purposes as effectively as the naval patrols it was designed to counter.`,[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#2`,null,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#3`,null,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#4`,null,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#5`,null,null],`ammo-is-light-machine-gun-standard`,{structuralTechRating:`e`,engine:`fusion`,engineTechRating:`e`,fuelTons:7,chassisModifications:[`amphibious`],bar:8,armorTechRating:`e`,fireControl:`advanced`},`The Generic Expandable Services Vehicle, commonly known as the GESV, is a multipurpose wheeled support platform developed by the Federated Suns and introduced in 3066. Built around OmniVehicle technology, the design can be rapidly reconfigured to serve virtually any rear-echelon role, from mobile command post to field kitchen to frontline medical facility. The platform serves military units, government agencies, police forces, and civilian relief organizations operating in field environments. Its purpose-built trailer extends the tractor's capabilities further, offering additional configurations for personnel housing and cargo transport. The GESV's design philosophy emphasizes adaptability over specialization, allowing any organization engaged in extended field operations to meet diverse support needs with a single standardized platform rather than a fleet of dedicated vehicles. This flexibility, combined with straightforward maintenance requirements, has made the design a common sight in Federated Suns rear-echelon formations.`,`The GESV tractor is a medium wheeled support vehicle powered by a fuel cell engine. The fuel cell system eliminates dependence on volatile liquid fuels, reducing the logistical burden in forward areas and improving fire safety when the vehicle is configured as a medical unit or command post. The engine provides sufficient speed to keep pace with most mechanized formations, and the vehicle carries enough fuel for a range of approximately 2,700 kilometers.

Two tons of commercial-grade armor rated at BAR 7 protect the crew and internal equipment against light weapons fire and battlefield debris. This protection level is adequate for a support platform operating behind the front lines, though it provides little security against direct combat engagement. The GESV carries no integral weapons, leaving defense entirely to escort forces or crew personal arms, which is a meaningful limitation in fluid operational environments.

As an OmniVehicle, the GESV dedicates eleven tons of pod space to mission equipment, enabling installation of any combination of modules within that weight limit. A crew of two operates the vehicle itself, with configuration-specific personnel added as required. The tractor can pull up to two purpose-built trailers simultaneously, substantially multiplying available support capacity without additional prime movers. The trade-off for this flexibility is entirely in the vehicle's lack of self-defense capability.`,`The GESV emerged from a Federated Suns initiative to standardize rear-echelon support infrastructure. By the mid-3060s, the complexities of operating across multiple theaters had exposed the limitations of specialized support vehicles. Different organizations fielded incompatible equipment, creating maintenance and logistics complications that impaired operational efficiency across extended campaigns.

The OmniVehicle architecture provided the engineering solution. By applying the same modular technology used in OmniMechs to a wheeled support platform, engineers produced a vehicle that could be refitted between missions to serve whichever role was most needed. The tractor's eleven tons of pod space accommodated a command post module, a field kitchen, or a MASH unit, while the companion trailer expanded capacity for bunking, cargo, or workspace needs.

Introduced in 3066, the GESV entered service during the final years of the FedCom Civil War. Its timing proved fortunate, as the conflict generated exactly the kind of extended, multi-role operational demand the design was built to address. AFFS rear-echelon units quickly adopted the platform, and its reputation for versatility spread beyond the Federated Suns. Civilian agencies, police departments, and non-governmental relief organizations operating in conflict zones began acquiring the vehicle as well.

The GESV's generic specifications and civilian-grade components allowed manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce the vehicle with minimal tooling investment. This distributed production base contributed to the platform's resilience during the Jihad era, when targeted industrial strikes disrupted dedicated combat vehicle lines while GESV production continued at secondary and civilian facilities with little interruption.`,`Generic Expandable Services Vehicle`,{front:15,left:10,right:10,rear:15,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},`communications-equipment`,{structuralTechRating:`e`,engine:`fuel-cell`,engineTechRating:`e`,fuelTons:3.5,chassisModifications:[`tractor`,`omni`],bar:7,armorTechRating:`e`,fireControl:`none`},[`infrared-imager`,`#1`,`front`],[`seating-standard`,`#3`,null],[{tag:`cargo`,amount:.05,doors:1}],[`environmental-sealing`],[{tag:`seating-standard`,uuid:`#0`}],[`seating-standard`,`#7`],`quirk-difficult-to-maintain`,[`searchlight`,`front`,`#0`],[`seating-standard`,null,`#4`],[`seating-standard`,null,`#5`],[`seating-standard`,null,`#6`],{manufacturer:`Wakazashi Enterprises`,primaryFactory:`Dover`},`quirk-vtol-rotor-arrangement`,[`seating-standard`,`#8`],[`seating-standard`,`#9`],[`seating-standard`,`#10`],[`seating-standard`,`#11`],[`seating-standard`,`#12`],[`seating-standard`,`#13`],[`quirk-vtol-rotor-arrangement`,`dual`],{front:27,left:20,right:20,rear:25,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#2`,null,null],{manufacturer:`Pesht Motors`,primaryFactory:`Pesht`},{chassis:`Pesht Motors`},{front:12,left:15,right:15,rear:12,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{structuralTechRating:`e`,engine:`fuel-cell`,engineTechRating:`e`,fuelTons:1,chassisModifications:[`off-road`],bar:7,armorTechRating:`e`,fireControl:`none`},[`machine-gun`,`front`,`#0`,null,null],[`ammo-machine-gun-standard`,null,`#3`,!0,`machine-gun`],{manufacturer:`Johnston Industries`,primaryFactory:`New Syrtis`},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#0`],[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#1`]]},[`lift-hoist`,`rear`,`#1`],{manufacturer:`Earthwerks Limited|Vandenberg Mechanized Industries`,primaryFactory:`Grand Base|New Vandenberg`},`The Sherpa Armored Truck is a medium tracked support vehicle that has served Inner Sphere and Periphery militaries since the Age of War. Introduced in 2430, the design was built to provide logistical backbone support for mobile campaigns, hauling supplies, fuel, and equipment behind the front lines. Its straightforward, rugged construction made it practical for manufacturers across known space to produce and maintain. Earthwerks Limited in the Capellan Confederation became the most prolific single producer, while Vandenberg Mechanized Industries in the Taurian Concordat operated the second major production line. The Sherpa's adaptable chassis has been configured into numerous specialized variants over the centuries, from mobile medical facilities to field kitchens, reflecting the broad utility commanders have found in a simple, dependable, armored cargo platform.`,`The Sherpa is powered by a petrochemical-fueled Internal Combustion Engine, trading the unrestricted operational range of fusion-powered vehicles for lower unit cost and broad parts availability. Its cruise speed of 43 kilometers per hour and flank speed of 65 kilometers per hour are adequate for rear-echelon operations. The vehicle's principal shortcoming is its high fuel consumption, which strains logistics chains during extended campaigns and earned it a reputation as demanding in the supply sense, even when demand for its cargo services is high.

The standard cargo configuration carries seven tons of supplies accessible through a rear bay door, with the reinforced chassis allowing emergency towing of considerably heavier loads. For self-defense, the Sherpa mounts a single machine gun in a turret, paired with Basic Fire Control to give the gunner modest accuracy. One ton of ammunition sustains defensive use without frequent rearming. Two tons of BAR 10 armor protect the crew against small arms and light support weapons, though the vehicle is not rated for front-line combat.

The Armored Chassis Modification provides greater crew protection than a standard civilian truck. The Sherpa's most tactically significant attribute is its Easy to Maintain design, which allows field repairs using components sourced from common commercial freight vehicles. This compatibility cuts both ways, however, as locally sourced civilian parts are equally serviceable to anyone seeking to immobilize the vehicle.`,`The Sherpa Armored Truck entered service in 2430, early in the Age of War, as armies recognized that a campaign's outcome depended as much on logistics as on firepower. While fusion-powered BattleMechs drew the attention of military planners, field commanders valued reliable cargo transport with enough armor to survive near the front. The Sherpa filled that role across dozens of worlds, manufactured by local concerns and major industrial firms alike from its earliest years of production.

During the Star League era the Sherpa spread broadly through commercial licensing and independent manufacture. Earthwerks Limited on Grand Base became the dominant Capellan producer, and the Taurian Concordat established its own line at Vandenberg Mechanized Industries on New Vandenberg. Both states relied heavily on ICE-powered support vehicles as economical alternatives to fusion options, and the Sherpa fit their operational doctrine well. The Capellan Confederation and the Taurian Concordat both integrated the truck into their planetary militia structures, and it saw service with paramilitary formations throughout the Periphery.

The Succession Wars did little to reduce the Sherpa's presence in service. The collapse of Star League industry and the loss of advanced technology actually increased demand for simple, easily-maintained vehicles. As fusion-powered alternatives became harder to produce and service, militia and paramilitary formations that could not afford front-line equipment came to depend on the Sherpa for supply, medical evacuation, and field support. Its compatibility with civilian freight components proved invaluable during the LosTech era, keeping vehicles operational in theaters where purpose-built military spares were unavailable.

Production at Grand Base and New Vandenberg continued through the Jihad and into the Dark Age, with both facilities maintaining output for their respective states. The vehicle's long service record reflects less a lack of alternatives than the practical reality that a low-cost, easy-to-repair cargo platform with basic self-defense capability remains useful long after more sophisticated designs have come and gone.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`],[`quirk-gas-hog`]]},{front:7,left:6,right:6,rear:6,turret:6,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},{structuralTechRating:`d`,engine:`fusion`,engineTechRating:`d`,fuelTons:1.5,chassisModifications:[`armored`],bar:10,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`basic`},[`seating-standard`,`#4`,null],[`seating-standard`,`#5`,null],[`seating-standard`,`#6`,null],`Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion`,[`paramedic-equipment`,`right`,`#0`],[`seating-standard`,null,`#7`],[`seating-standard`,null,`#8`],[`seating-standard`,null,`#9`],{manufacturer:`Lockheed-CBM Corporation`,primaryFactory:`Furillo`},`The St. Christopher Cargo Transport is a 25-ton medium VTOL manufactured by the Lockheed-CBM Corporation on Furillo. Designed during the 3050s as a cost-effective multi-role transport, the first prototype flew in 3060 with production models entering service during the final stages of the FedCom Civil War. The design was intended to compete with heavier military transport VTOLs like the Karnov UR, offering a lighter and more affordable alternative built around off-the-shelf civilian components. Lockheed-CBM developed two core configurations to address separate market segments: the C1, a militarized variant with reinforced armor and chassis modifications, and the C2, a civilian model prioritizing cargo volume over protection. Both variants were marketed to the Lyran Alliance government and approved Lyran corporations, establishing the St. Christopher as a versatile platform suited to logistics and passenger transport roles where frontline durability was not the primary concern.`,`Both configurations share a common 25-ton VTOL airframe powered by a fuel cell engine that delivers a cruise speed of roughly 130 km/h and a flank speed approaching 195 km/h, with a fuel range of 740 kilometers. The dual rotor arrangement provides stable flight characteristics in varied weather conditions, and the design seats a crew of two enlisted personnel alongside six passengers.

The C1 mounts an armored chassis modification and military-grade armor composites rated at BAR 10, giving it reasonable survivability against small arms fire and shrapnel. This protection comes at the cost of cargo capacity, limiting the C1's configurable bay to approximately 8.5 tons. The C2 trades this protection for utility, reducing armor to BAR 4 commercial-grade composites and employing a lighter chassis frame. These changes increase cargo capacity to nearly 12.5 tons, and factory-built modifications allow installation of specialized holds including refrigerated units, pressurized compartments, expanded passenger seating, and electronic warfare equipment.

Neither variant carries weapons, relying entirely on escorts or operating in permissive environments. The civilian component base makes both variants cheap to acquire and straightforward to maintain, though neither can withstand the rough handling that fully militarized designs like the Karnov tolerate without issue. Lockheed-CBM supplements vehicle sales with a range of maintenance service contracts, from minimum servicing required by the Lyran Aviation Authority to full contract staffing on call at any time.`,`Lockheed-CBM began development of the St. Christopher in the 3050s as Lyran Alliance military planners sought affordable transport solutions to supplement their existing VTOL fleet. The first prototype completed flight testing in 3060, but the outbreak of the FedCom Civil War disrupted the production timeline. It was not until the conflict's final stages that production models began rolling off the Furillo assembly line, with both variants entering the market simultaneously.

The Lyran Alliance Armed Forces was the first military customer, purchasing several dozen C1 models for operational evaluation. The initial delivery arrived in September 3067, and the Quartermaster Corps assumed responsibility for the evaluation program. Plans called for deployment to several front-line units for field testing, though the volatile political situation following the Civil War complicated procurement schedules. Industry analysts observed that Lockheed-CBM's maintenance service agreements often generated better financial returns for the company than the vehicle sales themselves, a business model the corporation actively cultivated.

The civilian C2 found an early high-profile customer in the Lyran Diplomatic Corps, which purchased six units finished in distinctive blue and gold livery. These aircraft operated passenger shuttle routes between the Triad and other districts of Tharkad City, and the design proved well suited to the capital world's harsh climate. Robert Kelswa-Steiner publicly expressed interest in acquiring both C1 and C2 variants to support reconstruction efforts on Skye, citing a desire to rebuild the battered regional economy. At the time of the design's introduction, Tharkad had not yet approved those purchases.`,{$k:[`tag`,`parameter`],$v:[[`quirk-vtol-rotor-arrangement`,`dual`],[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null]]},`St. Christopher Cargo Transport`,{$k:[`tag`,`uuid`],$v:[[`seating-standard`,`#0`],[`seating-standard`,`#1`],[`seating-standard`,`#2`],[`seating-standard`,`#3`],[`seating-standard`,`#4`],[`seating-standard`,`#5`]]},{manufacturer:`Nashan Diversified`,primaryFactory:`Furillo`,communications:`Nashan Optichat`,targeting:`Nashan T-800 Series 4`},{engine:`Nashan Generation 5.26`,armor:`Nashan ArcShield Heavy Indi`},{front:11,left:7,right:7,rear:4,turret:0,rotor:0,frontLeft:0,frontRight:0,rearLeft:0,rearRight:0,turret2:0},[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#1`,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#2`,null],[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#3`,null],`ammo-is-light-gauss-rifle-standard`,{structuralTechRating:`e`,engine:`ice`,engineTechRating:`e`,fuelTons:0,chassisModifications:[`armored`,`amphibious`],bar:10,armorTechRating:`d`,fireControl:`advanced`},`The Sturmvogel Maritime Patrol WiGE is Nashan Diversified's answer to the coastal patrol and maritime security needs of the Lyran Alliance following its secession from the Federated Commonwealth. Originally conceived as a military gunship, the design was refocused toward coast guard and militia applications when Nashan recognized broader commercial potential in a fast, versatile patrol craft. Built on Furillo and introduced in 3063, the Sturmvogel, whose name translates roughly as Storm Bird, quickly earned a reputation for flexibility and endurance, serving in conditions ranging from tropical island chains to subarctic coastlines. Its well-publicized performance during the Word of Blake siege of Tharkad cemented its standing as a valuable light patrol asset, expanding its market to coast guards and planetary militias well beyond Lyran territory.`,`The Sturmvogel's primary asset is speed. Its fusion power plant drives the craft to a maximum speed exceeding 120 kilometers per hour with an unlimited operational range, making it well suited to extended patrol missions far from support facilities. The main armament is a single rotary autocannon paired with a battery of four mine dispensers, a combination that lets crews engage pursuing vessels directly or lay area-denial fields to channel enemies into kill zones. An onboard remote sensor dispenser supports search and rescue operations, helping detect missing vessels and hidden contacts across wide stretches of coastline and open water.

A cargo bay can carry rescue equipment on patrol missions or be converted for a boarding party when interdiction operations require it. The craft's modular architecture makes swapping weapons and equipment straightforward, allowing rapid reconfiguration for different mission profiles.

The Sturmvogel's most significant weakness is its light armor. Crews compensate by exploiting the WiGE's mobility over terrain that would stop conventional vehicles, avoiding direct engagement whenever possible. The design was never intended for front-line combat, and commanders who use it in that role pay a steep price.`,`Nashan Diversified began development of the Sturmvogel in the late 3050s, initially pursuing a military gunship contract. The original prototype carried a light Gauss rifle and mine dispensers, but Lyran evaluators found the armor protection insufficient for front-line service. Rather than abandon the project, Nashan redesigned the craft for coast guard and militia service, a market growing steadily since access to the Federated Suns-built Fulmar and Cormorant became uncertain following the Lyran Alliance's secession in 3058.

The Maritime Patrol variant entered production on Furillo by 3063 and spread quickly through Lyran coast guard units and planetary militias. Its reputation changed significantly during the Word of Blake siege of Tharkad, where Sturmvogels served in virtually every conceivable role, from direct combat to ferrying troops, search and rescue, medevac, and acting as detection platforms for ambushes against Blakist forces across the Tatyana Islands, the New Bismarck Archipelago, the industrial terrain of Heidelberg, and the frozen wastes of Franz and Sutherland. The tri-vid footage and testimonials from Tharkad drove sales sharply upward, pushing the craft into service with coast guards and militias throughout the Alliance and beyond.

The Sturmvogel's commercial success persuaded Nashan to develop faster, more capable successors. Partnering with Johnson Industries, the company produced the Fensalir combat WiGE in 3084, followed by the Swallow attack WiGE in 3092. Both designs trace their lineage to the Sturmvogel's hull architecture, confirming the Maritime Patrol's enduring influence on Lyran WiGE development.`,`Sturmvogel Maritime Patrol WiGE`,[`vehicular-mine-dispenser`,`rear`,`#4`,null],`ammo-is-rotary-ac-2-standard`,`ammo-is-lb-10x-standard`,[`quirk-poor-performance`,null],{manufacturer:`Whitestreak, Inc.`,primaryFactory:`Marantha`},{engine:`Electric (Fuel Cell)`}],designs:JSON.parse(`{"0":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Bordello Military Goods, Inc.|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Hadrian Mechanized Industries|Quikscell Company|Aldis Industries","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Antwerp|Ares|Kalidasa|Layover|Oliver|Pandora|Pompey|Richvale|Terra"},"components":"~0","text":{"overview":"The AC/2 Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle designed to provide long-range ballistic fire support at minimal cost. Part of the broader family of weapons carrier vehicles that originated during the Age of War, the AC/2 Carrier dates back to approximately 2520 in its primitive form, with the modernized version entering service alongside its missile carrier cousins. Built by Quikscell and numerous other manufacturers across the Inner Sphere, the vehicle fills a niche as cheap, expendable suppressive fire support, using volume of autocannon fire to compensate for the relatively modest damage output of individual Class 2 weapons.","capabilities":"The primitive AC/2 Carrier represents the earliest member of the weapons carrier family to mount ballistic weapons, entering service around 2520. Built as a fifty-five-ton support vehicle using primitive construction methods, it mounts four Class 2 autocannons behind BAR 6 armor with advanced fire control systems that partially compensate for the crude platform.\\n\\nThe design's straightforward maintenance requirements made it accessible to militaries with limited technical infrastructure. These vehicles were rendered obsolete by modernized carriers around 2580 but served through the Age of War era, establishing the ballistic carrier concept alongside the earlier missile carrier designs.","deployment":"The primitive AC/2 Carrier served during the Age of War, providing long-range ballistic fire support for armies still relying on pre-spaceflight military technology. Quik Products Inc manufactured these vehicles alongside the primitive missile carriers, establishing the full weapons carrier family that would endure for centuries.","battleHistory":"The AC/2 Carrier traces its lineage to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed a family of standardized tracked weapons platforms. The primitive ancestor entered service around 2520 mounting four Class 2 autocannons behind BAR 6 armor with advanced fire control systems. Despite crude construction, the design proved effective enough to inspire the modernized version that served through the Star League era and beyond. Like the missile carriers, the AC/2 Carrier suffered from widespread unauthorized copying during the Succession Wars, and Quikscell's post-Fourth Succession War legal campaign shut down many competitors.\\n\\nProduction spread across an extensive network. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Richvale formed the core, supplemented by Hadrian Mechanized Industries on Pompey, Aldis Industries on Terra, Bordello Military Goods on Antwerp, and Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain. This distributed base mirrored the missile carrier network.\\n\\nThe AC/2 Carrier received its first major modernization in 3063 with the LB-X variant. This substantial redesign replaced the internal combustion engine with a fusion powerplant, doubled armor protection, and substituted the standard autocannons with LB-X Class 2 weapons capable of firing both solid slugs and cluster ammunition. A C3 slave computer enabled networked targeting. Quikscell produced the LB-X variant at most of its carrier plants.\\n\\nDespite its long service history, the AC/2 Carrier has always occupied a somewhat ambiguous position within the weapons carrier family. The vehicle's similarity to the Pike support vehicle has caused persistent identification problems in the field. However, the AC/2 Carrier continues to fill its role as cheap long-range suppressive fire support, its simplicity and low cost ensuring continued relevance."}},"quirks":"~1","name":"AC/2 Carrier","model":"(Primitive)","tonnage":55,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":"~2","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["autocannon-standard-a","front","#0",null],["autocannon-standard-a","front","#1",null],["autocannon-standard-a","front","#2",null],["autocannon-standard-a","front","#3",null],["ammo-ac-2-standard",null,"#4","autocannon-standard-a"],["ammo-ac-2-standard",null,"#5","autocannon-standard-a"]]},"supportVehicle":"~4"},"1":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Alstom Rail Corporation / Blackwell Corporation","primaryFactory":"Various"},"components":{"chassis":"Rail (Large)","engine":"Fusion","armor":"BAR 7","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Adelante is a six-hundred-ton rail tractor developed jointly by Alstom Rail Corporation and Blackwell Corporation in the early 3060s. Conceived as the driving force behind an innovative modular rail system, the Adelante replaced centuries of inefficient switchyard coupling operations with a streamlined approach to long-distance ground transportation. Powered by a massive fusion engine, the tractor hauls one or more of Alstom's interchangeable TGV Omni Railcar trailers across continental rail networks at impressive speeds. Its combination of raw hauling power and compatibility with the omni-configured trailer system made the Adelante an immediate commercial success, attracting buyers across the Inner Sphere. Both civilian freight operators and military logistics commands recognized the platform's potential, leading to widespread adoption that has kept the design in continuous service for decades.","capabilities":"Powered by a fusion engine generating enough output to move its considerable mass at a cruise speed of twelve rail movement points and a flank speed of eighteen, the Adelante is among the fastest heavy rail tractors in the Inner Sphere. Its six-hundred-ton frame accommodates twenty-eight tons of standard cargo space alongside five second-class crew quarters, providing berths for the three-person operating crew with room for additional personnel during extended hauls. The tractor chassis modification allows it to couple with any compatible trailer, though the design was purpose-built around the TGV Omni Railcar system.\\n\\nProtection comes in the form of BAR 7 armor distributed across the hull, with heavier plating concentrated on the front and flanks where collision and combat damage are most likely. While not designed for direct combat, this level of protection provides adequate resistance against small arms fire and light anti-vehicle weapons, a practical consideration for operations in contested areas. The Adelante mounts no weapons of its own, relying instead on escort vehicles or the military configurations of its attached trailers for defensive capability. Its Equipment Rating of D/X-E-D reflects the advanced fusion powerplant and specialized tractor systems that can make sourcing replacement parts challenging outside of major industrial centers.","deployment":"The Adelante serves as the sole tractor configuration in the Alstom rail system, designed exclusively to haul TGV Omni Railcar trailers across continental distances. With twenty-eight tons of onboard cargo space and crew quarters for its three-person complement, the tractor can operate independently for extended periods between major rail depots. Most Adelante units spend their service lives hauling passenger and freight configurations in commercial service, though military operators have found the platform equally valuable for rapid logistical support and force deployment along established rail corridors.","battleHistory":"Alstom Rail Corporation's partnership with Blackwell Corporation began in the early 3060s as both companies recognized growing demand for faster, more efficient ground logistics across the Inner Sphere. Conventional rail operations still relied on coupling and decoupling individual cars at switchyard junctions, a process largely unchanged for millennia that created significant bottlenecks during high-volume shipping periods. The Adelante tractor and its companion TGV Omni Railcar trailer were designed together as an integrated solution, using omni-technology applied to the trailer component to cut cargo turnaround times in half while dramatically improving operational flexibility.\\n\\nThe system's military potential was demonstrated during the FedCom Civil War on New Avalon. Operating from the Alsace depot, the Davion Heavy Guards used the Adelante rail system to rapidly transport battle armor squads and light hovercraft to the outskirts of Avalon City. This rapid deployment blocked a key flanking maneuver by Loyalist forces during the fighting for the Federated Suns' capital world. The Heavy Guards further exploited the rail network by fitting a modified mobile command base into a TGV trailer, allowing their command staff to remain mobile across the continent's extensive rail infrastructure. Even after repeated air strikes targeted the rail lines, enough track survived that the Heavy Guards continued to deliver strike groups and supplies across roughly sixty percent of the planet's landmass.\\n\\nThe success on New Avalon brought widespread attention to the platform's military applications, complementing its already established commercial role. Production has continued under universal availability, with the Adelante seeing service across multiple successor states and commercial operators throughout the Inner Sphere well into the Dark Age and beyond."}},"name":"Adelante Passenger/Cargo Train","tonnage":600,"motiveType":"rail","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":28,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":12,"armorAllocation":{"front":40,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":20,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":35,"frontRight":35,"rearLeft":35,"rearRight":35,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":[["quarters-crew","#0"],["quarters-crew","#1"],["quarters-crew","#2"],["quarters-crew","#3"],["quarters-crew","#4"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor"],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"2":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"GM/Rowell"},"text":{"overview":"The Apocalypse World Rover is GM/Rowell's military-grade adaptation of their popular civilian World Rover line of wheeled off-road vehicles. Introduced in 2983 to meet a longstanding need among militia, paramilitary, and secondary military forces across the Inner Sphere and Periphery, the Apocalypse addresses the widespread practice of pressing civilian vehicles into military service. Rather than accept the limitations of unmodified commercial vehicles (poor cross-country mobility, no weapons hardpoints, and minimal protection) GM/Rowell engineered the Apocalypse from the outset for the demanding conditions faced by motorized and mechanized infantry units. Available to buyers across all political boundaries, it has become one of the most recognizable light utility vehicles in the known universe, serving everything from House military rear-echelon formations to Periphery planetary militias and corporate security details.","capabilities":"The Apocalypse is a small wheeled support vehicle massing just two metric tons, powered by an internal combustion engine running on petrochemicals. It achieves a cruise speed of 4 movement points and a flank speed of 6, adequate for keeping pace with infantry formations across varied terrain. An off-road chassis and controls modification gives the vehicle meaningful cross-country capability that its civilian counterparts lack.\\n\\nThough the Apocalypse carries no fixed weapons, it is equipped with a pintle mount capable of accommodating heavy support weapons up to 80 kilograms in weight, giving crews the flexibility to arm the vehicle with whatever crew-served weapons are available in the field. Armor protection uses BAR 7 composite construction, providing eight points distributed across the hull; three points to the front, two on each side, and one point protecting the rear. This offers meaningful protection against small arms and light support weapons, though it affords minimal defense against heavier military-grade fire. The vehicle seats a crew of one driver alongside up to six passengers, with a 501-kilogram cargo bay accessible through a single rear door.","deployment":"The Apocalypse World Rover serves in motorized and mechanized infantry support roles across virtually every military and paramilitary organization in the Inner Sphere and Periphery. House military forces assign it to rear-area logistics, security, and reconnaissance missions, while planetary militias use it as a general-purpose tactical vehicle for infantry units that lack access to more sophisticated military transports. Its pintle mount allows individual units to configure it for specific mission requirements, armed with whatever crew-served heavy support weapons are available locally. The vehicle's simple petrochemical powerplant and low maintenance burden make it well-suited to operations far from major supply depots, and the rear-loading cargo bay accommodates a range of payloads from ammunition resupply to casualty evacuation.","battleHistory":"The practice of assigning civilian vehicles to military roles dates back to humanity's earliest planetary militias, and by the time of the Succession Wars the Inner Sphere's armies had long since normalized the use of commercial transports as a substitute for proper military vehicles. Faced with constrained budgets and diminishing industrial output, militia and paramilitary formations made do with whatever wheeled vehicles could be sourced locally. These vehicles were frequently inadequate, lacking off-road capability, hardpoints for weapons, or any meaningful protection, and frontline commanders routinely noted the dangers this posed to infantry units dependent on them for mobility.\\n\\nGM/Rowell recognized this gap and entered the market in 2983 with the Apocalypse, positioning it as a purpose-designed alternative to ad hoc civilian vehicle conversions. Rather than simply modifying an existing commercial chassis, the company developed a military variant of their World Rover line with off-road suspension upgrades, reinforced body panels, and integrated weapons mounting provisions. This approach allowed buyers to receive a ready-made military vehicle without the expense and uncertainty of field modifications.\\n\\nThe Apocalypse found a ready market across a broad customer base. House militaries acquired it for rear-area security and support roles. Periphery states and independent worlds without sophisticated manufacturing capabilities valued the vehicle's simplicity and the ease with which petrochemical engines could be maintained and supplied far from industrial centers. Corporate security forces and mercenary units operating on tight budgets rounded out the customer base. GM/Rowell's decision to market the vehicle without faction restrictions made the Apocalypse one of the most widely distributed light utility vehicles of the late Succession Wars era and beyond."}},"name":"Apocalypse World Rover","tonnage":2,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.501,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":3,"left":2,"right":2,"rear":1,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":["~5","~6","~7","~8","~9","~10","~11"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.024,"chassisModifications":["off-road"],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"3":{"tro":{"production":"~12","components":{"engine":"ICE (rating unknown)","armor":"BAR 6 (5 tons)"},"text":{"overview":"The Apostle is an eighty-ton primitive self-propelled artillery vehicle commissioned by the Terran Hegemony in 2321. Designed alongside its lighter companion, the Reaper, the Apostle represented the heavier of two artillery platforms developed to modernize the Hegemony Armed Forces' outdated indirect fire capabilities. Intended to provide long-range heavy fire support at the division level, the vehicle was assigned to battalion-sized artillery formations supporting HAF divisions in the field. As one of the earliest purpose-built self-propelled guns fielded by the Hegemony, the Apostle established a new standard for mobile artillery support that would influence Hegemony military doctrine for generations to come.","capabilities":"The Apostle's primary armament is a single Sniper artillery piece mounted in the hull, supported by three tons of ammunition providing thirty rounds of sustained fire. An advanced fire control system guides the weapon, enabling accurate engagement of targets at extreme range, while the ammunition capacity permits the crew to carry multiple munition types as the tactical situation demands. A single front-mounted small laser provides minimal self-defense capability against threats that manage to reach the gun line.\\n\\nPowered by an internal combustion engine, the Apostle achieves a cruise speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour with a flank speed of fifty-four. Its generous fuel supply provides an operational range of nearly eleven hundred kilometers, well suited to extended campaigns. Five tons of BAR 6 armor afford basic protection, reflecting the expectation that the vehicle would operate well behind the front lines rather than engage in direct combat. A tractor chassis modification allows the Apostle to tow additional equipment or ammunition trailers, extending its logistical footprint. The large crew of ten, including two officers and six dedicated gunners, ensures smooth operation of the complex artillery system.","deployment":"The Apostle Self-Propelled Artillery served as the primary heavy indirect fire platform for Hegemony Armed Forces division-level operations. Its substantial ammunition capacity and long operational range made it well suited to sustained bombardment campaigns in support of frontline formations. The tractor chassis allowed Apostle batteries to tow additional supplies during extended operations, making the vehicle the centerpiece of HAF heavy artillery battalions.","battleHistory":"The Apostle and Reaper emerged from a period of urgent military reform following James McKenna's rise to power as the first Director-General of the Terran Hegemony. Field Marshal Mears Coblitz, retained as Commanding General, recognized that the fractured remnants of the old Terran Alliance military desperately needed modernization to prevent the Hegemony from descending into civil war. Among his priorities, the artillery corps had long suffered from neglect, relying on outdated static, towed, and self-propelled pieces inherited from the Alliance era.\\n\\nCoblitz personally drafted requirements for two new self-propelled artillery vehicles. The Apostle filled the heavier role, intended for assignment to division-level artillery battalions, while the Reaper served a lighter requirement at the regimental and brigade level. Both vehicles entered service remarkably quickly, debuting in less than two years from initial requirements. The Apostle first saw combat during the later stages of the Campaign of Persuasion, as the Hegemony worked to expand and strengthen its influence across nearby star systems.\\n\\nThroughout the late twenty-third century and the decades that followed, the Apostle and Reaper gradually supplanted virtually all of the older Alliance artillery still in service. Both platforms remained in frontline use for more than a century before advancing technology rendered them obsolete. The Apostle received its formal obsolescence designation in 2485, replaced by newer designs incorporating standard rather than primitive technology. Despite this, a handful of examples survived into the thirty-first century. By the dawn of the Dark Age, at least a few Apostles remained as ceremonial vehicles stationed on Terra and New Earth."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-obsolete",2485],"~13"]},"name":"Apostle Self-Propelled Artillery","tonnage":80,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":14,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~14",["sniper-artillery","front","#1",null],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#2","sniper-artillery"],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#3","sniper-artillery"],["ammo-sniper-standard",null,"#4","sniper-artillery"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":3,"chassisModifications":["tractor"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"advanced"}},"4":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Apple-Churchill Inc.","primaryFactory":"Sian"},"components":{"jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Apple-Churchill Surveillance VTOL is a purpose-built intelligence platform produced exclusively by Apple-Churchill Inc. on Sian. Introduced in 2971, the airframe was developed for the Capellan Confederation's internal security apparatus, sold only to the Maskirovka and select constabulary and militia forces across the Confederation. Apple-Churchill maintains total in-house control over every aspect of production and maintenance, with no outside contractors or labor involved at any stage. The company trains all operators and mechanics personally, producing a platform so thoroughly integrated into the Confederation's intelligence structure that its presence has become synonymous with the Maskirovka's reach across Capellan space. Quiet, reliable, and deliberately obscure, it is one of the most tightly guarded designs in the Inner Sphere.","capabilities":"At twenty tons, the Apple-Churchill Surveillance VTOL is a lightweight platform built around concealment and observation rather than combat. Powered by an internal-combustion engine and equipped with a dual-ring, counter-rotating multiblade rotor system, the craft achieves near-complete acoustic concealment. Confirmed reports document observers standing no more than three hundred meters from a hovering Apple-Churchill VTOL yet unable to hear it, a capability that suggests an undisclosed noise-reduction system guarded closely by the manufacturer. Cruise speed reaches 75.6 km/h, with a top speed of 118.8 km/h, and a petrochemical fuel system provides a range of 1,250 kilometers.\\n\\nThe sensor suite is the heart of the design. A high-resolution imaging camera and an infrared imager are mounted in the nose, while the rear compartment houses trained operators who manage a sixty-sensor remote sensor dispenser, typically configured for sonic and visual detection. A dedicated ton of communications equipment rounds out the package. Armor protection is minimal, providing only enough coverage to survive incidental hazards, and the vehicle carries no weapons. Three crew members operate the platform: a pilot and two rear-compartment operators managing the sensor suite and remote dispensers. The design's philosophy is unambiguous, built to observe without engaging, operating in environments where silence and invisibility matter more than any weapon.","deployment":"The Apple-Churchill Surveillance VTOL is distributed exclusively within the Capellan Confederation to the Maskirovka and select constabulary and militia units. With output deliberately limited to a small annual run, the total fleet remains carefully controlled and never exceeds the Confederation's internal security needs. Operators receive direct training from Apple-Churchill personnel and are not shared outside the Confederation's security structure. The vehicle is used for surveillance, intelligence gathering, and the covert tailing of individuals, with the ability to follow a single target through dense urban environments using only its airborne sensor suite. The absence of weapons and limited armor confirm the design is not intended for contested airspace, instead operating in controlled or permissive environments where acoustic concealment provides more protection than any gun.","battleHistory":"The Apple-Churchill Surveillance VTOL entered service with the Capellan Confederation in 2971, a product of the close relationship between Apple-Churchill Inc. and the Confederation's internal security apparatus. From the outset, the company maintained an unusual degree of secrecy over its construction, ensuring no outside workers or contractors entered the production wing of its Sian facility. In the decades since introduction, not one outside worker has been employed in that section of the factory, a policy enforced with the same discipline as the Maskirovka itself.\\n\\nApple-Churchill produces no more than sixteen units annually under normal circumstances, expanding output only when the Maskirovka or other Confederation agencies place extraordinary orders. Each vehicle leaves the factory with a supply of spare parts and detailed maintenance documentation, and when damage occurs in the field, Apple-Churchill dispatches its own certified technicians to perform repairs on-site rather than allowing outside maintenance personnel access to the airframe. The approach has been successful. In all the years since introduction, only three Apple-Churchill VTOLs have been destroyed in crashes, and all three were attributed to pilot error rather than mechanical failure.\\n\\nThe level of secrecy surrounding the design is unusual even by Capellan standards. The parts machining is conducted entirely in-house, and the company trains its own operators and mechanics rather than relying on shared Confederation personnel. The result is a platform whose technical details remain almost entirely unknown outside Maskirovka circles, with even allied Confederation military commands reporting no access to its maintenance specifications. Rumors persist within intelligence circles that a second variant of the airframe exists, intended to transport and insert Maskirovka special operations teams from low altitude using zip-line deployment. Apple-Churchill has neither confirmed nor denied these accounts, and the question of whether such a vehicle is a distinct design or merely the standard airframe repurposed remains officially unresolved."}},"name":"Apple-Churchill Surveillance VTOL","motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~15","era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":{"front":4,"left":3,"right":3,"rear":3,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":["~17",["~18","#1",null],"~19",["infrared-imager","#3","front"]]},"supportVehicle":"~20"},"5":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Quik Products Inc.|Quik Products Inc.|Aldis Industries","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Ares|Pandora|Terra"},"components":{"engine":"Primitive ICE","armor":"BAR"},"text":{"overview":"The Armored Personnel Carrier is a ubiquitous ten-ton combat transport found in virtually every military force across the Inner Sphere and Periphery. Produced in three standard configurations -- hover, tracked, and wheeled -- these vehicles serve as the primary means of delivering infantry squads to and from the combat zone. The basic designs date back to the Star League era around 2500, with primitive predecessors operating as early as 2390, and production has continued without interruption for centuries. Their simplicity, low cost, and ease of manufacture ensure that every House military, mercenary command, and planetary militia fields them in quantity, making them among the most commonly encountered military vehicles in the galaxy.","capabilities":"The primitive tracked APC dates to approximately 2390, representing one of the earliest purpose-built military personnel carriers. Using civilian drive train components and a petrochemical-powered internal combustion engine, this ten-ton vehicle carries one ton of infantry -- a single squad -- at a maximum speed of only approximately fifty-four kilometers per hour. Two turret-mounted machine guns provide basic self-defense.\\n\\nConstructed with barrier armor rating six protection -- twenty-four points of armor coverage -- the primitive tracked APC offers better protection than the hover primitive but at a significant cost in mobility. The extremely low speed leaves the vehicle vulnerable during approach and withdrawal phases, and the reliance on petrochemical fuel limits operational endurance. Despite these limitations, the tracked primitive remained in service with the most resource-constrained forces for centuries, its simplicity and ease of manufacture ensuring continued production long after superior designs became available.","deployment":"The primitive tracked APC persists in service with militia and paramilitary forces on worlds where more advanced vehicles are unavailable or too expensive to maintain. The Draconis Combine and Free Worlds League both maintain tracked cargo variants derived from the primitive chassis for rear-area logistics. The design's extremely low speed limits its tactical utility, but its simplicity and reliability ensure continued production on the most resource-constrained worlds.","battleHistory":"The armored personnel carrier concept predates the modern Star League designs by over a century. Primitive APCs first appeared around 2390, using civilian drive train components and petrochemical-powered internal combustion engines to deliver troops under fire. Though crude by later standards, these early carriers established the fundamental template -- a lightly armed, armored box on wheels, tracks, or a hoverskirt -- that would endure for centuries. The Age of War and the technological advances it brought rendered these primitive designs obsolete for front-line service, but they never fully disappeared, remaining in use with militia and paramilitary forces well into the thirty-first century.\\n\\nThe modern APC entered service around 2500, when Star League-era manufacturers standardized three motive configurations optimized for different operational environments. Production spread rapidly across the Inner Sphere, with virtually every military manufacturer offering some version of the basic design. During the Succession Wars, demand far outstripped supply, and APCs became one of the few military vehicles that even the most devastated economies could continue producing. Hundreds of local manufacturers emerged to fill the gap, many working from incomplete specifications or reverse-engineered examples.\\n\\nThe standard APC's enduring legacy owes as much to its limitations as its capabilities. Its minimal carrying capacity of a single infantry squad pushed militaries to develop heavier alternatives, while its thin armor and light armament drove the creation of numerous weapon-platform conversions. Militia commanders, unable to afford purpose-built combat vehicles, frequently stripped the infantry compartment to mount LRM or SRM launchers, creating improvised fire support vehicles. The Taurian Concordat and Marian Hegemony independently developed sensor-equipped hover variants for border patrol duties. One notable action occurred in 3034, when Overste-Lojtnant Illya Stroncek of the Lyran Commonwealth led a column of tracked APCs through Kurita lines on Last Frontier, evacuating three companies of wounded infantry under sustained fire and earning the Order of the Griffin for her actions."}},"name":"Armored Personnel Carrier","model":"(Tracked Primitive)","tonnage":10,"troopSpace":1,"armorAllocation":{"front":5,"left":5,"right":5,"rear":4,"turret":5,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":["~22",["machine-gun","turret","#1",null,null],"~23"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"6":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Sheinhardt Universal Technologies","primaryFactory":"Sirius V"},"components":{"engine":"ICE (Unknown)"},"text":{"overview":"The Asher Hover Scout emerged in 2298 as the debut military product from Sheinhardt Universal Technologies, a company better known for agricultural and industrial logistics than battlefield hardware. The design originated as a commercial failure, a heavy freight hauler that found no market in its intended role, repurposed by SUT engineers who installed a more capable engine, added weaponry, and bolted on several tons of armor. Marketed to planetary militias across the Terran Alliance as a cost-effective scouting solution, the vehicle found early acceptance with the Alliance Global Militia, whose initial procurement validated SUT's aggressive entry into defense manufacturing. The small number of Ashers built directly by SUT on Sirius were regarded as capable and reliable machines, earning genuine praise from experienced operators, though the company's reliance on a broad subcontractor network to meet demand created quality inconsistencies that shadowed the design's reputation throughout its service life. The Asher remained a common, if imperfect, fixture of early twenty-fourth century militia forces.","capabilities":"The Asher is a 45-ton medium hover vehicle capable of a top speed of 129 kph, providing adequate mobility for reconnaissance and screening operations in open terrain, though operators found its handling unreliable at high speed due to a persistent tendency to yaw uncontrollably. Its main offensive punch comes from a ten-tube long-range missile launcher mounted in the turret, giving the vehicle the ability to engage targets at significant range before closing. Machine guns and flamethrowers supplement the missile armament, making the Asher effective against infantry and light vehicle targets at close quarters. The turret provides reasonable firing arcs, though a mechanical tendency for the traverse mechanism to slow or freeze during extended operations reduced reliability in sustained engagements.\\n\\nThe vehicle's armor protection is comparatively heavy for its class, generally sufficient to absorb punishment that would destroy lighter scouts outright. This durability made it popular in a heavy cavalry role beyond its nominal reconnaissance mission, as field commanders valued its ability to press engagements where more fragile platforms could not. The primary tactical limitation was logistical: the widespread use of subcontractors across SUT's production network meant that replacement components from one manufacturer frequently proved incompatible with vehicles from another, complicating repair operations for multi-source regiments. Sirius-built examples avoided most of these issues, as SUT's own production line maintained consistent tolerances.","deployment":"The Asher Hover Scout served with planetary militia formations throughout the Terran Alliance and, through SUT's subsidiary distribution network, with forces aligned with each of the five Great Houses during the twenty-fourth century. Within the Alliance Global Militia, the vehicle's above-average armor for its class pushed it toward a heavy cavalry role rather than pure reconnaissance, as unit commanders valued its ability to survive contact that would destroy lighter scouts. Regiments operating Sirius-built examples generally assigned the Asher to its intended scouting mission, while those receiving subcontractor-built vehicles more often used them in screening and support duties where precision handling mattered less. AGM crews developed the informal nickname \\"Ten-Pack\\" for the vehicle, referencing both its ten-tube missile launcher and its notorious tendency to handle unpredictably under hard maneuvering. Surviving examples continued in local service after SUT's dissolution, gradually declining in operational readiness as the parts incompatibility problems left by the multi-source production network took their toll on fleet maintenance.","battleHistory":"Sheinhardt Universal Technologies designed the original vehicle as a heavy hover transport for agricultural and industrial cargo, but the design attracted no meaningful commercial interest. Rather than abandon the development investment, SUT engineers converted the platform for military use, fitting a more powerful engine, adding a ten-tube long-range missile launcher along with machine guns and flamethrowers, and bolting on additional armor. The resulting vehicle, designated the AS-H and marketed as the Armed Scout-Hover, entered production on Sirius in 2298.\\n\\nSUT built four demonstration models to secure initial procurement interest. Alliance Global Militia evaluators responded favorably, their enthusiasm reportedly assisted by substantial corporate donations made on the company's behalf to militia officials. The AGM placed an initial order of five thousand units, a figure that far exceeded SUT's own production capacity on Sirius. The company responded by subcontracting production across dozens of subsidiary firms distributed throughout the Terran Alliance and beyond, few of which applied consistent quality control measures or used standardized components. Ashers produced directly by SUT on Sirius were consistently regarded as well-built machines, earning high marks from their crews. The far larger subcontractor-built portion of the fleet represented a different story, assembled from incompatible components that made maintenance difficult and reliability unpredictable.\\n\\nAGM regiments receiving mixed-source fleets found that spare parts rarely transferred between vehicles of nominally identical specification, complicating logistics across the force. Despite these problems, the Asher remained in production for several decades. SUT's subsidiaries marketed cosmetic variations of the design to all five Great Houses throughout the twenty-fourth century, cultivating sales relationships across interstellar political boundaries. This practice eventually attracted the attention of the Terran Hegemony government, which governed commercial activity on Sirius and viewed SUT's multi-faction marketing of military hardware as a violation of Terran commercial law. The Hegemony declared SUT in open violation of its statutes, dissolved the company, and auctioned its assets across the realm. Military historians have since theorized that a substantial portion of those assets were eventually acquired by subsidiaries of the Quikscell Company, whose low-cost vehicle manufacturing practices bore a recognizable resemblance to SUT's operating model.\\n\\nProduction of the Asher ended permanently with SUT's dissolution. No successor firm resumed the line, and vehicles already in service continued operating wherever they had been distributed, sustained by locally available components for as long as maintenance remained possible. The chronic parts incompatibility left by the subcontractor network made long-term sustainment increasingly difficult for regiments operating large mixed-source fleets."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~24",["quirk-obsolete",2360],"~25"]},"name":"Asher Hover Scout","tonnage":45,"motiveType":"hover","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":11,"left":11,"right":11,"rear":6,"turret":10,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~26",["vehicle-flamer","front","#1",null],"~27",["lrm-5","turret","#3",null],["machine-gun","turret","#4",null],["machine-gun","turret","#5",null],["~28",null,"#6","machine-gun"],["ammo-vehicle-flamer-standard",null,"#7","vehicle-flamer"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#8","lrm-5"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":2,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"7":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Hamilton Consortium","primaryFactory":"Oriente"},"text":{"overview":"The Atlantia Luxury Yacht is a prestige naval vessel produced by the Hamilton Consortium on Oriente for the high-end leisure market of the Free Worlds League. Introduced in 2890 during the Late Succession Wars, the design was aimed at the wealthy civilians and merchant class who continued to prize luxury and exclusivity even during a period of broader interstellar conflict. Each thirty-meter vessel is built to order from a core design schematic, with the construction fee of 575,000 C-bills covering the development of unique custom features for every hull, meaning no two Atlantias are exactly alike. A two-year waiting period from order to delivery became a hallmark of the line, reinforcing its status as a symbol of affluence throughout the League and in export markets beyond it.","capabilities":"The Atlantia is built around a high-strength polymer hull with equally resilient decking and paneling, designed to handle Oriente's harsh equatorial storms without compromising passenger comfort. Many buyers opt for Oriente caoba timber overlays on the decking and interior panels, providing a warm aesthetic without sacrificing structural strength. At 190 tons, the vessel achieves a top speed of approximately 55 kilometers per hour under standard configuration, with sustained cruising at just over 30 kph. BAR 3 protection is distributed across all facings, with the heaviest concentration forward where storm and wave impact is greatest.\\n\\nThe standard powerplant is an internal combustion engine providing a range of 1,153 kilometers on a full fuel load, sufficient for extended coastal and archipelago cruising. Hamilton Consortium offers fusion powerplant alternatives at significant additional cost, and enhanced-engine planing-hull configurations have demonstrated speeds exceeding 100 kph in private commission builds. The vessel carries nine crew in addition to seven luxury passenger berths, with custom orders able to adjust these capacities in exchange for speed or range. The bridge is outfitted with satellite navigation and communications equipment keeping the vessel connected regardless of location. Optional appointments include top-deck jacuzzis, fishing decks, a sliding roof over the dining salon, and exterior trim packages.","deployment":"The Atlantia is deployed almost exclusively by private civilian owners throughout the Free Worlds League and export markets. Its Equipment Rating of D/X-D-C reflects its open civilian availability, practically constrained by cost and the two-year order queue. The vessel serves as a private yacht, corporate transport, and executive retreat platform for the League's wealthiest citizens. A small number of fusion-powered configurations have been built for buyers requiring higher performance. No formal military applications are on record, though the vessel's communications suite and extended range make it a practical courier or liaison platform in permissive environments. Production has continued through multiple political transitions on Oriente, remaining active under Free Worlds League, Duchy of Oriente, and Oriente Protectorate oversight before returning to FWL control in the ilClan era.","battleHistory":"The Hamilton Consortium developed the Atlantia series in 2890 to serve the wealthy civilian market that persisted through the Late Succession Wars on Oriente and across the broader Free Worlds League. The design filled a gap for buyers who wanted a vessel that combined genuine seagoing capability with the luxury appointments expected by affluent clients, and the built-to-order production model ensured that exclusivity was baked into every hull from the outset. The combination of the 575,000 C-bill construction fee and the two-year waiting period transformed ownership of an Atlantia into a visible marker of status among League nobility and the merchant class.\\n\\nExport contracts extended the Atlantia's reach beyond the League, and Hamilton steadily built a reputation for delivering resilient, well-appointed vessels that could handle Oriente's demanding equatorial conditions. The line gained broader recognition in 3065 when a custom high-performance variant built for media mogul Elliot Ikegami competed in the Halberstan endurance race on New Olympia. Ikegami placed third, but his vessel's demonstrated capability at over 100 kph drew considerable attention to the platform's engineering flexibility and generated a new wave of orders for the Consortium.\\n\\nProduction on Oriente continued through the Jihad and into the Dark Age, persisting under Duchy of Oriente ownership during the early Republic period before transitioning to Oriente Protectorate oversight through the DA-LR, DA-1, and DA-2 eras. The MUPL corrections log flags DA-2 Protectorate ownership as potentially suspect, noting that the Oriente Protectorate formally ended in 3139 while some records still reflect Protectorate control at the 3145 snapshot. The factory returned to Free Worlds League control by the ilClan era. Throughout these political transitions, the built-to-order civilian production model kept the line active and the Hamilton Consortium's order books filled."}},"name":"Atlantia Luxury Yacht","tonnage":190,"motiveType":"naval-surface","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~29","era":"early-sw","armorAllocation":{"front":25,"left":16,"right":16,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":[["field-kitchen","#0",null],["lifeboat-maritime","#1","right"],["lifeboat-maritime","#2","left"],["quarters-officer","#3",null],["quarters-officer","#4",null],["quarters-officer","#5",null],["quarters-officer","#6",null],["quarters-officer","#7",null],["quarters-officer","#8",null],["quarters-officer","#9",null],["quarters-crew","#10",null],["quarters-crew","#11",null],["quarters-steerage","#12",null],["quarters-steerage","#13",null],["quarters-steerage","#14",null],["quarters-steerage","#15",null],["quarters-steerage","#16",null],["quarters-steerage","#17",null],["quarters-steerage","#18",null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":3,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":3,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"8":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Caliphate Airfield Alpha|King Dumount Defense Facility|Pride of Granada Industries","primaryFactory":"Granada|Castile|Granada","communications":"Various Various","targeting":"Various Various"},"text":{"overview":"The Auxiliary Defense Tank emerged from political necessity following the Umayyad Caliphate's conquest of Castilian territories in 2830. Seeking to appease local nobles while keeping their forces outgunned, Umayyad administrators commissioned a fighting vehicle built around an existing earthmover chassis. Manufactured at several sites across Granada and Castile beginning in 2833, the ADT was never intended as a front-line warfighting machine. Its designers prioritized availability over performance, producing a vehicle that could be fielded quickly and cheaply, if not effectively. Crews and commanders alike regarded it with ambivalence, and it earned the Periphery nickname 'Oruga Negra,' the Black Caterpillar, a backhanded acknowledgment that while no individual tank posed a serious threat, enough of them together could still cause harm.","capabilities":"The ADT-1 is a 28-ton tracked vehicle powered by a Colonial Agricultural Standard 85 internal combustion engine, providing a top speed of 54 km/h, modest but compatible with the infantry-heavy formations that formed the backbone of Castilian and Umayyad ground forces. The Granada Guard Mk 2 armor meets only BAR 6 standards, offering adequate resistance to small arms but little comfort against dedicated anti-armor weapons. Its armament reflects the constraints of Periphery industry: a single medium rifle with two tons of ammunition provided the main punch, supplemented by a machine gun for anti-personnel work. In practice the machine gun saw heavier use, as the ADT was frequently deployed to suppress civil unrest rather than fight peer adversaries. The design carries Poor Workmanship and Unreliable quirks throughout its service life, reflecting the rushed conversion of agricultural machinery and persistent maintenance demands that frustrated crews.\\n\\nThe ADT-2 replaced the medium rifle with an AC/5 on a redesigned turret and incorporated an updated drivetrain allowing heavier armor protection. Introduced through Hanseatic League commercial distribution, the upgrade gave the design more credible anti-armor capability while preserving the fundamental chassis.","deployment":"The ADT-2 entered service through an unusual commercial arrangement, with the Hanseatic League supplying upgraded hulls to both Umayyad and Castilian forces simultaneously. This broad availability made it a common sight across the region during the early thirtieth century. The redesigned turret and AC/5 armament provided credible anti-armor capability the ADT-1 lacked, finding a niche in combined-arms formations alongside infantry and older support vehicles. By 2973, more capable designs had displaced it from front-line roles, and the ADT faded from operational use across both Umayyad and Castilian inventories.","battleHistory":"The Umayyad invasion of Castilian space in 2830 left the conquerors with a governance problem as much as a military one. Castilian nobles, fiercely proud and armed with personal retinues, represented a persistent insurgency risk that required careful management. The Umayyad solution was pragmatic: provide the nobles with a standardized fighting vehicle built from locally available agricultural machinery, giving them something to command while ensuring their forces remained outgunned by Umayyad regulars.\\n\\nProduction began at Caliphate Airfield Alpha and Pride of Granada Industries on Granada in 2833, with the design later replicated by the Castilians at King Dumount Defense Facility. From 2833 to 2862 the ADT-1 saw front-line service, first in skirmishes between rival Castilian noble houses and later as the Umayyads fractured into internal conflict. Unable to keep pace with escalating weapons technology, the ADT was pushed from front-line to garrison roles after 2862. The Castilians' own development of the Reconquista BattleMech ultimately made the ADT obsolete as a serious combat asset.\\n\\nThe ADT-2 arrived not through indigenous development but through commerce. The Hanseatic League recognized a market opportunity and began selling the upgraded variant to both sides of the Umayyad conflict around 2903. The simultaneous appearance of the same tank on opposing sides initially raised fears of espionage, but the commercial explanation emerged quickly. The AC/5 armament and improved drivetrain extended the design's useful service life, though it too was phased out by 2973 as more capable equipment arrived in the region."}},"name":"Auxiliary Defense Tank","model":"ADT-2","tonnage":28,"era":"early-sw","armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":10,"right":10,"rear":8,"turret":10,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~26",["autocannon-standard-b","turret","#1",null],["~28",null,"#2","machine-gun"],["ammo-ac-5-standard",null,"#3","autocannon-standard-b"],["ammo-ac-5-standard",null,"#4","autocannon-standard-b"],["seating-pillion",null,"#5",null],["seating-pillion",null,"#6",null],["seating-pillion",null,"#7",null],["seating-pillion",null,"#8",null],["seating-pillion",null,"#9",null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"advanced"}},"9":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Gienah Automotive","primaryFactory":"Gienah"},"components":{"chassis":"Gienah Automotive","engine":"Gienah Automotive","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Bailey Armored Car represents Gienah Automotive's direct response to the demonstrated inadequacy of standard civilian security vehicles against Battle Armor-equipped adversaries. Developed after the 3059 Briggs robbery incident on Alexandria exposed the vulnerability of existing armored cars to modern military threats, the Bailey incorporates recovered First Star League technology into a wheeled platform substantially more capable than the Knox-type vehicles it was designed to supplement. Weighing more than twice as much as the Knox and costing six times as much, the Bailey is explicitly positioned as a premium security transport for clients whose cargo and personnel justify serious investment in protection. Gienah Automotive, headquartered on Gienah in the Lyran Alliance, has received consistent orders from mercenary security contractors, planetary governments, and commercial enterprises since the Bailey entered production, establishing the vehicle as the premier civilian armored car of its generation.","capabilities":"The Bailey is built around a wheeled medium platform powered by a fusion engine, providing substantially faster speeds than comparable internal combustion designs while eliminating fuel range constraints. Its armor suite provides fifty-eight points of BAR 10 protection, a dramatic improvement over the Knox-class baseline that offers meaningful resistance against light military weapons and reliable protection from Battle Armor-class threats. The armored chassis modification reinforces the vehicle's structural integrity beyond standard civilian construction standards.\\n\\nThe Bailey's armament centers on two turret-mounted small pulse lasers supplemented by a turret-mounted anti-missile system with twelve rounds of AMS ammunition. The pulse lasers provide accurate, heat-efficient firepower against lightly armored targets, while the AMS offers active defense against guided munitions, a critical capability in threat environments where shoulder-launched missiles are readily available to criminal or insurgent forces. Advanced fire control supports both weapon systems, providing targeting capability well above the basic fire control found in Knox-type vehicles. The Bailey carries three tons of standard cargo, sufficient for a small dismounted security team alongside high-value cargo, giving it a viable secondary role as a light armored personnel carrier. The wheeled drive system performs well on paved and improved surfaces but restricts the Bailey to urban and developed terrain, requiring combined arms support for operations beyond established road networks.","deployment":"Bailey Armored Cars serve primarily in urban and developed-terrain security roles throughout the Lyran Alliance and in smaller numbers across the broader Inner Sphere via mercenary and commercial export channels. Their most common operators are planetary governments, banking institutions, and mercenary security contractors requiring vehicles capable of resisting Battle Armor-class threats. The anti-missile system makes the Bailey particularly valuable in environments where guided munitions are a credible threat from criminal or insurgent forces. The three-ton cargo bay allows operators to transport a small security team alongside high-value cargo, supporting combined escort and transport missions with a single vehicle. The Bailey's lack of off-road capability restricts it to urban environments and paved infrastructure, and operators in rural or undeveloped areas typically pair it with tracked or hover alternatives capable of operating in rough terrain.","battleHistory":"The Bailey Armored Car emerged directly from the 3059 Briggs robbery incident on Alexandria, in which a criminal organization using Battle Armor overpowered a Knox convoy despite its BattleMech escort. The scale and audacity of the robbery demonstrated that even well-planned commercial security operations could not protect high-value cargo against adversaries with access to modern military hardware. The incident received significant attention across Lyran Alliance commercial channels, generating industry demand for a security transport capable of engaging Battle Armor-class threats directly rather than relying on BattleMech escorts that could not always be economically justified.\\n\\nGienah Automotive responded by designing a purpose-built upgrade around recovered First Star League military equipment, sourcing fusion engine components, pulse laser systems, and anti-missile hardware through channels opened by the renewed interest in rediscovered Star League technology during the late Clan Invasion period. The resulting design entered production on Gienah shortly after 3062, drawing on the company's established wheeled vehicle manufacturing infrastructure and leveraging the supply chains Gienah Automotive had built over prior decades of civilian transport production.\\n\\nThe Bailey commanded strong initial orders despite its significant price premium. Mercenary units seeking contract work in security transportation roles found the Bailey's capabilities particularly attractive, as the anti-missile system and pulse lasers allowed a single vehicle to provide credible deterrence against the armed criminal organizations and light insurgent forces that dominated security contracting work during the Civil War era. Planetary governments with active criminal threats or low-level insurgencies adopted the Bailey as a cost-effective alternative to deploying dedicated military vehicles for constabulary duties. Gienah Automotive's production line maintained consistent output through the Civil War period and into the Jihad era, with the Lyran Alliance and later the Commonwealth Worlds of Europe remaining the primary customer base through the Dark Age period and into the ilClan era."}},"name":"Bailey Armored Car","tonnage":27,"motiveType":"wheeled","bays":"~30","era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":{"front":13,"left":11,"right":11,"rear":13,"turret":10,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["small-pulse-laser","turret","#0",null],["small-pulse-laser","turret","#1",null],["is-ams","turret","#2",null],["ammo-is-ams-standard",null,"#3","is-ams"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"advanced"}},"10":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"Clan Hell's Horses upgraded their standard Clan hoverpods with Omni technology, adding a larger engine to improve versatility and speed. The result was the Boreas, which can swap weaponry based on battlefield needs. It has since become the premier covert operations support vehicle for infantry scout units in the Horses' touman, providing fast, flexible reconnaissance with a two-seat crew arrangement."}},"name":"Boreas Cavalry Hovercraft","model":"A","tonnage":0.75,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"tech":"clan","era":"clan-golden-years","cruiseMP":14,"armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":{"$k":["tag","location","uuid","size"],"$v":[["sv-light-weapon-inf-support-laser-semi-portable","front","#0",0],["seating-standard",null,"#1",null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"f","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"f","fuelTons":0.029,"chassisModifications":["bicycle","omni"],"bar":8,"armorTechRating":"f","fireControl":"advanced"}},"11":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"New Earth Trading Company","primaryFactory":"New Earth","targeting":"N/A N/A"},"components":{"engine":"Electric (Fuel Cell)","armor":"BAR 2","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Buffalo is a 100-ton large-class civilian hovertruck developed during the Star League era for the Terran Hegemony Supply and Support Services. Its designation (BFFL, standing for Behind Friendly-lines, Fixed Logistics) reflects the vehicle's intended purpose as a rear-echelon cargo hauler. The design's origins trace to the entrepreneurial vision of Jarod Westphal, a veteran of the Hegemony Armed Forces Logistics Command who recognized the commercial potential of surplus military transports at the end of his service. Westphal restored ten older BFFLs, founded Westphal Cargo Carriers Company, and secured a contract with the New Earth Trading Company, which eventually purchased his firm outright. NETC then used its licensing network to spread the design across the Inner Sphere through its CargoCrawlers division. The Buffalo's combination of hover mobility, high cargo capacity, and straightforward maintenance made it one of the most enduring logistics vehicles of its era.","capabilities":"The Buffalo is powered by a fuel-cell electric engine, providing a cruise speed of 27 kilometers per hour and a flank speed of 43 kilometers per hour. Its large-class hover propulsion system allows it to cross both improved roads and open terrain, an advantage for supply operations in varied environments. The vehicle's primary value lies in its cargo capacity: four universal container hardpoints accommodate 40 tons of supplies, and a crew of three operates from a basic cab configuration. Armor protection is deliberately light (less than a ton of BAR 2-rated material) as the design was intended for rear-echelon operations rather than combat exposure. The fuel-cell power plant generates no waste heat, eliminating thermal management concerns entirely. No weapons are mounted in the standard configuration, and the Buffalo depends on escort forces or favorable positioning to avoid hostile contact. The transmission system is the vehicle's most mechanically demanding component, though improvements in available materials in later production runs simplified maintenance considerably.","deployment":"The Buffalo serves as a general-purpose cargo hauler wherever rear-echelon logistics support is required. Military formations use it to shuttle ammunition, spare parts, rations, and replacement equipment between depots and forward staging areas. Commercial operators have employed the design on dozens of worlds for industrial freight, agricultural distribution, and surface transport, capitalizing on its hover capability to reach locations inaccessible to wheeled vehicles. Because the Buffalo carries no defensive systems, operators in contested territory depend on escort detachments or operate only in secured zones. The design's broad licensing history means vehicles of varying age and mechanical condition can be found across the Inner Sphere's diverse supply networks.","battleHistory":"The Buffalo entered service with the Terran Hegemony Supply and Support Services during the Star League era, gaining a reputation as a practical and undemanding logistics platform. On New Earth, Jarod Westphal's commercial venture brought the aging design back into organized production, and the New Earth Trading Company's acquisition of his firm secured a stable manufacturing base. NETC extended licensing across the Inner Sphere through its CargoCrawlers division, establishing New Earth as the primary production point while supporting manufacturing at multiple sites.\\n\\nThe collapse of the Star League had minimal impact on the Buffalo's commercial standing. Unlike combat vehicles whose manufacturers often suspended production during the Succession Wars, demand for cargo transport persisted regardless of political alignment. New Earth passed through the hands of the Lyran Commonwealth, the Federated Commonwealth during the 3040s merger, and back to Lyran control through the Civil War. The design's one significant evolution came through metallurgical advances recovered from the Helm Memory Core, which permitted the use of lighter transmission materials as that knowledge spread through the Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe Buffalo's ubiquity and open cargo bay made it a recurring choice for improvised weaponization. During Anton Marik's rebellion against his brother Janos, loyalist forces on New Delos loaded surplus Buffalos with explosives and remote-control systems and deployed them against Wolf's Dragoons' Delta Regiment. The detonations were powerful enough to convince elements of Summer's Company they were under tactical nuclear attack, inflicting heavy casualties before the nature of the threat was identified. Word of Blake forces revisited the concept during the Jihad, deploying converted Buffalo drones extensively in urban combat zones, with documented examples including military-grade armor and, in some reports, actual nuclear payloads. Word of Blake forces seized New Earth during the Jihad, and production records from that occupation remain uncertain. Following the Jihad, the Republic of the Sphere administered New Earth's facilities, though the Buffalo's production status grew unclear as the Dark Age progressed."}},"name":"Buffalo","model":"BFFL Hovertruck","tonnage":100,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":8,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":6,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":6,"frontRight":6,"rearLeft":6,"rearRight":6,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":[["cargo-container","rear","#0"],["cargo-container","rear","#1"],["cargo-container","rear","#2"],["cargo-container","rear","#3"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":6,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"12":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Dortmein Wagons Ltd.","primaryFactory":"Arcturus"},"components":{"engine":"Unknown (Primitive ICE)","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Carter Medical Emergency Response Vehicle, introduced by Dortmein Wagons, Ltd. on Arcturus in 2341, represents an early Lyran Commonwealth effort to provide organized, mobile battlefield medical care at the regimental level. Developed as a primitive prototype rather than a production design, the Carter MERV was built to deliver surgical-grade treatment far forward from fixed medical installations. Found in both military formations and civilian emergency services organizations across the Inner Sphere, the vehicle earned a reputation as a practical and accessible platform for mobile medical teams despite its primitive construction. Though it eventually gave way to more advanced successors, the Carter MERV served as an early model for the wheeled battlefield medicine concept that would persist across multiple eras.","capabilities":"The Carter MERV is a 25-ton wheeled vehicle powered by a primitive internal combustion engine, capable of cruising at modest speeds with off-road mobility provided by its modified chassis and control systems. Unlike conventional combat vehicles, the MERV carries no weapons, dedicating all available mass to medical equipment, crew accommodations, and operational supplies. Its most distinctive feature is an expanding pop-out structural design that allows a compact road footprint to unfold into a substantially larger working space when deployed in the field. This configuration permits a crew of fifteen, including three officers, to operate simultaneously across four interior compartments.\\n\\nThe forward command section serves as both the driver's compartment and the management station for the senior medical officer. The rear compartment handles triage and walking-wounded cases, with capacity for approximately two tons of cargo or up to twenty unequipped personnel. The center section functions as an emergency room with four treatment bays, each served by paramedic-grade diagnostic and monitoring equipment. Two operating theaters, one fore and one aft of the center section, provide dedicated surgical capability, with one full set of MASH equipment servicing both theaters.\\n\\nThe vehicle's armor provides protection appropriate to a non-combatant platform designed to operate behind the forward line of engagement. Nominal facing armor is adequate to deflect stray small arms fire and shell fragments but offers little resistance against dedicated anti-vehicle weaponry. The ICE powerplant, while fuel-intensive and demanding of resupply, is straightforward to service and diagnose in the field, and the vehicle's overall construction earned a formal designation as easy to maintain even by the primitive engineering standards of its era.","deployment":"The Carter MERV was designed to deploy in groups of two to twelve vehicles, with larger formations providing comprehensive medical coverage for brigade-scale operations. In military service, individual MERVs attached to regimental medical trains were typically positioned well to the rear of the forward engagement line, operating under whatever local arrangement protected non-combatant vehicles while remaining close enough to receive casualties quickly.\\n\\nCivilian emergency services organizations across the Inner Sphere adopted the design wherever mobile forward-deployed medical care was required, particularly on worlds lacking the infrastructure for permanent trauma centers. The vehicle's wheeled mobility and off-road capability allowed it to reach incident sites across a wide range of terrain. The Carter MERV's low technical maintenance burden made it especially attractive to planetary defense forces and independent emergency services that lacked access to advanced technical support personnel, keeping examples in service long after the design's nominal obsolescence date.","battleHistory":"The Carter MERV emerged from centuries of hard-won battlefield medical doctrine. Military commanders had long understood that survival rates for combat casualties improved dramatically when trained medical personnel reached the wounded quickly. This recognition drove continuous evolution in field medicine, from static regimental aid stations to air-transportable MASH units, then VTOL air ambulances, fixed-wing air hospitals, and dedicated hospital DropShips. By the early thirty-fourth century of the Common Era, military theorists had begun cycling back toward highly mobile ground vehicles as an ideal complement to those larger assets, capable of moving under their own power directly to forward casualty collection points without the logistical complexity of air operations.\\n\\nDortmein Wagons, Ltd., a manufacturer based in the Lyran Commonwealth on Arcturus, developed the Carter MERV in 2341 under the direction of supervising engineer Minka Albrecht. The project was classified as non-production equipment from its inception, intended as a proving ground for the self-contained wheeled medical facility concept rather than a candidate for series manufacture. The timing placed its development in the formative years of the Lyran Commonwealth, as that state was consolidating its holdings and building the logistical infrastructure necessary to support a major regional military. Battlefield medicine, long a secondary concern in frontier conflicts, had become a recognized priority as the scale of potential operations between the emerging Successor States grew.\\n\\nDespite its non-production status, the Carter MERV's simple construction and accessible mechanical systems allowed it to remain in service well beyond the lifespan of more sophisticated contemporaries. Its design quirk records formally noted the vehicle as obsolete within the first century of its introduction as more capable platforms became available, but practical considerations kept examples operating in garrison and support roles for decades past that designation. As the Star League era progressed and the Succession Wars followed, primitive systems and limited operational range gradually excluded the Carter MERV from serious front-line consideration. By the later Succession Wars, the design was found primarily in the inventories of low-technology planetary defense forces and civilian emergency organizations where technical simplicity outweighed performance limitations. The design's lasting contribution lay not in its production numbers but in demonstrating that a wheeled, crew-intensive medical platform could operate effectively far forward of permanent installations, a lesson that informed generations of successor designs."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~33",["quirk-obsolete",2580],["quirk-gas-hog",null]]},"name":"Carter Medical Emergency Response Vehicle","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":2,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":14,"right":14,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":[["mash-core","#0",null],["paramedic-equipment","#1",null],["paramedic-equipment","#2","front"],["paramedic-equipment","#3","right"],["paramedic-equipment","#4","left"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":["off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"13":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Skye Pleasure Craft Ltd","primaryFactory":"Skye","communications":"N/A N/A","targeting":"N/A N/A"},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Coanda Personal Sports Craft is a lightweight Wing-in-Ground-Effect racing vehicle produced by Skye Pleasure Craft Ltd on the Lyran world of Skye, introduced in 3054. Built for competitive aquatic racing, the Coanda was developed to serve the commercial opportunities presented by high-profile racing events such as the Skye Water Racing Championship, held every four years and drawing competitors and spectators from across Lyran space. Technically an unarmed civilian utility vehicle, its purpose is purely competitive sport. Interstellar media coverage of the championship circuit gave the Coanda significant commercial visibility, establishing Skye Pleasure Craft as a recognized manufacturer within the recreational WiGE market.","capabilities":"The Coanda masses 3,500 kilograms, making it among the lightest Wing-in-Ground-Effect designs in production. Powered by an internal combustion engine, the craft skims the water surface using aerodynamic ground effect to achieve competitive racing speeds while maintaining a fuel range adequate for multi-kilometer championship courses. A BAR 6-rated armor shell provides minimal protection, intended for collision resistance and spray management rather than any combat application. The amphibious chassis modification enables operation across water and marginal coastal terrain, a practical feature for race courses that include shore transitions. No weapons are fitted. A single crew station is complemented by one passenger position, permitting a race observer or technical specialist to accompany the pilot without significantly affecting performance.","deployment":"The Coanda is produced at Skye Pleasure Craft's facility on Skye and is distributed primarily to professional racing teams and wealthy private buyers within the Lyran sphere. While technically capable of performing fast reconnaissance or liaison functions given its performance characteristics, the craft sees no meaningful military use. Its commercial prominence derives entirely from its racing profile, sustained by the Skye championship's interstellar media coverage. Distribution outside Lyran space remains limited, though the design's media visibility has created awareness in neighboring states.","battleHistory":"The Coanda was developed by Skye Pleasure Craft Ltd in direct response to the commercial potential of the Skye Water Racing Championship, a month-long aquatic competition held every four years that draws competitors and spectators from across the Lyran sphere and beyond. While the championship encompasses surfing, wind-powered sailing, and powerboat events, its most prestigious and dangerous competition is the speed and endurance WiGE race, an event that had driven competitors to abandon conventional watercraft in favor of Wing-in-Ground-Effect craft. Extensive media coverage of the championship, with sponsorship from aerospace and watercraft manufacturers, created an environment where producing the winning vehicle carried significant commercial value.\\n\\nSkye Pleasure Craft introduced the Coanda in 3054 to immediate competitive success. Hans Moore, a veteran figure on the Skye racing circuit, won the championship at the craft's debut and repeated the victory in the 3058 race. Moore's run ended tragically during the 3062 championship when his Coanda broke apart mid-race. Post-race investigators concluded he had pushed his engines beyond safe limits during the final drive for the finishing line. Despite the incident, the Coanda had by then established Skye Pleasure Craft as the dominant manufacturer on the circuit, garnering considerable prestige and attracting sponsorship interest from throughout the Lyran sphere.\\n\\nThe Coanda's success drew competition from Lockheed/CBM Corporation, which entered the racing market with the Quicksilver design around 3058. The rivalry injected fresh energy into the championship and created ongoing development pressure on both sides. Skye Pleasure Craft subsequently announced the Coanda II, an improved design introduced during the Jihad era, as the company sought to maintain its position against Lockheed/CBM's continued investment in the circuit."}},"name":"Coanda Personal Sports Craft","tonnage":3.5,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~34","era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":11,"armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":["~5","~6"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.475,"chassisModifications":["amphibious"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"14":{"tro":{"production":"~35","components":{"engine":"ICE","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"~36","capabilities":"~37","deployment":"The original production model of the Cobra VTOL Transport, entering service with the Hegemony Armed Forces in 2317. Built around the standard tilt-rotor airframe with an internal combustion power plant, the baseline configuration carries nine tons of cargo across three configurable hold sections and supports a slung load for heavier deliveries. Field conversion kits were developed in parallel with the original design, allowing individual vehicles to serve in a range of support roles without dedicated workshop support. The standard Cobra was produced in quantity and issued across all branches of Hegemony forces, with large numbers eventually demilitarized and sold to civilian operators following decommissioning.","battleHistory":"~38"}},"quirks":"~39","name":"Cobra VTOL Transport","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":9,"cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~40","equipment":"~41","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"15":{"tro":{"production":"~35","components":{"engine":"Fusion","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"~36","capabilities":"~37","deployment":"The final military production variant of the original Cobra line, fulfilling the last Hegemony Armed Forces orders from 2489. Replacing the baseline internal combustion engine with a fusion power plant, the Fusion configuration achieves roughly a twenty-five percent improvement in top speed and a modest increase in cargo capacity relative to the original model. This variant represents the design's operational peak within the original airframe and marks the endpoint of Pacific-Euro's Cobra production before the line was permanently discontinued.","battleHistory":"~38"}},"quirks":"~39","name":"Cobra VTOL Transport","model":"(Fusion)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":9.5,"cruiseMP":10,"armorAllocation":"~40","equipment":"~41","supportVehicle":"~42"},"16":{"tro":{"production":"~43","components":"~44","text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The Cormorant serves primarily as a bulk cargo transport on ocean worlds across the Federated Suns, with Ulysses representing its most concentrated operational environment. Operators use the craft for inter-island freight, coastal supply runs, and transport to locations without conventional airstrips or port infrastructure. Military customers deploy small numbers in logistical support roles, particularly for supplying remote installations on water-dominated worlds. Crews in wilderness environments frequently weigh the defensive value of the standard machine guns against the cargo revenue lost by carrying them, a calculation that varies considerably by route and local threat conditions.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"~48","tonnage":70,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":29,"doors":1}],"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~49","equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":["~50","~51","~23"]},"supportVehicle":"~52"},"17":{"tro":{"production":"~43","components":"~44","text":{"overview":"~45","capabilities":"~46","deployment":"The dedicated cargo configuration removes the standard machine guns and their ammunition, freeing the weight for additional payload capacity. Operators choosing this variant accept the absence of self-defense capability in exchange for maximizing commercial throughput, a trade-off viable primarily on established routes between settled worlds where piracy risk can be managed through convoy scheduling and occasional escort arrangements rather than onboard weapons. Bulk agricultural shipments, industrial equipment, and emergency relief supplies represent typical cargoes. The rear door design allows rapid turnaround at primitive landing sites, a capability particularly valued in disaster response operations on the ocean worlds and archipelagoes of the Federated Suns interior.","battleHistory":"~47"}},"name":"~48","model":"(Cargo)","tonnage":70,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":30.5,"doors":1}],"era":"early-sw","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~49","supportVehicle":"~52"},"18":{"tro":{"production":{"primaryFactory":"New Avalon","targeting":"Beagle Active Probe"},"components":"~53","text":{"overview":"The Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar represents the modernized production configuration of a protected executive transport first introduced by the Federated Suns in 3024. Entering production in 3059 as an upgraded variant of the original design, this version incorporates a military-grade electronics suite and active anti-personnel systems reflecting the heightened security environment of the post-Clan Invasion Inner Sphere. Retaining the armored chassis and first-class passenger accommodations of its predecessor, the design continues to serve as a prestige transport for senior Federated Suns personnel and allied nobility, combining luxury and survivability with a limited electronic warfare capability suited to high-threat environments.","capabilities":"The 3059 configuration retains the medium hover chassis, fusion powerplant, and BAR 10 armor layout of the original Deusenberg, preserving the same cruise and flank speeds while upgrading all major systems to Tech D standards. The Armored Chassis Modification is carried forward, and armor distribution across the front, flanks, and rear is unchanged from the original design.\\n\\nWhere the original configuration devoted internal volume primarily to passenger comfort and cargo, this variant replaces most of that cargo space with a military-grade electronic suite. A Beagle Active Probe mounted at the front of the vehicle provides enhanced sensor coverage, capable of detecting concealed infantry and hidden vehicles well beyond normal sensor range. A Guardian ECM Suite occupies the right side, providing electronic countermeasures against missile guidance and fire-control systems that might target the vehicle or its escort. Three A-Pods mounted on the front, right, and left arcs deliver anti-personnel defense against infantry threats at close range.\\n\\nFirst-class quarters accommodate the primary VIP passenger, and six passenger seats retain space for accompanying staff or security. Available cargo space is reduced to 1.55 tons by the electronic and defensive equipment, limiting logistical flexibility. An Equipment Rating of E/X-X-F reflects the classified nature of the vehicle's intelligence and ECM systems, restricting independent access to the design's core electronics.","deployment":"The 3059 variant entered service with the Federated Suns command establishment as the prestige executive transport for flag-rank officers and senior nobility requiring active electronic protection in addition to armored mobility. Its Beagle Active Probe proved particularly valuable in the vanguard of high-security convoys, providing advance warning of ambushes and concealed threats that armor alone could not counter. The Guardian ECM suite added a further layer of protection against guided weapons. Deployed primarily at New Avalon and at major Federated Suns command installations, the vehicle continued in service well into the ilClan era, reflecting the durability of its upgraded specifications relative to the original configuration it replaced.","battleHistory":"The 3059 variant of the Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar emerged from modifications applied to the Deusenberg One, the First Prince's personal limousine, during the post-Clan Invasion security review conducted across the Federated Commonwealth's executive fleet. Following the Battle of Tukayyid and the subsequent truce, security concerns for senior Federated Commonwealth leadership intensified considerably. Awareness that Clan forces remained concentrated across the border, combined with rising internal political tensions in the years before the Civil War, drove demand for vehicles that could combine executive comfort with active electronic countermeasures rather than passive armor alone.\\n\\nThe electronic upgrades applied to the Deusenberg One, incorporating a Beagle Active Probe and Guardian ECM suite alongside anti-personnel defenses, were judged sufficiently valuable to warrant inclusion in the standard New Avalon production line. The upgraded variant entered production in 3059, extending its security capabilities to a broader range of senior Federated Suns and Federated Commonwealth personnel beyond the First Prince's immediate household.\\n\\nThe 3059 design outlasted the original configuration by a considerable margin, remaining in production and service through the Civil War and Jihad eras as the authoritative Federated Suns VIP transport standard. New Avalon's production facilities avoided the catastrophic losses suffered by many other Federated Suns manufacturers during the Jihad, allowing uninterrupted output of the upgraded design. By the ilClan era, New Avalon's industrial arena had shifted considerably as political and commercial changes reshaped the former Federated Suns industrial base."}},"name":"~54","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":1.55,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":"~55","equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":[["a-pod","front","#0"],["beagle-active-probe","front","#1"],["a-pod","right","#2"],["ecm-suite","right","#3"],["a-pod","left","#4"],["quarters-officer",null,"#5"]]},"supportVehicle":"~56"},"19":{"tro":{"production":{"primaryFactory":"New Avalon","targeting":"N/A"},"components":"~53","text":{"overview":"The Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar is a fifty-ton civilian hover transport commissioned by First Prince Hanse Davion of the Federated Suns in 3024. Conceived to provide Davion royalty and senior court personnel with a protected executive transport capable of operating in close proximity to military operations, the design blends first-class passenger accommodations with an armored chassis far exceeding ordinary civilian vehicle standards. Produced on New Avalon, the Deusenberg became the prestige conveyance of the Davion court and is perhaps best remembered for its central role in events surrounding the Fourth Succession War. Its unarmed configuration relies on speed and protection rather than firepower, prioritizing the survival of its occupants over independent combat capability.","capabilities":"The Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar rides on a medium hover platform massing fifty tons, powered by a fusion powerplant that delivers a cruise speed of roughly seventy-five kilometers per hour and a flank speed approaching one hundred twenty kilometers per hour. This combination places it among the faster vehicles in its weight class, allowing the vehicle to clear most threat environments quickly without direct confrontation. The chassis carries an Armored Chassis Modification alongside just over three and a half tons of BAR 10 armor, providing meaningful protection against small arms and fragmentation while offering limited resistance to direct weapons fire. Frontal protection is prioritized, reflecting the assumption that the vehicle will typically be advancing toward or withdrawing from a threat rather than absorbing fire from multiple angles simultaneously.\\n\\nTen tons of first-class passenger quarters provide accommodations appropriate to royalty or senior military leadership, supported by six passenger seats for a security detail or accompanying staff. The remaining 6.05 tons of cargo capacity handles luggage, personal communications equipment, and mission-essential supplies. The original configuration carries no weapons, relying entirely on speed and armored protection for survivability. An Equipment Rating of D/X-F-D reflects the use of high-quality Federated Suns military-grade components, accessible to well-funded operators but not requiring cutting-edge technological infrastructure to maintain.","deployment":"The original Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar served throughout its operational life as the designated executive transport for Federated Suns and, after 3028, Federated Commonwealth senior leadership. Without weapons and with accommodations scaled for VIP passengers rather than military personnel, the design saw no front-line combat service, appearing instead in command-zone convoys, planetary capital circuits, and high-security installations where its combination of speed and armored protection made it a credible executive vehicle without demanding dedicated military escort. The design's Obsolete quirk, triggered in 3068, reflects its formal retirement from production as newer variants and alternative designs entered the Federated Suns executive fleet.","battleHistory":"The Deusenberg VIP Luxury Hovercar traces its origins to 3024, when First Prince Hanse Davion of the Federated Suns quietly commissioned a new class of armored limousine intended to impress the nobility of his entourage while providing genuine protection for senior personnel he planned to bring within range of active military operations. Davion was at the time preparing what would become the Fourth Succession War, and he understood that managing the Federated Suns and Lyran Commonwealth aristocracy accompanying him, none of them trained MechWarriors, would require a vehicle capable of surviving battlefield proximity without demanding military expertise from its occupants. Produced on New Avalon, the Deusenberg entered court service before hostilities commenced.\\n\\nThe vehicle became inseparably associated with the events of 3028, when Hanse Davion wed Melissa Steiner at a ceremony that also marked the opening of the Fourth Succession War. At this ceremony, Davion made his legendary declaration to his bride, announcing that his wedding gift to her was the Capellan Confederation itself. His words, \\"In honor of our marriage... I give you a vast prize. I give you the Capellan Confederation!\\", were destined to ring throughout history as a symbol of the age. Davion was well aware of the tempest he was inciting, and the Deusenberg's protective capabilities were not merely decorative during those dangerous years.\\n\\nThe most celebrated individual example of the type, designated the Deusenberg One, served as the First Prince's personal limousine from its commissioning until the vehicle's loss in the Jihad era. Hauptmann Gershvin Jackson served as its driver and caretaker until his death in 3051, considering his position and duties a matter of personal honor despite never having met the Prince directly. Following Hauptmann Jackson's death, the vehicle was modified during the 3050s to add enhanced electronics and active anti-personnel defenses suited to the elevated threat environment of the post-Clan Invasion era. After Katherine Steiner-Davion's assumption of power on New Avalon, the Deusenberg One fell into disrepair and was subsequently lost. It was last reported as of late 3062, its whereabouts unknown. The original configuration of the design was declared obsolete in 3068 as the Federated Suns' vehicle fleet modernized during the Jihad."}},"name":"~54","model":"(Original)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":6.05,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":"~55","equipment":[{"tag":"quarters-officer","uuid":"#0"}],"supportVehicle":"~56"},"20":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Kressly Warworks","primaryFactory":"Epsilon Eridani"},"components":{"chassis":"Kressly Warworks"},"text":{"overview":"The Dillinger Police Vehicle is a forty-ton hover support vehicle designed and built by Kressly Warworks on Epsilon Eridani in 3059. Created at the direct request of planetary president Pierre Benton, the vehicle was intended to give local police forces a credible and visible presence in cities overwhelmed by rising crime during the instability of the Chaos March period. Kressly's engineers deliberately expanded the vehicle's physical footprint beyond its strictly functional requirements, producing a hovercraft large enough to tower over civilian traffic and signal authority before a single officer stepped out. Although intended purely for law enforcement, its military-grade components gave it durability well suited to the volatile conditions of an independent world, and its blend of command, medical, and transport capabilities quickly attracted interest from buyers across the Inner Sphere.","capabilities":"The Dillinger is a hover support vehicle built around a fusion engine, giving it effectively unlimited operational range without refueling constraints. A cruise speed of seventy kilometers per hour allows it to respond rapidly across an urban area while still maintaining the imposing bulk that defines its visual deterrent role. Two tons of armor rated to BAR 10 provide meaningful protection against the small arms and light weapons most commonly encountered in riot and criminal interdiction environments, and an Armored Chassis Modification further reinforces the vehicle's structural integrity against improvised attacks.\\n\\nFor self-defense, the vehicle carries four military-grade machine guns distributed across its hull: one mounted in a roof turret for overhead engagement, one fixed forward, and one on each side. A single ton of ammunition supports extended patrols before resupply is required. The guns are loaded with rubber projectiles as standard but carry live ammunition as well, giving commanders flexibility in how they respond to escalating situations.\\n\\nThree integrated support systems define the Dillinger's operational role. A ton of military-grade communications equipment provides encrypted tactical coordination, allowing the vehicle to serve as a mobile command post directing multiple units across a district. Paramedic equipment is fitted in the rear compartment for immediate casualty treatment. A rear-access cargo bay provides space for police equipment and supplies, while a forward infantry compartment can carry over a dozen officers directly to an incident. The vehicle's Distracting design quirk reflects its intentionally overwhelming presence.","deployment":"The Dillinger serves law enforcement and paramilitary police roles in urban environments, functioning primarily as a mobile command post capable of directing operations across an entire district from a single vehicle. Its infantry transport capacity allows a full squad to be delivered directly to an incident without exposing officers to ambush during approach. Beyond law enforcement, individual units have found use as armored executive transport, emergency response coordination platforms, and tactical headquarters for small garrisons without dedicated command vehicles. The design is most commonly encountered operating alone or in pairs at the precinct level, though the Word of Blake Protectorate deployed them in coordinated groups as part of broader civil control operations across occupied worlds.","battleHistory":"The political dissolution of the Sarna March left Epsilon Eridani adrift as an independent world within the Chaos March, and the withdrawal of interstellar oversight brought a rapid deterioration in public order. The counties and palatinates of the planet found their local police forces wholly inadequate to contain the surge of criminal activity that accompanied the collapse of central authority. President Pierre Benton recognized that his Eridani Guards could not simultaneously address conventional military threats and rampant street crime, and in 3059 he contracted Kressly Warworks to develop a dedicated law enforcement platform.\\n\\nKressly's design entered production at their Epsilon Eridani factory and was issued to police departments across the planet's cities, where the vehicle's mass and military-grade systems gave law enforcement a degree of credibility their conventional equipment could not provide. Exports followed quickly. Free Worlds League police departments purchased the Dillinger in significant numbers before the Jihad, with the largest concentrations appearing on Irian, where corporate executives found the vehicle's armored bulk equally suited to use as a super-heavy limousine. The design's reputation for reliability in unstable environments made it a natural fit for any world experiencing civil unrest.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake's absorption of Epsilon Eridani during the Jihad brought the Kressly factory under Protectorate control. A team of Federated Suns researchers visiting the factory at the time of annexation disappeared without explanation, their fate unconfirmed. The Protectorate subsequently distributed the Dillinger to police forces across its territory, using the vehicle's conspicuous profile and law enforcement association to reinforce an image of civil order throughout worlds under Blake governance. By the end of the Jihad the Dillinger was present on every significant police force within the former Protectorate."}},"name":"Dillinger Police Vehicle","tonnage":40,"motiveType":"hover","troopSpace":3,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":5.275,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":{"front":6,"left":6,"right":6,"rear":6,"turret":7,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~57","$v":["~58",["machine-gun","#1","front",null],["machine-gun","#2","right",null],["machine-gun","#3","left",null],["paramedic-equipment","#4","rear",null],["machine-gun","#5","turret",null],"~59","~60"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"21":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Michaelson Aircraft","primaryFactory":"Ruchbah","targeting":"Advanced Fire Control"},"components":"~61","text":{"overview":"~62","capabilities":"~63","deployment":"The updated configuration, with light machine guns, Advanced Fire Control, and rear mine dispensers, became the definitive production standard in 3065 and the version most closely associated with the Fulmar's operational reputation. Ulysses Navy squadrons employed it in active patrol and convoy escort, using the mine dispenser capability to interdict coastal approaches and deny pirates the shallow-water transit routes they had previously exploited freely. The type's cargo capacity, sufficient for a full company of marines and their equipment, was demonstrated during the Penelope island assault and has been replicated in subsequent operations across the Federated Suns. The Fulmar has also attracted criminal users: smugglers recognized that the design's speed, amphibious capability, and military-grade sensors made it valuable for exactly the operations that naval patrols were trying to prevent.","battleHistory":"~64"}},"name":"Fulmar Patrol Craft","tonnage":70,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":8.5,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":8,"doors":1}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":"~49","equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":[["machine-gun-light","right","#0",null,null],["machine-gun-light","left","#1",null,null],"~65","~66","~67","~68",["~69",null,"#6",true,"machine-gun-light"]]},"supportVehicle":"~70"},"22":{"tro":{"production":"~43","components":"~61","text":{"overview":"~62","capabilities":"~63","deployment":"The initial Fulmar configuration entered service with the Ulysses Navy in 3056, replacing improvised armed Cormorants in convoy escort duties along the planet's major shipping lanes. The standard machine gun armament proved adequate against lightly armed pirate craft in most engagements, though crews identified limited accuracy against fast-moving targets as a meaningful constraint. The Original configuration remained in production only until the updated variant superseded it, and most units were subsequently upgraded or replaced rather than retained in their initial form. Small numbers served in patrol roles on other ocean-dominated worlds in the Federated Suns during the transition period.","battleHistory":"~64"}},"name":"Fulmar Patrol Craft","model":"(Original)","tonnage":70,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":16.5,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":"~49","equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":["~50","~51","~65","~66","~67","~68",["~28",null,"#6",true,"machine-gun"]]},"supportVehicle":"~70"},"23":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"The Galaport Ground Tug is a massive tracked support vehicle designed for heavy cargo operations at major DropPorts throughout the Inner Sphere. Originally built to serve the enormous Galaport facility outside Galatea City on Galatea, the design dates to 2457 and has remained essentially unchanged for centuries. The tug's primary purpose is hauling cargo trailers across the ferrocrete expanses of busy DropShip landing fields, moving supplies, equipment, and reaction mass between storage areas and grounded vessels. Versions of the design, ranging from modest luggage carts to true behemoths, operate on virtually every inhabited world in the Inner Sphere. The Galaport variant, built to handle the extreme demands of one of the Inner Sphere's busiest DropPorts, represents the high end of the type: a 200-ton fusion-powered crawler capable of moving freight loads that would challenge dedicated military logistics units.","capabilities":"Powered by a fusion engine and moving on wide tracked drives, the Galaport Ground Tug achieves a modest cruise speed of roughly twelve kilometers per hour and a flank speed approaching eighteen. This deliberate pace is adequate for DropPort work, where precision matters more than speed. The hull is protected by commercial-grade plating sufficient to withstand the hazards of active tarmac operations, including debris strikes, proximity to landing thrust, and minor vehicle collisions. Environmental sealing protects the three-person crew from exhaust gases, dust clouds, and other atmospheric hazards endemic to busy landing facilities.\\n\\nA tractor chassis modification allows the tug to couple with and haul the paired ground trailer. A forward-mounted searchlight provides illumination for operations in poor visibility, including the dense dust clouds raised by landing or departing DropShips. The vehicle carries no weapons and relies on commercial communications and navigation systems suited to its logistics mission rather than military-grade electronics. The absence of a turret and any armament is a deliberate trade-off, freeing mass for the structural and mechanical systems required to haul enormous cargo loads reliably over thousands of operational hours.","deployment":"The Galaport Ground Tug operates primarily at major DropPorts and spaceport facilities throughout the Inner Sphere. In standard DropPort operations, the tug pairs with one or more cargo trailers to haul freight between DropShips and storage facilities. The vehicle's environmental sealing and commercial durability make it suitable for operations on worlds with hostile atmospheric conditions, and its size allows it to work alongside even the largest DropShip classes. Outside of DropPort contexts, similar vehicles serve military logistics bases, supply depots, and large manufacturing facilities where bulk cargo movement over short distances is a regular requirement.","battleHistory":"The Galaport Ground Tug originated on Galatea during the Age of War, developed to meet the logistical demands of the massive Galaport DropPort complex outside Galatea City. As one of the largest DropPorts in the Inner Sphere, Galaport required industrial vehicles capable of handling the constant flow of cargo serving hundreds of simultaneously grounded DropShips. The drivers' unions that operated the crawlers took fierce pride in their work, claiming their vehicles moved more material than most standing armies, a boast that was difficult to dispute given the volume of traffic through Galaport at its peak.\\n\\nThe rise of Outreach as the premier mercenary hub of the Inner Sphere in the 3040s devastated the Galaport economy and with it the market for ground tugs. Most of the Galaport crawler fleet was scrapped or transferred offworld as mercenary traffic dwindled to a fraction of its former volume. A small number of tugs remained operational on the increasingly quiet ferrocrete fields, operated by skeleton crews waiting for business that rarely came. Those operators who maintained their equipment through the lean years developed an intimate familiarity with the vehicles that newer operators lacked.\\n\\nThe destruction of Outreach during the Jihad revived Galatea's fortunes and with them the demand for ground tugs. Mercenary traffic began returning to Galaport, and the surviving crawler operators found themselves well-positioned to capitalize on renewed demand. Unions pressed for expanded budgets to construct additional crawlers to handle the growing volume, and the long-dormant fleet gradually returned to full operational tempo. Throughout these changes, the fundamental design of the Galaport Ground Tug remained constant, a practical acknowledgment that a machine this well-suited to its purpose required no reinvention."}},"name":"Galaport Ground Tug","tonnage":200,"hasTurret":false,"cruiseMP":2,"armorAllocation":{"front":6,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":5,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":5,"frontRight":5,"rearLeft":5,"rearRight":5,"turret2":0},"equipment":[{"tag":"searchlight","location":"front","uuid":"#0"}],"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","environmental-sealing"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"24":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell"},"text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"The Command Post configuration equips the GESV tractor with seven tons of communication equipment and two Remote Sensor Dispensers, transforming the vehicle into a mobile headquarters. Requiring a nine-person crew, this configuration is deployed with command-level units at the company, battalion, and regimental echelons to provide flexible communications infrastructure in the field. Its mobility allows command elements to reposition frequently, reducing vulnerability to counter-battery fire and enemy intelligence targeting.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"name":"~74","model":"(Command Post)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":"~30","era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~75","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","pod","location"],"$v":[["~76","#0",true,null],["~18","#1",true,"rear"],["~18","#2",true,"rear"]]},"supportVehicle":"~77"},"25":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell"},"text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"The Field Kitchen configuration mounts two field kitchens alongside refrigerated and standard cargo to support sustained field operations. With an expanded kitchen crew of eight, the configuration feeds upward of 300 personnel per service. It is deployed primarily with large rear-echelon formations, refugee operations, and disaster relief missions where providing hot meals across a broad population is a significant morale and operational factor.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"name":"~74","model":"(Field Kitchen)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":{"$k":["tag","amount","doors"],"$v":[["cargo",3,1],["cargo-refrigerated",1.7,1]]},"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~75","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid","pod"],"$v":[["field-kitchen","#0",true],["field-kitchen","#1",true]]},"supportVehicle":"~77"},"26":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell"},"text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"The MASH configuration equips the GESV with a full MASH unit and six surgical theaters, supported by a medical complement of 36 personnel. It provides organic surgical capability to formations operating beyond the range of fixed medical facilities. The configuration is deployed immediately behind front-line units to minimize patient transit time, and is frequently paired with GESV Trailer bunk configurations to expand patient housing capacity.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"name":"~74","model":"(MASH)","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":2.5,"doors":1}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":"~75","equipment":[{"tag":"mash-core","uuid":"#0","pod":true}],"supportVehicle":"~77"},"27":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"The GESV Trailer is a fifteen-ton OmniVehicle designed as a dedicated companion to the GESV tractor. Built on an Omni chassis, the trailer is protected by one and a half tons of BAR 7 armor, sufficient to shield cargo and occupants against incidental fire and debris during rear-echelon operations. The trailer carries no engine of its own, relying entirely on the tractor for movement.\\n\\nThe trailer's OmniVehicle architecture provides full reconfiguration flexibility within available pod space. Unlike traditional cargo or personnel trailers, which commit to a single function from manufacture, the GESV Trailer's interior can be arranged into virtually any configuration that fits within tonnage limits. This makes the platform adaptable to shifting operational priorities without requiring different trailer types in the logistics chain.\\n\\nTwo GESV Trailers can be coupled and pulled simultaneously by a single tractor, allowing commanders to combine functions, such as pairing bunk space with a workspace module, to support diverse unit requirements from a single prime mover. The principal limitation of the trailer is its complete dependence on the tractor for mobility. Without the tractor, the trailer is stationary, which makes initial positioning decisions critical in dynamic operational environments.","deployment":"The Cargo configuration maximizes available interior space for general logistics transport, with a capacity of up to eleven and a half tons. The flexible interior can also serve as a workspace for intelligence personnel, staff officers, or technical specialists requiring office space in the field. It is the most versatile of the trailer configurations and is frequently deployed with any of the tractor configurations depending on the current operational requirement.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"name":"Generic Expandable Services Vehicle Trailer","model":"(Cargo)","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"omni":true,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":11.5,"doors":1}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":0,"armorAllocation":{"front":8,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","trailer","omni"],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"28":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Irian Technologies","primaryFactory":"Irian"},"components":{"engine":"Unknown (ICE)"},"text":{"overview":"The Hector Road Train Tractor is a 160-ton wheeled heavy transport vehicle introduced in 2500 to address a persistent logistics gap on worlds without adequate rail networks, where air freight and DropShip transport are prohibitively costly for bulk shipments. The Irian-built Hector serves as the prime mover of the Irian Road Train system, capable of hauling 150 tons of cargo or more across distances up to 2,500 kilometers. Built for multi-day overland runs without external support infrastructure, the tractor includes crew accommodations for two off-duty drivers, a kitchenette, and a miniature bathroom, allowing crews to rotate and maintain continuous movement. The Hector became a recognizable presence across the Free Worlds League and found use throughout the Inner Sphere, valued as a dependable logistics backbone for worlds that commercial trucking made accessible where rail did not reach.","capabilities":"Powered by an internal combustion engine, the Hector Tractor achieves a cruise speed of four movement points and a flank speed of six, with a 2,500-kilometer fuel range suited to the long overland hauls that define its role. Its wheeled propulsion system optimizes fuel efficiency on improved roads and prepared surfaces, though the large footprint of the 160-ton vehicle limits it to favorable terrain. The tractor carries 12 tons of cargo in its own compartment, supplemented by whatever trailer modules it tows. BAR 3 armor provides coverage appropriate for a civilian vehicle, offering basic protection against minor hazards rather than battlefield threats. The Hector has no weapons and is not intended for combat operations.\\n\\nThe tractor operates at full capacity when running independently, but performance degrades when pulling trailer modules, as documented in the operator manuals. Experienced operators plan routes and loads accordingly, typically limiting trailer combinations to two modules on flat terrain and to a single trailer where grades or poor road conditions are a factor. The built-in driver accommodations support continuous 24-hour operations: off-duty drivers rest while the vehicle moves, allowing the train to cover distances that would require multiple-day layovers under normal commercial trucking arrangements. Standard equipment fitments include an IR camera for low-visibility navigation and a lift hoist for cargo handling at destinations without dedicated unloading infrastructure.","deployment":"The Hector Road Train Tractor operates across the Free Worlds League and numerous Inner Sphere states as a commercial long-haul transport vehicle, typically assigned to planetary logistics contractors, government freight operations, and independent transport cooperatives. The tractor may run independently for short-haul or light cargo missions, or couple with one or two trailer modules for extended bulk operations. Performance degrades progressively with each additional trailer, and operators on difficult terrain typically limit loads to a single module. Crews of three manage extended multi-day operations using the built-in driver accommodations. The IR camera and lift hoist support independent cargo handling at destinations without dedicated unloading infrastructure.","battleHistory":"Irian manufacturers developed the Hector Road Train system in 2500 to address a structural gap in planetary logistics. On worlds without developed rail networks, bulk freight moved slowly and expensively. Air cargo and DropShip freight handled high-value goods, but agricultural commodities, raw materials, and manufactured goods required a ground transport solution that could match rail volume capacity at a fraction of the capital cost. Road trains offered that solution, and the Hector became among the most widely recognized implementations of the concept in the Free Worlds League.\\n\\nThe Irian design distinguished itself through practical crew amenities. Earlier road train designs required relay crews and overnight stops at way stations, introducing logistical complexity and schedule uncertainty. Irian's engineering team incorporated driver accommodations directly into the tractor unit, allowing a three-person crew to rotate through driving shifts continuously. The result was a vehicle that could sustain four days of uninterrupted travel before delivering its cargo, covering distances that competing designs managed only through elaborate support infrastructure. This capability proved especially valuable on frontier worlds where way stations were sparse or nonexistent.\\n\\nThe Hector spread through the Free Worlds League and into neighboring states through licensing and commercial export. Its civilian character insulated it from the worst effects of the Succession Wars: while military vehicles were destroyed or stripped for parts, commercial road trains continued to haul cargo, wear out through normal use, and be repaired by local workshops. The simplicity of the ICE-powered drivetrain and the availability of common wheeled vehicle components meant that maintenance could be performed far from any major manufacturing center.\\n\\nBy the Late Succession Wars, Hector tractors in various states of modification remained operational on dozens of worlds. Some units had been in continuous service for over a century, rebuilt repeatedly with locally sourced components. The design's endurance became its own form of legacy, a sign of the soundness of the original 2500 engineering. The Hector continued in service through the Clan Invasion era and into the Dark Age largely unchanged, as operators found little reason to replace a working vehicle when access to more sophisticated alternatives remained uncertain.","notes":"Unit is 1 ton over weight, removed 1 ton of Cargo to make legal."}},"name":"Hector Road Train Tractor","tonnage":160,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":11,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":9,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":9,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":8,"frontRight":8,"rearLeft":8,"rearRight":8,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":["~17","~78",["lift-hoist","#2","rear"],["quarters-crew","#3",null],["quarters-crew","#4",null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":18,"chassisModifications":["tractor"],"bar":3,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"29":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"ScolTek Associates","primaryFactory":"Inarcs"},"text":{"overview":"The Hi-Scout drones are remote-controlled reconnaissance vehicles produced by ScolTek Associates on Inarcs as integral components of the Hi-Scout Drone Carrier system. Introduced in 3000 for the Lyran Commonwealth Armed Forces, the drones were designed to extend the carrier's sensor network across a wide area without risking manned vehicles in hostile territory. Produced in two complementary variants, the hover-based NapFind and tracked PathTrak, these small unmanned platforms work in concert with their parent vehicle to create overlapping sensor chains capable of covering far more ground than the carrier alone. Though lightly built and essentially defenseless, the drones proved remarkably effective during the late Third Succession War, when sophisticated detection equipment was scarce across the Inner Sphere. ScolTek's design philosophy emphasized sensor capability and speed over durability, producing platforms that were expendable by intent but operationally invaluable to the commanders who employed them.","capabilities":"Both drone types share a common remote control architecture linked to the Hi-Scout carrier's StealthMat-Q communications system with MultiTrack coordination. This allows the carrier crew to manage all six drones simultaneously across separate sensor channels. The drones relay their findings in real time, building a composite picture of the operational area that far exceeds what any single vehicle could achieve. The system's primary limitation is its vulnerability to high levels of background radiation, which can disrupt the MultiTrack coordination link and leave drones unable to communicate with their parent vehicle.\\n\\nThe NapFind is a two-ton hover platform built for speed, capable of rapidly covering open terrain to establish initial contact with enemy forces. Its sensor package includes infrared, seismic, sound, and motion detection. The PathTrak is a heavier three-ton tracked platform with a more comprehensive sensor loadout, including the ability to intercept enemy radio transmissions, making it the primary intelligence-gathering element in the drone chain. Both variants carry minimal armor protection, sufficient only to shield their electronics from environmental hazards rather than hostile fire. Neither drone mounts weapons capable of engaging enemy combat units, and both rely entirely on speed, small size, and the disposable nature of unmanned platforms to survive on the battlefield.","deployment":"The NapFind is a two-ton hover drone designed for rapid area coverage. Employing a fuel cell engine and an advanced venturi-disk skirtless hover system, it achieves speeds approaching 250 kilometers per hour, allowing it to quickly sweep large areas and report contacts back to the carrier. The NapFind operates at ranges of thirty to fifty kilometers from its parent vehicle, using intermediary drones as relay stations when working at extended distances. Its sensor range of approximately ten kilometers is shorter than the PathTrak's, but the NapFind's speed allows it to cover proportionally more ground in the same time. Armed only with a pair of manipulators used primarily for demolitions work or hazmat handling, the NapFind has no combat capability and relies entirely on speed and its small profile to avoid engagement.","battleHistory":"ScolTek Associates developed the Hi-Scout drone family as part of an integrated reconnaissance system designed to fill a critical intelligence gap in LCAF planetary defense. During the late Third Succession War, dedicated scout vehicles were rare and BattleMechs assigned to reconnaissance roles were increasingly vulnerable to combined-arms ambushes. The drones offered a disposable alternative that preserved both expensive equipment and irreplaceable MechWarriors.\\n\\nThe NapFind and PathTrak entered service together in 3000, purpose-built to work as complementary halves of a sensor network. Early deployments proved the concept sound, and the LCAF quickly expanded Hi-Scout formations across garrison commands on less strategically vital worlds. The system's value as a ground-based early warning network made it particularly attractive for planets that lacked satellite reconnaissance coverage, a common situation during the technology-poor late Succession Wars era.\\n\\nThe drones' relatively simple construction made them vulnerable to reverse engineering. After the first LCAF Hi-Scouts were captured during a Kurita raid, functional copies and knock-off designs appeared across the Inner Sphere within two decades. ScolTek continued producing the original drones at full capacity through the Jihad, suffering no damage to their Inarcs facilities from the Word of Blake or other hostile forces. The original drone designs remained in production alongside upgraded carrier variants well into the Dark Age, a reflection of the fundamental soundness of the platform despite its aging technology base."}},"name":"Hi-Scout Drone","model":"(NapFind)","tonnage":2.5,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.015,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":15,"armorAllocation":{"front":1,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":0,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":[["drone-operating-system","#0",null],["manipulator","#1","front"],["manipulator","#2","front"],"~79"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"a","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"f","fuelTons":0.033,"chassisModifications":["ultra-light"],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"a","fireControl":"none"}},"30":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"The sealed Hoodling is a vehicle of extremely high quality that has been continuously manufactured on Terra since the twenty-ninth century. With its high speed, excellent communication and sensor suite, and comfortable crew accommodations, it is the premier covert operations vehicle for ComStar and its Explorer Corps. On a battlefield, there are few better eyes and ears than those of the Hoodling."}},"name":"Hoodling Sensor HoverJeep","tonnage":4.999,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~80","era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":{"front":2,"left":1,"right":1,"rear":1,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":["~17",["~18","#1","rear"],["seating-standard","#2",null],"~79"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":"~81","bar":5,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"31":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"The tiny and relatively slow HoverPod is a very common tool for motorized and mechanized infantry units. These vehicles are very small and can easily be transported by most APCs. They award a high measure of individual mobility to troopers, but are in no way actual combat units, as their use requires a level of dexterity that precludes the deployment of support weaponry."}},"name":"HoverPod","tonnage":0.25,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.04,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":5,"equipment":"~82","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"battery","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.052,"chassisModifications":["monocycle"],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"32":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Earthwerks-FWL, Incorporated"},"text":{"overview":"The Ibex RV is a two-ton wheeled utility vehicle introduced in 3049 by Earthwerks-FWL, Incorporated, designed to serve farmers and rural settlers across the Free Worlds League's more rugged worlds. Rugged, reliable, and capable off-road, the design quickly gained broader appeal far beyond its original agricultural market, becoming a fixture in urban centers where its oversized form and commanding presence made it a peculiar status symbol among the social elite. Law enforcement agencies and Free Worlds League Militia units also recognized its practical utility, adopting the platform for light patrol and security duties. A militarized variant followed almost immediately, adding heavier armor and light weaponry without fundamentally altering the vehicle's core character as a tough, go-anywhere utility truck.","capabilities":"The Ibex RV is built around a wheeled off-road chassis and powered by an internal combustion engine with a 500-kilometer range on its eleven-kilogram fuel tank. Civilian versions carry BAR 4 armor, sufficient for industrial hazards but not designed to stop weapons fire. The vehicle accommodates eight occupants including the driver, accessed through two side doors per side and a rear entry, making it well-suited for transporting small work teams across rough terrain. Its 601-kilogram cargo bay handles tools, equipment, and supplies for extended fieldwork.\\n\\nThe militarized version upgrades armor protection to BAR 7, adding meaningful resistance against light weapons fire. The initial military variant mounts twin King David man-pack Gauss rifles in a pintle arrangement, while the later Gauss variant places side-mounted King David light Gauss rifles for improved standoff capability. Neither variant is intended for frontline combat, but both provide security personnel and militia forces with a credible deterrent against unarmored threats and light opposition. The reduction in cargo capacity on the militarized version to 388 kilograms is a meaningful trade-off for operators who rely on the platform for logistics as well as security.","deployment":"The standard Ibex RV serves across the Free Worlds League in agricultural, commercial, and light government roles. Rural settlers and farming operations use it as a primary transport and logistics vehicle. Urban markets have adopted it as a premium utility vehicle, with security firms and corporate fleets operating it alongside civilian buyers. Law enforcement agencies deploy it for rural patrol and perimeter security on outlying worlds.","battleHistory":"Earthwerks-FWL, Incorporated introduced the Ibex RV in 3049, targeting the agricultural and rural communities that define much of the Free Worlds League's settlement patterns on its less-developed worlds. The design filled a genuine gap in the civilian utility market, offering a capable off-road vehicle with sufficient cargo capacity and passenger room to serve as a farm workhorse, a community transport, and a light logistics platform for rural businesses.\\n\\nDemand spread quickly beyond the fields. The Ibex's imposing size and rugged character attracted urban buyers who valued its image as much as its capability. In League cities, the trucks became familiar sights driven by security executives, corporate managers, and others seeking to project an image of active, self-reliant outdoor living. The gap between that aspiration and the reality of commuting and school runs earned the design the affectionate nickname of \\"snob tractors\\" among those who noted the irony of farm vehicles navigating city streets. Security services took a more practical view, appreciating the platform's durability and load capacity for patrol and perimeter work.\\n\\nRecognizing the military potential of the existing chassis, Earthwerks-FWL introduced the Militarized variant alongside the base model in 3049, adding heavier armor and twin King David man-pack Gauss rifles to equip militia and security forces. The Free Worlds League Militia integrated the type into light patrol and support roles on numerous member worlds. In 3064, a second militarized configuration replaced the pintle-mounted weapons with permanently fixed side-mounted King David light Gauss rifles, providing improved accuracy and greater standoff range for units operating in contested environments."}},"name":"Ibex RV","tonnage":2,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.601,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":2,"left":2,"right":2,"rear":2,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":["~5","~6","~7","~8","~9","~10","~11","~83"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.011,"chassisModifications":["off-road"],"bar":4,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"33":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Hoyle Systems","primaryFactory":"Zdice"},"components":{"engine":"Electric (Fuel Cell)","armor":"BAR 5"},"text":{"overview":"The Ina-du Swamp Skimmer is a civilian hover support vehicle produced by Hoyle Systems on Zdice, a world defined by the sprawling canal metropolis of Zaniz City. Introduced in 2870, the lightweight ten-ton design was purpose-built to navigate the city's dense network of narrow waterways, serving the taxi and courier trades that thrived among the millions of residents who depended on canal transport. Named from an ancient Cherokee word for snake, the vehicle's slim profile and high acceleration made it well-suited for threading the city's tighter water routes.\\n\\nOver the generations the Ina-du became so closely associated with Zaniz City life that it eventually transcended its role as a working vehicle, becoming a cultural fixture celebrated in the biennial Extraordinaire Canal Contest racing circuit and a symbol of the city's commercial identity.","capabilities":"The Ina-du Swamp Skimmer is engineered for agility and efficiency in confined urban waterway environments. At ten tons, it achieves a flank speed of 14 movement points using its fuel cell powerplant, making it responsive and quick for a vehicle of its configuration. The fuel cell drive provides efficient operation across 740 kilometers on a single fuel load, well-suited to the continuous service demands of a working taxi fleet. The hoverskirt design allows transit across both open water and low-gradient wet terrain, giving operators flexibility along canal edges and shallow banks.\\n\\nThe vehicle carries only minimal armor rated to BAR 5 standards, sufficient against incidental collisions and waterway hazards but meaningless in any combat scenario. It mounts no weapons and carries no military-grade systems. This is by design, reflecting Hoyle Systems' purely commercial intent for the platform.\\n\\nA precision electronic sensor suite linked to the primary rear steering vanes automatically adjusts small sections of the airskirt in real time, compensating for tight maneuvers and improving handling in the narrow passages typical of Zaniz City's canal grid. The system adds some maintenance complexity but is valued by operators for the gain in responsiveness. Standard configuration accommodates two crew and four passengers in a convertible seating arrangement, with 0.2 tons of cargo capacity available when the passenger layout is configured.","deployment":"The Ina-du Swamp Skimmer operates exclusively in the civilian sector and has never been adapted for military purposes. Its primary operational environment is the canal network of Zaniz City on Zdice, where Hoyle Systems has maintained uninterrupted production since its 2870 introduction. The vehicle is standard equipment across all three of the city's major taxi unions and is widely used for courier, freight, and passenger transport on the waterway grid.\\n\\nIts lack of weapons, minimal armor, and passenger-focused configuration make it unsuitable for any role beyond civilian transport. Beyond its working life, the Ina-du also sees competitive use in the biennial Extraordinaire Canal Contest, where strict regulations require all participating vehicles to be drawn from active motor pool assets, ensuring the racing circuit remains a showcase for the production model rather than purpose-built machines.","battleHistory":"Hoyle Systems introduced the Ina-du in 2870 to address the growing transportation demands of Zaniz City, a sprawling metropolis of over ten million residents laced with a complex network of canals and waterways. The design entered service quickly, adopted across the three major taxi unions that controlled fares and routes on the city's water grid. Over the following decades Hoyle made few significant changes to the design, maintaining the three-man skimmer's basic configuration while updating internal systems incrementally to reflect available components.\\n\\nThe hoverskirt was overhauled in 2997, the most significant engineering change in the vehicle's history. The redesign improved the precision airskirt adjustment system and was well-received across the taxi fleets, providing a measurable gain in handling in the tightest sections of the canal grid.\\n\\nCompetition among the taxi unions for routes and fares grew increasingly contentious through the late twenty-ninth and early thirtieth centuries. By 2999, over four hundred accidents and incidents in a single year reflected how violent that competition had become. After three members of the DeWalt Group died in what union leadership described as accidents, representatives from all three unions met to address the crisis. Their first conference resolved little, but out of those talks emerged the concept of a formal, organized outlet for competition. Jonas Silt proposed the Extraordinaire Canal Contest, a high-speed racing circuit using existing motor pool vehicles on a complex water course through the city.\\n\\nThe first ECC was held in 3001 over three days, drawing over 300,000 spectators despite initial resistance from the Zaniz City council. The event proved a commercial and cultural success. The ECC Racing Board adopted biennial scheduling, revealing a new eighty-kilometer course for each contest. Recognizing the commercial opportunity, Hoyle Systems developed a customization line for the Ina-du to serve the growing aftermarket. The Zdice Custom Expo grew from this activity, drawing over half a million visitors by 3066 to view accessories and custom show skimmers."}},"name":"Ina-du Swamp Skimmer","tonnage":10,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.2,"doors":1}],"era":"early-sw","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":{"front":4,"left":4,"right":4,"rear":2,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":["convertible"],"bar":5,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"34":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Interstellar Expeditions|Napa Tidal Research - Branson United","primaryFactory":"Blue Hole|Blue Hole"},"components":{"engine":"Fusion"},"text":{"overview":"The Jonah JN-002 is a miniaturized submersible designed for deep-water exploration and research in extreme liquid environments. Its origins trace to Ryan Systemics, an oceanographic research subsidiary of the Ryan Cartel, which had specialized in water-world environmental analysis for decades. When the Cartel's financial troubles triggered a liquidation cascade, the working prototype of a new deep-water submarine was nearly lost. The survival of that design, and its eventual transformation into the production Jonah, represents one of the more unlikely industrial recoveries in the Periphery's civilian technology sector. Introduced in 2714 on the oceanic world of Blue Hole, the Jonah carved out a niche market among deep-sea research organizations and resource extraction operations working in environments hostile to standard marine craft. Though never produced in large numbers, the design proved remarkably well-suited to its intended mission.","capabilities":"The Jonah is an ultra-light submersible weighing two thousand kilograms, driven by a fusion drive that delivers moderate underwater speed. A cruise pace of four and a flank pace of six are adequate for navigating deep-ocean environments without excessive energy consumption. The hull is rated to withstand depths exceeding ten kilometers, well beyond the operational envelope of most comparable craft. Armor protection is minimal, using a BAR 3 construction rating that prioritizes pressure resistance over combat survivability. The Jonah carries no weapons of any kind.\\n\\nStandard equipment includes a forward-mounted searchlight, twin manipulator arms at the bow for sample collection and site work, and a rear-mounted remote sensor dispenser. Crew complement is one operator with two passenger positions, giving the vessel a total capacity of three personnel. Life-support systems are rated for continuous dives exceeding ten days without resupply.\\n\\nThe design's chief weaknesses are its absence of defensive armament and its classification as an obsolete, non-standard design throughout much of its operational life. Sourcing replacement components requires custom fabrication or dedicated supply chains. Cargo capacity is limited to under two hundred kilograms, constraining the volume of samples or equipment the sub can carry on a single mission. These limitations narrow the Jonah's appeal to well-supported organizations with established logistics networks.","deployment":"The JN-002 is manufactured at the Interstellar Expeditions facility on Blue Hole and distributed exclusively to IE member organizations. Its missions center on deep-ocean scientific investigation, underwater resource surveys, and environmental monitoring in liquid environments beyond the reach of surface or near-surface craft. The Jonah has seen deployment across Periphery water worlds where IE-sponsored expeditions regularly operate under conditions requiring sustained deep-dive capability. Its manipulator arms and remote sensor systems make it particularly suited for sample collection and site documentation. Operators outside IE-affiliated networks face significant logistical challenges in sourcing replacement components, effectively limiting the design to well-supplied organizational fleets with dedicated support infrastructure.","battleHistory":"Ryan Systemics began as the environmental science arm of the Ryan Cartel, tasked with studying water-world ecosystems and developing filtration technologies for colonial water supplies. As new filtration methods undercut the Cartel's broader profits, Ryan Cartel began liquidating subsidiaries, and Systemics faced dissolution. A working prototype submarine, informally called the Jarvis, had just completed qualification trials when the liquidation began. Trevor Stefanis, a Systemics employee, managed to remove the prototype before the facilities were foreclosed, preserving the only working example of what would eventually become the Jonah.\\n\\nDecades later, Trevor's great-grandson Unger Stefanis brought the dormant Jarvis design to Napa Tidal Research on Blue Hole in 2714. Pairing with Branson United to establish a manufacturing line, the venture faced immediate production constraints: a single assembly line required eight months to build each vehicle. Despite this bottleneck, sales outpaced production throughout the partnership's existence. The NTR-BU venture collapsed within a decade, around 2724, and the factory fell silent for more than three centuries. The Jonah acquired its obsolete designation during this long dormancy, and surviving units grew increasingly difficult to maintain as original components aged out of any supply chain.\\n\\nIn 3032, Interstellar Expeditions acquired the derelict factory site on Blue Hole. Restoration took fifteen years and over 1.6 billion Kroner, but by the mid-3040s the plant was again producing Jonahs, this time exclusively for IE member corporations. The revival coincided with renewed interstellar exploration activity across the Periphery, and the Jonah found a second generation of customers among teams investigating deep-ocean worlds and resource-rich aquatic environments. During this same period, Unger Stefanis had leveraged the Cokery Isles chain near Blue Hole as a testing ground for underwater purification infrastructure, establishing Blue Hole Bottling and reshaping the local planetary economy in the process.","notes":"Armor exceeds point limit for 2.0-ton Submarine support vehicle: 5 points > 4."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"~84"}],"name":"Jonah Submarine","model":"JN-002","tonnage":2,"motiveType":"naval-sub","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.194,"doors":1}],"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":2,"left":1,"right":1,"rear":0,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":["~85",["manipulator","front","#1"],["manipulator","front","#2"],["~18","rear","#3"],"~86","~87","~88"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["submersible","ultra-light"],"bar":3,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"35":{"tro":{"components":{"jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Knox Armored Car is among the most common civilian security vehicles in the Inner Sphere, descended from a Terran Hegemony transport design known as the Ranger. Built on a reinforced five-ton truck chassis, the Knox provides modest protection against small arms and light criminal weaponry for high-value cargo movements across dozens of inhabited worlds. Its light armament functions primarily as a deterrent, discouraging opportunistic theft while signaling that a convoy is not entirely undefended. The vehicle's low cost and straightforward maintenance requirements have made it the default security transport for planetary constabularies, banking institutions, and commercial enterprises that cannot justify or afford dedicated military vehicles. Knox-type vehicles appear throughout the Inner Sphere in the service of anyone who must move valuable cargo through contested or unstable environments, forming the unglamorous backbone of civilian security logistics from the Succession Wars era onward.","capabilities":"The Knox is built on a wheeled medium truck platform powered by an internal combustion engine, giving it adequate performance on improved roads and paved surfaces. Its cruise and flank speeds are sufficient for convoy escort and urban patrol work, keeping pace with commercial traffic without requiring military-grade power plants or maintenance infrastructure. The vehicle's BAR 10 armor provides fifteen total points of protection distributed across its hull and turret, adequate against small arms and fragmentation but offering little resistance to military weapons. This armor level is deliberately matched to the threat environment the Knox is designed for: criminal elements carrying civilian firearms and light anti-vehicle weapons, not organized military opposition.\\n\\nThe Knox mounts a single turret-mounted machine gun with a hundred rounds of ammunition, its primary purpose being visual deterrence rather than combat effectiveness. A basic fire control system supports the gunner with minimal targeting assistance. The vehicle carries 1.5 tons of standard cargo, enough for small payroll shipments, sensitive equipment, or priority personnel. The wheeled drive system performs well on prepared surfaces but restricts the Knox to urban and developed terrain, limiting its utility outside of cities and established road networks. The Knox is explicitly not a combat vehicle, lacking the firepower or armor to engage military opposition and the cargo capacity to serve effectively as an armored personnel carrier.","deployment":"Knox Armored Cars operate throughout the Inner Sphere, appearing wherever commercial enterprises, planetary governments, or militia organizations require inexpensive armored transport for high-value cargo. Their most common roles are payroll escort, banking transport, and the movement of sensitive equipment across worlds too poor to afford dedicated military security assets. The Knox's prevalence ensures that replacement parts remain widely available, simplifying logistics for operators across multiple factions. The vehicle performs best when treated as a deterrent platform with infantry escort rather than a standalone combat asset, and operators who attempt to use the Knox as a fighting vehicle against military-grade opposition do so at their considerable peril.","battleHistory":"The Knox's predecessor, the Ranger, entered service with the Terran Hegemony as a military utility transport. When the Star League collapsed and the Succession Wars fragmented interstellar industry, the basic design survived by transitioning from military to civilian application. Local manufacturers on dozens of worlds adapted the platform to available industrial components, producing Knox-type vehicles from common truck parts and locally fabricated armor plating. The resulting vehicles varied considerably in quality but retained the fundamental characteristics of the original design: light, cheap, and easy to maintain.\\n\\nThroughout the Succession Wars, planetary militias occasionally pressed Knox cars into limited security roles, a function the design handled adequately as long as it faced only criminal opposition. The growing availability of military-grade hardware in criminal hands, particularly as black market salvage from the constant warfare of the era found its way into civilian channels, steadily eroded the Knox's effectiveness. By the late thirty-first century, well-organized criminal organizations and rogue military units possessed weapons capable of defeating Knox armor with ease, and the lack of any anti-vehicle capability left Knox crews with few options beyond attempting to flee.\\n\\nThe Briggs Armored Car robbery of late 3059 on Alexandria crystallized the problem. A criminal gang with access to surplus Grey Death Legion Battle Armor overwhelmed a Knox convoy despite a BattleMech escort, exploiting the fundamental mismatch between the Knox's civilian armor rating and the capabilities of modern military hardware. The incident attracted commercial and media attention across the Lyran Alliance, generating industry discussion about the gap between the Knox-class civilian security standard and the threat environment that Inner Sphere operators actually faced. This episode directly motivated Gienah Automotive to begin development of the Bailey Armored Car as a credible alternative."}},"name":"Knox Armored Car","tonnage":12,"motiveType":"wheeled","bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":1.5,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":3,"left":3,"right":3,"rear":3,"turret":3,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":["~22",["~28",null,"#1",true,"machine-gun"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"36":{"tro":{"production":"~89","text":{"overview":"The Koi Heavy Transport is a large wing-in-ground-effect cargo vehicle designed and manufactured by Wakazashi Enterprises for the Draconis Combine's domestic transport market. Introduced in 3056, the Koi was created to address a recognized gap in planetary logistics on Combine worlds where DropShip transport is economically impractical for routine cargo movement. Wakazashi positioned the design for the civilian sector, emphasizing cargo capacity and long-range overwater transit over military utility.\\n\\nThe Koi's enormous two-hundred-forty-ton frame carries over one hundred twenty tons of reconfigurable cargo, and its fusion powerplant provides operational range limited only by crew endurance. Coordinator Theodore Kurita's economic modernization reforms drove demand for precisely this kind of high-capacity domestic transport, and the Koi quickly became a fixture of commercial logistics across the Draconis Combine and neighboring states.","capabilities":"The Koi's most significant asset is its cargo flexibility. The main bay accommodates standard freight containers, infantry formations, wheeled and tracked vehicles, or even a single BattleMech, with quick-release flotation tanks and powerful pumps enabling rapid reconfiguration between load types. This versatility makes the Koi useful for planetary militia and light garrison commands that lack dedicated combat transport assets.\\n\\nWing-in-ground-effect movement gives the Koi effectively unlimited overwater range. Its amphibious chassis modification allows bay doors to be opened while the vehicle is afloat without risk of flooding, enabling loading and unloading from open coastlines or shallow anchorages with no port infrastructure required. Cruise speed over water is modest, though flank speed is adequate for emergency maneuver away from a threat.\\n\\nThe Koi's limitations are considerable for any military context. It carries no weapons, relying entirely on escorts for protection. All WiGE vehicles are inherently vulnerable to conventional aircraft and aerospace fighters, and the Koi's enormous radar profile makes evasion impossible. Its large size means combat damage while carrying a full load risks catastrophic loss of both cargo and vehicle. The design is also exposed to ground fire if forced to transit near hostile positions at low altitude.","deployment":"The Koi Heavy Transport serves primarily as a commercial cargo vehicle throughout the Draconis Combine, operated by trading houses, planetary governments, and militia logistics commands. Export units are found across the Inner Sphere wherever cost-effective long-range overwater cargo movement is needed. The design is unarmed and unsuited for frontline service, but its enormous payload makes it an indispensable asset in rear-area logistics and disaster relief operations. On Tabayama, the Hunters International collective operates a large fleet for hunting and agricultural transport across the world's extensive croplands.","battleHistory":"Wakazashi Enterprises developed the Koi against a backdrop of rising commercial demand across the Draconis Combine's interior worlds. Coordinator Theodore Kurita's economic modernization program, begun in the decades before the Clan Invasion, created pressure on domestic logistics networks that existing transport infrastructure struggled to meet. Wakazashi, already an established producer of military WiGE vehicles, recognized an opportunity in the civilian cargo market and committed development resources to a purpose-built design.\\n\\nThe Koi entered production at the Wakazashi facility on Dover in 3056 and found ready buyers among planetary governments, trading houses, and militia commands throughout the Combine. Its combination of payload and aquatic flexibility made it especially attractive on worlds with extensive coastlines or inland waterways. The Hunters International collective on Tabayama adopted the design early, using it to move hunting parties and equipment across the world's vast croplands far faster than ground vehicles could manage.\\n\\nThe commercial success of the Koi attracted unwanted attention. In 3061, a factory fire on Dover halted production for three months. Wakazashi executives publicly accused Pesht Motors, the Combine's primary manufacturer of civilian transports, of deliberate sabotage. An investigation confirmed the fire was arson, but no definitive evidence linking Pesht Motors to the incident was produced. Relations between the two corporations have remained strained since, and the disruption gave Wakazashi internal justification to shift some research and development activity off Dover. That decision would later shape how the Koi's military derivatives were developed.\\n\\nThe Koi has continued as a commercial backbone for planetary logistics in the Draconis Combine, with export units reaching neighboring states. Militia commands short on dedicated transport WiGEs have repeatedly pressed civilian Koi units into emergency service during periods of conflict, a role for which the vehicle was not designed but has proven capable of fulfilling."}},"name":"Koi Heavy Transport","tonnage":240,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":123.5,"doors":2}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":14,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":14,"frontRight":14,"rearLeft":13,"rearRight":13,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["amphibious"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"37":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"New Hessen ArmorWorks|Wu Industries|Wu Industries","primaryFactory":"New Hessen|Capricorn III|Sian"},"text":{"overview":"The Korvin Tank emerged in the early 2300s as the Capellan Confederation's purpose-built response to large laser technology. Constructed under the supervision of engineer Wu Han at New Hessen ArmorWorks on New Hessen, the design exploited reverse-engineered Terran Hegemony weapons to give Confederation forces a decisive edge over contemporary armored vehicles. Named in honor of explorer and philosopher Alana Korvin DeVall, the Korvin became the standard battle tank of the Capellan Confederation for much of the 24th century. A 50-ton tracked medium vehicle powered by a fusion engine, it combined energy firepower and solid speed in a package uncommon among vehicles of the era. The Korvin served Capellan forces for over two centuries across multiple production sites.","capabilities":"The Korvin's primary identity is as an energy weapon platform. The Wu Heavy Laser in the turret delivers considerable damage at medium and longer ranges without ammunition dependency, while a Longbow LRM-10 rack provides indirect fire support to engage targets beyond the laser's effective reach. Five Browning Large Caliber machine guns are positioned to cover all arcs of the vehicle, deterring infantry from closing. The Magna 250 fusion powerplant drives the 50-ton hull to a flank speed of 86 km/h, giving the Korvin the mobility to serve as a flanking unit or pursue retreating forces alongside heavier assets.\\n\\nEight tons of ProtecTech 4 armor offers reasonable protection for a medium vehicle, though it falls short of the most heavily armored vehicles in its weight class. The fusion engine, while providing a weight advantage over internal combustion rivals, proved demanding to maintain. Environmental controls and other internal systems are persistent reliability concerns, a consequence of the design's rushed development. The tank's unusual silhouette, another artifact of that rapid design process, makes it easily recognizable on the battlefield but offers no benefit in concealment.","deployment":"The KRV-2 served in the front-line armored regiments of the Capellan Confederation during the 24th century, where its large laser firepower gave it a genuine edge against the vehicles of the era. As BattleMechs came to dominate the battlefield and armor technology advanced, the KRV-2 transitioned to garrison and militia roles, serving roughly another century and a half in secondary assignments. With the introduction of the KRV-3, most surviving KRV-2s were sold to Periphery states rather than scrapped. Spare parts for the fusion reactor are now extremely scarce, and operational examples are exceptionally rare.","battleHistory":"The Korvin's origins trace to the Tikonov Grand Union, which spent decades contesting border systems with the Terran Hegemony. Despite numerous defeats, Tikonov engineers salvaged Hegemony technology from the battlefield and eventually replicated the large laser. Engineer Wu Han oversaw the translation of that achievement into a functional combat vehicle, producing the KRV-2 at New Hessen ArmorWorks in 2367. This second-generation design, named for the philosopher Alana Korvin DeVall, featured a lighter and more powerful fusion engine than its Hessen-series predecessor, increasing top speed by roughly thirty percent. Production later expanded to Capricorn III and Sian with the formation of Wu Industries.\\n\\nThe Korvin served as the backbone of Capellan armored forces through much of the 24th century. Assigned to premier regiments, it gave Confederation armor a genuine advantage in an era when few vehicles mounted adequate protection against large laser fire. The design's prominence helped shape early Capellan armored doctrine, and the tank became a symbol of Confederation military capability. As armor technology improved across the Inner Sphere, however, a single heavy laser was no longer sufficient to dominate the battlefield, and the Korvin was progressively shifted to garrison and militia duties.\\n\\nWu Industries introduced the KRV-3 in 2430, attempting to revitalize the aging design with improved performance. The new model performed adequately but could not reverse the Korvin's decline in the face of proliferating BattleMechs. Production on Capricorn III and Sian continued until Wu Industries closed its doors in 2612. Most remaining KRV-2s were sold to Periphery states rather than scrapped, while the design's influence reached even further when the Umayyad Caliphate began producing its own version on Granada in the 29th century.","notes":"Armor exceeds point limit for 50.0-ton Tracked support vehicle: 105 points > 104."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~24","~33",["quirk-obsolete",2612]]},"name":"Korvin Tank","model":"KRV-2","tonnage":50,"cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":22,"right":22,"rear":17,"turret":21,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~26",["large-laser","turret","#1",null],"~27",["~28",null,"#3","machine-gun"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#4","lrm-5"]]},"supportVehicle":"~42"},"38":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Pion-Laurier","primaryFactory":"Chenier"},"components":{"engine":"Unknown (ICE)","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Lama-Deux is a thirty-ton Medium VTOL manufactured by Pion-Laurier on Chenier and one of the established firefighting platforms in Federated Suns civil emergency service. Introduced in 3022, the Lama-Deux predates its companion design by more than four decades and represents the conventional engineering tradition to which Pion-Laurier returned after its PEQRAF experimental program failed in the early 3060s. The aircraft is designed specifically for combating high-rise and skyscraper fires, mounting dual high-speed water nozzles on cantilever arms that can be operated from the pilot's console or directed remotely by a firefighter positioned inside the burning structure. Lighter and more maneuverable than the Cascatelle, the Lama-Deux excels in dense urban airspace where larger aircraft cannot safely operate, and its range of over a thousand kilometers ensures broad planetary coverage.","capabilities":"The Lama-Deux carries just over eleven tons of liquid firefighting agent in its primary cargo bay, delivering it through two high-speed nozzles mounted on cantilever arms extending from the fuselage. These nozzles can be joystick-controlled from the operator's station in the cabin or operated remotely by a firefighter inside the target building, allowing precise directional suppression without requiring the aircraft to enter the most dangerous areas near the building face. Two sprayers mounted on the right and left sides supplement the primary nozzles for broader area coverage, while a mounted searchlight provides illumination for night operations and smoke-heavy environments. The aircraft carries six passengers in addition to its two-person crew, supporting rescue extraction or the insertion of firefighting teams.\\n\\nThe Lama-Deux shares the Cascatelle's cruise and flank speeds despite its smaller size, achieving roughly fifty-four and eighty kilometers per hour respectively. Armor protection is modest at BAR 3, consistent with the lighter construction of a platform not expected to operate in contested airspace. The dual VTOL rotor arrangement provides the hovering stability required for extended stationary positioning alongside a burning high-rise, which is the most demanding operational profile the design was built to handle. The absence of weapons reflects the Lama-Deux's purely civilian role, with no provision for self-defense.","deployment":"The Lama-Deux serves fire departments in urbanized regions throughout the Federated Suns, with the greatest concentration on worlds featuring dense high-rise construction and skyscraper-heavy metropolitan centers where ground ladder equipment cannot reach the required elevation. Its compact footprint and remote-operable nozzle system make it particularly well suited to airspace-congested environments where larger aircraft cannot maneuver safely near a structure. Civil fire services on industrialized worlds frequently operate Lama-Deux units alongside Cascatelles to provide both precision high-rise suppression and broader area coverage within the same squadron. On smaller or less populated worlds the design's range also supports planetary coverage from a single base, with response times acceptable for most emergency scenarios.","battleHistory":"The Lama-Deux entered service with Federated Suns planetary fire departments in 3022, establishing itself as a standard high-rise firefighting platform decades before Pion-Laurier developed the larger Cascatelle. The design's age and proven reliability made it a natural anchor for the manufacturer's product line when the PEQRAF experimental program failed in the early 3060s, and the Lama-Deux's continued production alongside the newer design reflects sustained demand for its specialized urban role. Fire services have found that the Cascatelle and Lama-Deux serve complementary functions, with the latter providing precision suppression capability in confined high-rise environments that the larger aircraft cannot safely enter.\\n\\nThe design was developed to address a category of emergency that conventional ground equipment and ladders cannot reach: fires in tall structures where the building's own suppression systems have failed and the blaze is above the operational ceiling of standard firefighting apparatus. This problem has intensified on heavily urbanized Federated Suns worlds as cities expanded vertically throughout the Succession Wars era, creating a steady demand for platforms like the Lama-Deux. The aircraft's remote nozzle capability was recognized early as a significant safety advantage, allowing operators to suppress fires in areas where smoke or structural instability would make direct approach lethal.\\n\\nThe Lama-Deux remains in production at Pion-Laurier's Chenier facility well into the thirty-second century, evidence of the durability of its basic design. Some fire services have updated individual aircraft with improved communications equipment over the years, but the core airframe and suppression system have required few modifications to remain operationally relevant. Its longevity stands as a counterpoint to Pion-Laurier's PEQRAF misadventure, demonstrating the enduring value of straightforward engineering in civil emergency equipment."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"~90","parameter":"dual"}],"name":"Lama-Deux Firefighting VTOL","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo-liquid","amount":11.393,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":{"front":11,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":5,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":["~85",["sprayer-vehicular","right","#1"],["sprayer-vehicular","left","#2"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":3,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"39":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"S. L. Lewis, Inc.","primaryFactory":"Carlisle"},"text":{"overview":"The Lewis Skimmer Bus is a large civilian hover transport produced by S. L. Lewis, Incorporated, introduced in 3026 near the close of the Third Succession War. Built as the company's first entry into the civilian passenger market after the success of the Savannah Master hovercraft, the Lewis offers high capacity passenger transport with meaningful cargo flexibility. Its large hover chassis provides terrain adaptability beyond what wheeled or tracked alternatives can offer, and its fuel cell powertrain made it an appealing option for transit operators concerned with air quality and fuel availability. The vehicle's introduction coincided with a modest period of economic recovery in the Lyran Commonwealth, where renewed interest in civilian infrastructure created demand for capable and cost-effective mass transit platforms.","capabilities":"The Lewis Skimmer Bus is built on a large hover chassis and weighs 80 tons fully equipped. Its fuel cell electric power plant provides clean, quiet operation well suited to enclosed transit environments and urban areas with air quality restrictions. The plant delivers enough power to push the vehicle to flank speeds exceeding 160 km/h, making it one of the faster large hover transports in civilian service. Cargo is accessed through a rear door, with 18.05 tons of standard cargo capacity available alongside seating for 86 passengers.\\n\\nThe vehicle is crewed by three personnel: a driver and two stewards who manage passengers and cargo. BAR 4 commercial armor covers the exterior, providing protection against minor collisions and road hazards. The Lewis carries no armament. The primary operational constraints are its large footprint, which limits access to narrow urban streets and passages, and its weight class, which requires dedicated maintenance facilities capable of servicing large support vehicles. A small number of plainclothes security personnel can be placed among the passengers when valuable cargo requires discreet protection.","deployment":"The Lewis Skimmer Bus operates primarily on high-capacity civilian transit routes in urban and metropolitan areas across the Lyran Commonwealth and former Federated Commonwealth territories. Transit authorities deploy it on high-density inter-district routes and as connectors between population centers where large passenger volumes justify the vehicle's size and maintenance requirements. The rear cargo bay makes the Lewis attractive for mixed passenger-and-freight operators on routes where dedicated freight vehicles are impractical. When carrying high-value cargo, operators may deploy plainclothes security personnel among the passengers, allowing protection without any outward military appearance. Deployment is generally limited to regions with maintenance facilities equipped for large support vehicles.","battleHistory":"S. L. Lewis, Incorporated was established in or shortly after 3017 by Samuel Laurence Lewis, a former warrant officer who recovered a large cache of Omni 25 fusion engines from an abandoned Star League supply depot found while serving aboard the JumpShip Winnetka. Rather than liquidating the salvage, Lewis used the engines to found a manufacturing company and develop the Savannah Master hovercraft for the Lyran Commonwealth Armed Forces. The military vehicle proved successful, and the company expanded into engine production and later the civilian market.\\n\\nThe Lewis Skimmer Bus entered production on Carlisle in 3026, representing the company's first civilian product. Shortly after its introduction, commanders on Severn pressed available Lewis buses into service as improvised infantry transports, ferrying troops to and from the front lines as the Third Succession War wound down and dedicated APCs ran short. The episode showed that the large hover platform could serve adequately in auxiliary military roles despite having no such intent in its design.\\n\\nThe vehicle found commercial success in the Lyran Commonwealth and beyond. During the FedCom era, the Carlisle facility operated under Federated Commonwealth ownership and continued production through the Clan Invasion. Following the breakup of the Federated Commonwealth, ownership reverted to the Lyran Alliance, and the Lewis remained in production through the Jihad and into the Republic era. S. L. Lewis, Inc. diversified over time, expanding its manufacturing footprint and developing the Edasich Omni 25 engine in partnership with Edasich Motors to sustain its vehicle lines when the original salvaged engine supply was exhausted."}},"name":"Lewis Skimmer Bus","tonnage":80,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":18.05,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-rn","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":{"front":11,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":10,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":9,"frontRight":9,"rearLeft":9,"rearRight":9,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":["~5","~6","~7","~8","~9","~10","~11","~83","~91","~92","~93","~94","~95","~96",["seating-standard","#14"],["seating-standard","#15"],["seating-standard","#16"],["seating-standard","#17"],["seating-standard","#18"],["seating-standard","#19"],["seating-standard","#20"],["seating-standard","#21"],["seating-standard","#22"],["seating-standard","#23"],["seating-standard","#24"],["seating-standard","#25"],["seating-standard","#26"],["seating-standard","#27"],["seating-standard","#28"],["seating-standard","#29"],["seating-standard","#30"],["seating-standard","#31"],["seating-standard","#32"],["seating-standard","#33"],["seating-standard","#34"],["seating-standard","#35"],["seating-standard","#36"],["seating-standard","#37"],["seating-standard","#38"],["seating-standard","#39"],["seating-standard","#40"],["seating-standard","#41"],["seating-standard","#42"],["seating-standard","#43"],["seating-standard","#44"],["seating-standard","#45"],["seating-standard","#46"],["seating-standard","#47"],["seating-standard","#48"],["seating-standard","#49"],["seating-standard","#50"],["seating-standard","#51"],["seating-standard","#52"],["seating-standard","#53"],["seating-standard","#54"],["seating-standard","#55"],["seating-standard","#56"],["seating-standard","#57"],["seating-standard","#58"],["seating-standard","#59"],["seating-standard","#60"],["seating-standard","#61"],["seating-standard","#62"],["seating-standard","#63"],["seating-standard","#64"],["seating-standard","#65"],["seating-standard","#66"],["seating-standard","#67"],["seating-standard","#68"],["seating-standard","#69"],["seating-standard","#70"],["seating-standard","#71"],["seating-standard","#72"],["seating-standard","#73"],["seating-standard","#74"],["seating-standard","#75"],["seating-standard","#76"],["seating-standard","#77"],["seating-standard","#78"],["seating-standard","#79"],["seating-standard","#80"],["seating-standard","#81"],["seating-standard","#82"],["seating-standard","#83"],["seating-standard","#84"],["seating-standard","#85"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":4,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":4,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"40":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"United Airframe|United Airframe","primaryFactory":"New Earth|Keid"},"components":{"engine":"Fusion","armor":"BAR 10 Commercial"},"text":{"overview":"Lexan Oceanic is a luxury transport manufacturer with roots in the mid-twenty-first century, when the firm designed and decorated high-end properties for the wealthy elite of the Inner Sphere. Under the direction of Corrine Yeardly, the company partnered with Boeing United in 2311 to enter the personal VTOL market. The renamed United Airframe company established production facilities on New Earth and Keid, producing custom aircraft that quickly became status symbols among interstellar corporate executives and nobility.\\n\\nThe original Series II personal VTOL established Lexan's reputation for exclusive, fusion-powered luxury aircraft during the Star League era. The larger Series VI luxury VTOL followed centuries later in 3053, expanding the product line to serve an even more demanding clientele. Both designs cater to the ultra-wealthy, offering unlimited range and bespoke interiors unmatched by any competitor.","capabilities":"The Series II is a medium VTOL weighing fifteen tons, powered by a compact fusion engine that gives it unlimited operational range and impressive speed. One ton of BAR 10 commercial armor provides protection comparable to standard military-grade plating, while a defensive ECM suite makes electronic detection difficult. The aircraft's six-passenger cabin is custom-fitted for each buyer, with exotic materials and appointments unique to every airframe. Foldable wings allow the Series II to be stored in a DropShip cargo bay for convenient interstellar transport.\\n\\nThe Series VI triples the Series II's mass at sixty tons, trading top speed for interior space and amphibious capability. Its fusion engine maintains unlimited range, while the armored and amphibious chassis allows water landings. The VI features first-class quarters, 2.5 tons of BAR 10 armor, and a 1.5-ton cargo capacity. Neither design carries armament of any kind, relying instead on speed, ECM protection, and low observability rather than firepower. The lack of weapons is a deliberate design choice, as any military hardpoints would undermine the craft's civilian certification and insurance classification.","deployment":"The Series II remains in production at United Airframe's New Earth facility, serving its original niche among corporate executives and the ultra-rich. Despite centuries of production interruptions, demand for the custom-built personal VTOL has never wavered among those who can afford it. The aircraft's compact size and foldable wings make it a practical transport option for executives who travel between worlds, allowing the VTOL to accompany its owner aboard DropShips and JumpShips.","battleHistory":"Lexan Oceanic began as a design firm specializing in luxury interiors for the wealthy, decorating everything from penthouse apartments to low-orbit space station suites and custom DropShips. Owning a Lexan product signified power, money, and social position among the Inner Sphere's elite. In 2311, Corrine Yeardly steered the company into aircraft production through a partnership with Boeing United, a small subsidiary of the larger Boeing conglomerate responsible for passenger VTOL production. Using a standard VT-1922 airframe, Lexan provided custom specifications while Boeing United handled construction. The venture proved enormously profitable, and Lexan shareholders eventually acquired Boeing United outright. The newly renamed United Airframe opened factories on New Earth and Keid.\\n\\nThe Series II became a favorite among interstellar corporate leaders during the Star League era. Each aircraft was unique in its appointments, containing such luxuries as Terran redwood floors, Capolla jade filigree, Acamarian diamond strings, and Tharkad coral inlay. The last Series II built before the fall of the original factories was commissioned for Stefan Pretanis, CEO of Earthwerks. Both United Airframe factories were destroyed during the First Succession War by Davion and Steiner troops, ending production for centuries.\\n\\nLexan Oceanic survived by returning to interior decoration for the rich and famous. In 3047, CEO Jason Ullman-Yeardly repurchased the New Earth facility from IrTech after IrTech exited the airframe business following a corporate reshuffling. Lexan's R&D division developed the Series VI, a larger and more versatile design three times the size of the Series II. The Series VI entered production in 3053 after a brief delay caused by the Clan Invasion. The most expensive Series VI ever produced was a pair of custom aircraft built for ex-Archon Katrina Steiner in 3066, whose combined customization costs exceeded the base airframe price by 42 million C-bills. Katrina never received her aircraft. Archon Peter Steiner donated them to the Tharkad Make-a-Dream Foundation in September of 3067."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~97","~13"]},"name":"Lexan Oceanic Personal VTOL","model":"Series II","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.55,"doors":1}],"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":15,"armorAllocation":{"front":7,"left":3,"right":3,"rear":2,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":[["ecm-suite","#0"],"~6","~7","~8","~9","~10","~11"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"41":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Joint Equipment Systems|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|King Dumount Defense Facility|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium|Quikscell Company|Quikscell Company|Aldis Industries|Amaris Armored Vehicles","primaryFactory":"Alshain|Ares|Broken Wheel|Castile|Kalidasa|Layover|Maxwell|Oliver|Pandora|Terra|Timbuktu"},"components":"~0","text":{"overview":"The LRM Carrier is a sixty-ton tracked combat vehicle designed to deliver massed long-range missile fire at minimal cost. Developed by the Quikscell Company as a modernized successor to primitive Age of War designs dating back to the 2440s, the current production model entered service around 2470 and has remained in continuous use ever since. Manufactured by nearly every major military supplier across the Inner Sphere, the LRM Carrier fills the role of cheap, expendable indirect fire support in armies that cannot afford dedicated artillery assets. Its reputation as a disposable weapons platform belies its effectiveness when properly employed in massed formations, where concentrated volleys of missiles can devastate even heavily armored targets.","capabilities":"The primitive LRM Carrier represents the original Age of War design developed by Quik Products Inc around 2440. Built as a fifty-five-ton support vehicle using primitive construction methods, it mounts three LRM-20 launchers behind BAR 6 armor rated for battlefield protection but far below modern military standards. The InterComBust 180 engine and basic fire control systems perform adequately but lack the refinements of later production models.\\n\\nAdvanced fire control systems partially compensate for the crude construction, and the design's straightforward maintenance requirements made it accessible to militaries with limited technical infrastructure. These vehicles were rendered obsolete by the modernized carriers that entered production around 2580, though a handful survived in Periphery arsenals for decades afterward.","deployment":"The primitive LRM Carrier served during the Age of War era, providing massed missile fire for armies still relying on pre-spaceflight military technology. Quik Products Inc manufactured these vehicles primarily on Ares and Pandora. The design was rendered obsolete by modernized carriers around 2580 but established the weapons carrier concept that would endure for centuries.","battleHistory":"The origins of the modern missile carrier stretch back to the Age of War, when Quik Products Inc developed the first standardized tracked platforms for massed missile delivery. These early designs, constructed using primitive technology, were crude but effective, establishing the basic formula of maximum firepower on a minimal chassis that would define the weapons carrier concept for centuries. As Quik Products evolved into the Quikscell Company, the original primitive carriers were refined into the modern designs that entered widespread service by the twenty-fifth century.\\n\\nDuring the Succession Wars, the desperate need for cheap fire support led dozens of manufacturers across the Inner Sphere to produce their own versions, many of them little more than unauthorized copies of Quikscell designs built to even lower quality standards. Shortly before the Fourth Succession War, Quikscell launched a series of lawsuits against these clone manufacturers, successfully shutting down many smaller competitors and forcing larger firms to cease production of derivative designs. The company further consolidated its market dominance by offering to exchange any non-Quikscell carrier for legitimate replacement vehicles and parts, effectively cornering the missile carrier market. One company not targeted by the legal attacks was Joint Equipment Systems, which had negotiated in good faith with Quik Products Inc centuries before.\\n\\nLRM Carrier production spread across an extensive network of facilities. Quikscell plants on Ares, Kalidasa, Layover, Oliver, Pandora, and Broken Wheel turned out thousands of units across every era. Joint Equipment Systems on Alshain, Aldis Industries on Terra, Amaris Armored Vehicles on Timbuktu, the King Dumount Defense Facility on Castile, and Maxwell Planetary Defense Consortium on Maxwell all contributed to a production network spanning the entire Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe first significant modernization came in 3055, when Quikscell introduced an upgraded variant for the Federated Commonwealth. By incorporating ferro-fibrous armor and Artemis IV fire control systems linked to each launcher, engineers dramatically improved missile accuracy while adding a C3 slave unit for networked targeting. The reduced engine size was considered an acceptable trade for the enhanced combat capability. Oliver and Richvale became the primary production sites for this upgrade package.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake produced their own radical revision during the Jihad era, utilizing an extralight fusion engine to boost both speed and armor protection while retaining Artemis IV systems and adding a C3i computer. Aldis Industries on Terra manufactured this variant throughout the Blakist occupation. In the Dark Age, Joint Equipment Systems developed the LRM Carrier C for the Rasalhague Dominion, mounting the launchers in a partial-traverse turret with hardened armor inspired by the success of the Viking IIC. The LRM Carrier's continued evolution across more than six centuries of service stands as sign of the enduring viability of cheap, massed missile platforms in modern warfare."}},"quirks":"~1","name":"LRM Carrier","model":"(Primitive)","tonnage":55,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":"~2","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["lrm-15","front","#0",null],["lrm-15","front","#1",null],["lrm-15","front","#2",null],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#3","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#4","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#5","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#6","lrm-15"],["ammo-lrm-standard",null,"#7","lrm-15"]]},"supportVehicle":"~4"},"42":{"tro":{"production":{"primaryFactory":"Sian"},"components":{"engine":"ICE","armor":"BAR 4"},"text":{"overview":"The Mao-Heng Charioteer is a light wheeled patrol vehicle commissioned in 2797 by Chancellor Sandol Quinn of the Capellan Confederation. Designed exclusively for the Palace Guard on Sian, the Mao-Heng was intended as a law enforcement cruiser capable of navigating urban streets under fossil fuel power. Despite its sleek exterior, the vehicle earned a reputation for sluggish handling and underwhelming performance almost immediately after entering service. Production was extremely limited, with fewer than two thousand units completed before the line was shut down. The design languished in obscurity for over two centuries before academic researchers revived it as a modernized police cruiser in the mid-thirty-first century.","capabilities":"The Mao-Heng Charioteer is a ten-ton wheeled support vehicle powered by an internal combustion engine. Its top speed of just sixty kilometers per hour makes it one of the slowest patrol vehicles ever fielded, a shortcoming that proved a constant liability during urban policing operations. The vehicle carries no weapons whatsoever, relying entirely on its crew and any embarked personnel to provide armed response. Eight passenger seats allow it to function as a personnel transport for small security details or prisoner transfers, with an additional four hundred kilograms of rear-mounted cargo space for equipment or evidence storage.\\n\\nProtective armor is minimal, rated at BAR 4, offering only modest resistance to small arms fire and virtually no protection against military-grade weaponry. The two-person crew consists of enlisted, non-rated personnel, reflecting the vehicle's purely civilian law enforcement role. While the Mao-Heng's low operating costs and simple maintenance requirements offered some practical advantages for cash-strapped planetary constabularies, these benefits could not offset its fundamental shortcomings in speed and survivability.","deployment":"The (Original) designation refers to the initial Mao-Heng Charioteer as produced from 2797 to 2799 under Chancellor Quinn's patronage. This variant carries the Obsolete design quirk for the period of 2801 through 3043, reflecting the two-century gap between the cancellation of first-run production and the vehicle's revival as a modernized police cruiser. The original production run saw exclusive deployment with the Palace Guard and Capellan Hussars on Sian, where the vehicles served in an urban law enforcement capacity until the last unit was retired in 2834.","battleHistory":"In early 2797, Chancellor Sandol Quinn personally established the requirements for a new patrol vehicle to serve the Palace Guard on Sian. The specifications called for a wheeled design powered by fossil fuels with a minimum speed of sixty kilometers per hour. Quinn's procurement process attracted considerable controversy, as rumors persisted that wealthy associates with interests in the fossil fuel mining industry had influenced the contract requirements. Multiple companies submitted entries, and despite several more promising candidates, the sleek but sluggish Mao-Heng was selected.\\n\\nInitial production lasted fewer than two years, yielding less than two thousand vehicles before political upheaval ended the program. When Dame Ilsa Liao came of age in 2801, Quinn abdicated his stewardship of the throne in favor of the rightful Liao heir. Shortly thereafter, production of the Mao-Heng was suspended and eventually cancelled entirely.\\n\\nFor several years afterward, the remaining Mao-Hengs continued to patrol the streets of Sian under the fanatically loyal Capellan Hussars. The vehicles developed a mixed reputation during this period. The most notorious example was Patrol 13, which gained infamy beginning in July 2809 after its fifth consecutive month of being towed away from armed confrontations with Triad malcontents. Historians on Purvo later claimed the vehicle received numerous commendations, though irregularities in their records suggest the awards reflected the uncommon valor of the Hussars assigned to the unlucky cruiser rather than any merit of the vehicle itself. Patrol 13 was the last Mao-Heng decommissioned, finally retired in 2834 and purchased by Duke Jian Kao of Purvo for his personal collection.\\n\\nThe Mao-Heng remained forgotten for over two centuries until 3042, when a team of student researchers led by Professor George Sheng at the Sian Regional Training Center selected the design as a graduation project. Their internally reworked prototype performed well enough that the Ministry of Transportation took notice. Within six months, a contract was awarded to Professor Sheng to oversee construction of a new production line featuring the modernized Mao-Heng. The vehicle was once again designated as a law enforcement standard issue, and production as a police cruiser resumed with plans for it to replace older patrol vehicles across Capellan worlds."}},"name":"Mao-Heng Charioteer","model":"(Original)","tonnage":10,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":0.5,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.4,"doors":1}],"era":"early-sw","cruiseMP":6,"armorAllocation":{"front":4,"left":4,"right":4,"rear":3,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":4,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"43":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Mótuö Chë Shang","primaryFactory":"Sian"},"components":{"chassis":"Mótuö Chë Shang Monocycle Chassis Modification","engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Mótuö Chë Shang No. 2 is a handcrafted luxury monocycle produced by the Mótuö Chë Shang family enterprise on Sian. Founded in 2467 by Trun Shang, the company built its reputation around faultless craftsmanship and thorough customer service, securing its first commission from the Chancellor's court in 2469. The No. 2 has remained a coveted status symbol within the Confederation's upper social strata ever since. Production is deliberately limited to a few dozen units annually, with every vehicle assembled by hand and customized entirely to the buyer's specifications. No two No. 2s are identical, and the company regards each machine as a commission rather than a product.","capabilities":"The No. 2 is a 320-kilogram wheeled support vehicle built on a Monocycle Chassis Modification, a single-wheel drive arrangement uncommon among production civilian vehicles. Its internal combustion engine produces a cruise speed approaching 130 kilometers per hour and a flank speed near 195 kilometers per hour, providing performance appropriate to its prestige positioning. A pillion seat accommodates a second rider of up to 25 kilograms, and 25 kilograms of storage is available beneath the seat. No armor or weapons are fitted.\\n\\nThe No. 2's Equipment Rating of C/D-D-D reflects its civilian orientation and limited but consistent availability to buyers of sufficient means. The \\"Easy to Maintain\\" design quirk demonstrates that extreme customization need not preclude practicality: the No. 2 uses proven, thoroughly understood components that any competent technician can service without specialist tooling. Parts wear predictably and can be fabricated locally, a deliberate design choice that reflects the company's philosophy of enduring reliability over fashionable novelty.","deployment":"The Mótuö Chë Shang No. 2 is found almost exclusively among Capellan nobility, senior government officials, and wealthy patrons of sufficient social standing to secure a commission from the workshop. With each example unique and output deliberately limited, the vehicle functions as much as a commissioned artwork as a practical transport. Occasional examples reach destinations beyond Sian through diplomatic gifts and private transactions. The company does not pursue export markets and has never sought to expand production volume, regarding the deliberate scarcity as inseparable from the product's identity and value.","battleHistory":"The Mótuö Chë Shang company was established on Sian in 2467 by founder Trun Shang, whose philosophy held that excellence required patient refinement rather than commercial ambition. Two years after its founding, the company secured its first commission from the Chancellor's court, beginning a relationship with Capellan aristocracy that would sustain the workshop across centuries of political upheaval. Each No. 2 was built entirely to the buyer's order, with design alterations made only after long deliberation and incorporated gradually rather than as discrete model changes.\\n\\nThe company's deliberately limited scale proved an advantage during the Succession Wars, as the workshop on Sian operated below the threshold of industrial targeting that devastated larger manufacturing operations during that period. The Shang family continued producing its handful of annual commissions across the centuries of conflict, the vehicle's design evolving slowly in keeping with the founder's principles. Following the Xin Sheng cultural revival of the late 3050s and 3060s, the workshop under Howard Shang's direction began incorporating traditional Chinese iconography into its designs, giving the latest-generation No. 2s a visual character closely tied to Capellan cultural heritage. By the Civil War era, the company continued operating with fewer than a dozen craftsmen, its small annual output as sought-after as ever among the Confederation's upper social circles."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"quirk-easy-to-maintain"}],"name":"Mótuö Chë Shang No.2","tonnage":0.32,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.025,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":12,"equipment":[{"tag":"seating-pillion","uuid":"#0"}],"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.01,"chassisModifications":["monocycle"],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"44":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Blue Bull, Inc."},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Nifty Transportable Field Repair Unit is an ICE-powered knockoff of Johnston Industries' JI-50 Jifty, produced by Blue Bull, Inc. during the Jihad era. Appearing in the latter half of the 3060s, the Nifty blatantly copied the Jifty's basic design while substituting a cheaper internal combustion engine for the original's fusion powerplant. The vehicle was marketed as a budget alternative to the Jifty for military organizations that could not afford or obtain the Federated Suns original. Despite legal challenges from Johnston Industries over patent violations, the chaos of the Jihad prevented any meaningful resolution, and the Nifty entered limited production.","capabilities":"The Nifty follows the Jifty's general layout as a tracked field repair vehicle, retaining the lift hoists and cargo capacity that define the role. Its most significant departure from the original is the substitution of an internal combustion engine for the Jifty's fusion powerplant. This reduces the vehicle's logistical independence, tying it to fossil fuel supply lines that the fusion-equipped Jifty avoids entirely. The cost savings are considerable, however, making the Nifty accessible to smaller militaries and mercenary commands that cannot justify the expense of a fusion-powered support vehicle.\\n\\nThe ICE powerplant also limits the Nifty's performance compared to its progenitor, though exact specifications remain poorly documented. Industrial-grade armor provides basic crew protection consistent with the vehicle's rear-echelon role. Like the Jifty, the Nifty is not designed for direct combat and relies on escorting forces for protection in contested areas. Its value lies in providing field repair capability to organizations that would otherwise lack any mobile maintenance platform.","deployment":"The Nifty saw limited adoption during the Jihad, primarily among smaller military organizations and independent commands unable to secure Johnston Industries' Jifty through normal procurement channels. Johnston Industries' JI-100, a larger and more capable successor to the Jifty introduced in 3074, proved that the original design concept was sound enough to scale upward. Military commands gradually upgraded from the JI-50 to the JI-100, and the Nifty found no lasting place on AFFS support rosters.","battleHistory":"Blue Bull, Inc. entered the field repair vehicle market in the latter half of the 3060s by copying Johnston Industries' JI-50 Jifty design. The company made no attempt to disguise the origins of their product, producing what was essentially a direct clone with a cheaper ICE powerplant substituted for the original's fusion engine. Johnston Industries initially attempted to stop the infringement through legal action, but the onset of the Jihad in 3067 made pursuing patent litigation effectively impossible.\\n\\nJohnston Industries' leadership quickly recognized that the courts offered no practical remedy and shifted strategy. Rather than continue an unwinnable legal battle, they directed their research and development resources toward creating a superior successor vehicle. The result was the JI-100 Transportable Recovery Repair Unit, introduced in the first quarter of 3074 to enthusiastic reception. The JI-100 massed 50 tons on a heavy tracked chassis with significantly greater cargo capacity than either the Jifty or the Nifty, positioning Johnston Industries firmly ahead of its imitator.\\n\\nThe Nifty's market position eroded steadily as the post-Jihad military rebuilding favored established manufacturers with proven supply chains. Blue Bull's inability to match Johnston's engineering improvements or factory capacity meant the Nifty remained a budget option in a market increasingly willing to pay for quality. The vehicle's legacy is primarily as a footnote in the Jifty's history, a reminder that wartime chaos creates opportunities for imitation that peacetime litigation would otherwise prevent."}},"name":"Nifty Transportable Field Repair Unit","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":19.5,"doors":1}],"era":"jihad","armorAllocation":"~98","equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":[["machine-gun-light","front","#0",null,null],["machine-gun-light","front","#1",null,null],"~99",["lift-hoist","rear","#3",null,null],["~69",null,"#4",true,"machine-gun-light"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"45":{"tro":{"production":"~100","components":"~101","text":{"overview":"The Obuzaabaa Tactical Vehicle is a wheeled command-and-control platform produced by Pesht Motors for the Draconis Combine's civilian police forces. Introduced in the late thirtieth century, the low-slung personnel carrier has served as the backbone of Combine law enforcement operations for over a century. Known colloquially as the 'zaabaa among the Friendly Persuaders, the vehicle fills a critical niche in low population zones where the minicars and bicycles common to dense urban areas are impractical. Its versatile design supports roles ranging from routine patrol coordination to tactical team deployment, and it can even coordinate with local DCMS units when situations escalate beyond standard police response.","capabilities":"The Obuzaabaa prioritizes communications capability over crew comfort, with its interior crammed with equipment for coordinating tactical teams, patrol groups, and local military units. A fuel cell engine provides a respectable top speed of 80 km/h, adequate for most law enforcement pursuits across the varied terrain of Combine worlds. The vehicle carries four tons of configurable cargo space that crews customize for their assigned missions. Standard loadouts include riot gear, emergency supplies, roadway assistance kits, and criminal suppression systems. Some vehicles carry temporary prisoner transportation cubes for field detention operations. Units assigned to more tactical duties replace standard cargo with additional weapons, surveillance equipment, and sensor arrays. An infantry compartment allows transport of a small team of officers, supplementing the vehicle's primary command role with a limited direct response capability. The utilitarian crew space reflects the design's emphasis on mission equipment over operator amenities.","deployment":"The Obuzaabaa serves throughout the Draconis Combine's police and Friendly Persuader formations. In sparsely populated regions, the vehicle operates as the primary law enforcement platform, replacing the lighter transport options used in high density urban areas. Its command-and-control capabilities make it the standard coordination vehicle for multi-unit police operations, and it frequently works in tandem with the closely related Sasayaku Control Transport.","battleHistory":"Pesht Motors introduced the Obuzaabaa in 2965 during the Late Succession Wars. The vehicle quickly became a fixture of Combine police operations, benefiting from Pesht Motors' unrivaled civilian vehicle production network. As the manufacturer of over sixty-five percent of the Combine's civilian wheeled vehicles, Pesht Motors ensured wide distribution across hundreds of worlds.\\n\\nThe Word of Blake's nuclear strikes against Pesht in 3071 destroyed the Pesht Motors manufacturing site, disrupting production of numerous civilian and military vehicles. Despite this setback, the sheer number of Obuzaabaa vehicles already distributed across Combine space ensured the design remained a common sight in police fleets well into the thirty-second century. The vehicle's simple civilian construction also made field maintenance straightforward, further extending its operational lifespan."}},"name":"Obuzaabaa Tactical Vehicle","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":4,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":4.5,"doors":1}],"era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~102","equipment":[{"tag":"~76","uuid":"#0"}],"supportVehicle":"~103"},"46":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"GSB, Incorporated","primaryFactory":"Tiber"},"components":{"engine":"240 Fusion","armor":"BAR 6 Standard"},"text":{"overview":"The Paramour Mobile Repair Vehicle was introduced in 2557 by Gilbert, Sachs, and Belafonte, a Tiber-based conglomerate in the Free Worlds League. Designed during the period when the League prepared to join the Star League, the Paramour represented a deliberate strategic choice to invest in logistics rather than direct combat capability. GSB, Inc. applied its expertise in luxury rough-terrain vehicles to create a mobile repair platform that would keep FWLM units operating at peak readiness in the field. The result was a vehicle that combined comfort, reliability, and genuine utility in a sixty-ton tracked chassis. The FWLM was so impressed with the design that it purchased every Paramour produced. Though the vehicle's polished appearance sometimes drew mockery from combat formations, the Paramour earned lasting respect through the competence and professionalism of its crews.","capabilities":"The Paramour is built on a sixty-ton tracked chassis powered by a fusion engine capable of reaching speeds up to 65 km/h. This mobility allows it to keep pace with armored formations while carrying the tools and parts needed for field repairs. The vehicle's primary asset is its spacious cargo area, which holds fifteen tons of spare parts and equipment for a wide variety of maintenance tasks. A pair of lift hoists enables the Paramour to handle loads up to sixty tons each, sufficient to lift and reposition damaged BattleMechs or heavy combat vehicles on the battlefield.\\n\\nThe crew compartment accommodates three personnel, with quarters comfortable enough to serve as a mobile field headquarters for quartermasters. A five-ton infantry bay provides space for additional technicians or a security detail. For self-defense, the vehicle mounts a single GM MiniGun machine gun in the front, providing a visible deterrent without adding excessive weight. BAR 6 armor offers adequate protection against small arms fire and shrapnel, while the armored chassis and controls modification provides additional resilience. The Paramour was never intended to see direct combat, and its light armament reflects that design philosophy.","deployment":"The base Paramour was deployed exclusively with Free Worlds League Military formations from its introduction in 2557 through the Star League era and into the Succession Wars. As a dedicated logistics platform, it served in support echelons behind the front lines, maintaining and repairing BattleMechs and vehicles damaged in combat. The vehicle's combination of cargo capacity and lifting power made it particularly valuable to independent FWLM regiments operating far from fixed repair facilities.","battleHistory":"The Free Worlds League commissioned the Paramour in the mid-twenty-sixth century as part of a broader effort to bring the FWLM up to Star League Defense Force standards. League planners recognized that matching the SLDF in firepower was impractical, and instead focused on logistical excellence as a way to demonstrate institutional competence. Gilbert, Sachs, and Belafonte, a conglomerate better known for building exotic excursion vehicles for wealthy Free Worlders, won the production contract. The company's experience with luxury vehicles translated into an unusually refined design for a military support platform.\\n\\nDuring the Star League era, duty aboard a Paramour became a prestigious assignment within the FWLM. Quartermasters routinely established their field headquarters aboard the vehicles, and daily inspections of crew areas and uniforms became standard practice. This attention to presentation drew scorn from visiting SLDF officers, who dismissed the Paramour crews as more concerned with appearance than function. The reputation proved unfair. When called upon to service combat units in the field, Paramour crews consistently delivered high-quality work that eventually won over even their harshest critics.\\n\\nGSB, Inc. did not survive the Second Succession War, and production of the Paramour ceased permanently. Although the vehicles were rigorously maintained wherever they served, attrition and entropy gradually thinned the ranks of functional examples over the following centuries.\\n\\nThe Jihad and the collapse of federal authority brought renewed attention to surviving Paramours. Two vehicles appeared on Regulus, where they were reconditioned for service with the Regulan Hussars. By replacing the quarters and cargo space with a mobile field base, the Hussars transformed the Paramour into a true repair vehicle suited to sustained independent operations. The Regulan Prince's intentions for deploying these rare vehicles remained a subject of speculation."}},"name":"Paramour Mobile Repair Vehicle","tonnage":60,"hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":5,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":15,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~98","equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":["~104",["lift-hoist","rear","#1",null,null],"~99","~105"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"47":{"tro":{"production":"~106","components":{"chassis":"Johnston Industries","engine":"Johnston Industries"},"text":{"overview":"The Prairie Schooner Land Train is a large wheeled support vehicle produced by Johnston Industries on New Syrtis, introduced in 3058. Johnston engineers drew on manufacturing data recovered from the Helm Memory Core to develop the design, including the techniques needed to build its fusion powerplant. Conceived for developing worlds lacking reliable rail or air networks, the ninety-ton tractor connects with up to three modular trailer units to form a complete land train capable of moving bulk cargo across hundreds of kilometers of undeveloped terrain. It quickly established itself as a commercial workhorse across the Capellan March, linking remote agricultural and mining communities to regional distribution centers. While not a military design, the Prairie Schooner's carrying capacity and cross-country mobility have occasionally drawn it into logistics support roles during wartime.","capabilities":"The Prairie Schooner tractor is built on a heavy wheeled large vehicle chassis with tractor and off-road modifications, enabling reliable mobility across the rough terrain typical of frontier worlds. Its fusion powerplant provides an effectively unlimited operational range, which is its most significant advantage over petrochemical competitors. The trade-off is cost: at roughly half a million C-bills for a complete land train, the Prairie Schooner is well outside the budget of smaller operators who can manage with less capable alternatives.\\n\\nThe tractor carries 17 tons of cargo in its main bay, accessible through a single rear door, and the bay serves as a warm hold by default for general freight. Its self-righting technology allows the vehicle to recover from rollovers in all but the most severe accidents, a feature that adds operational confidence on uneven ground. Operator quarters accommodate a second crew member for extended journeys without resupply stops.\\n\\nSix and a half tons of light composite armor provide protection against environmental hazards and minor threats common on undeveloped worlds, though the tractor carries no weapons from the factory. Customers on worlds with significant predator populations frequently add remotely operated light weapon blisters, typically armed with small arms or light support weapons. The Prairie Schooner is not designed to withstand military-grade weapons fire, and operators are advised to withdraw well before combat reaches their position.","deployment":"Prairie Schooner Land Train tractors operate across frontier and underdeveloped worlds throughout the Inner Sphere, with the heaviest concentrations in the Capellan March and adjacent regions. Civilian operations focus on bulk cargo transport between isolated settlements and regional hubs, with common payloads including grain, ore, manufactured goods, and construction materials.\\n\\nMilitary logistics units in multiple factions have conscripted or contracted Prairie Schooners for rear-echelon supply work, particularly in operational areas where road infrastructure is limited and refueling is impractical. Operators on hostile worlds routinely add light weapon systems to their tractors for defense against wildlife and low-level bandit threats.","battleHistory":"Johnston Industries began development of the Prairie Schooner in the mid-3050s, leveraging production data recovered from the Helm Memory Core by the New Avalon Institute of Science. Among the most valuable pieces of that recovered data were the manufacturing methods for fusion drive systems, which Johnston applied directly to the Prairie Schooner's powerplant. The design entered production at their New Syrtis facility in 3058, debuting as one of the more technically sophisticated civilian transport vehicles of the era.\\n\\nCommercial uptake was strong across underdeveloped worlds throughout the Capellan March. Agricultural communities valued the Prairie Schooner's ability to haul refrigerated and bulk cargo over long distances without concern for fuel supply, while resource extraction operations used standard cargo configurations to move ore from remote mine sites to processing centers. Johnston Industries marketed the system aggressively in the region, and the Prairie Schooner became closely associated with the March's frontier economy.\\n\\nThough civilian in purpose, the design attracted military attention almost immediately. The Word of Blake's 31st Division pressed Prairie Schooner trains into service on New Avalon at the outset of the Jihad, using them as part of their supply chain during their occupation of the Federated Suns capital. This was not an isolated incident. Commanders across multiple factions found that large land trains were practical logistics platforms when air transport was unavailable or at risk, and Prairie Schooners appeared in rear-echelon supply columns across several major campaigns.\\n\\nThe New Syrtis plant suffered severe damage during the Jihad. Taurian Concordat raids in 3076 struck the facility directly, and radiation contamination from those attacks rendered much of the production floor unusable for years afterward. Johnston Industries responded by upgrading their existing component facility on Benet III to a full production line, maintaining Prairie Schooner output throughout the disruption. The New Syrtis plant's recovery was slow, complicated by the broader devastation of the capital world and ongoing contamination cleanup efforts that extended well into the Republic era.\\n\\nProduction continued on Benet III through the Dark Age and into the ilClan period, with New Syrtis eventually returning to production as conditions stabilized. The design remained largely unchanged across its production run, its original specifications proving durable enough that major revisions were never pursued."}},"name":"Prairie Schooner Land Train","tonnage":90,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":17,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":34,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":30,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":30,"frontRight":30,"rearLeft":30,"rearRight":30,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":[["quarters-crew","#0"],["quarters-crew","#1"],["quarters-crew","#2"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"48":{"tro":{"production":"~106","components":{"chassis":"Johnston Industries"},"text":{"overview":"The Prairie Schooner Module is the standardized trailer component of the Johnston Industries Prairie Schooner Land Train system, designed to be towed by the Prairie Schooner tractor. Introduced alongside the tractor in 3058, each thirty-ton module connects to the tractor or to other modules in train sequences of up to three units. Modules are available in multiple configurations, including standard cargo, refrigerated, liquid container, passenger transport, and livestock variants. The Module's passive, interchangeable design is the foundation of the land train system's commercial versatility, multiplying hauling capacity without proportionally increasing operating costs beyond the investment in the tractor itself.","capabilities":"The Prairie Schooner Module uses a trailer chassis with off-road suspension and wide-profile tires that match the tractor's cross-country capability. The module has no independent propulsion, relying entirely on the tractor for movement. This simplicity makes it inexpensive to maintain and straightforward to repair in the field with basic tooling.\\n\\nEach standard module carries 22.5 tons of cargo in a main bay with two side access doors, one on the left and one on the right. This slightly larger capacity compared to the tractor's own bay, combined with the ability to chain three modules together, allows a fully loaded Prairie Schooner train to move well over 80 tons of goods in a single run. The standard configuration is a warm hold suited to general dry freight, though operators routinely specify refrigerated, liquid, passenger, or livestock configurations at the time of purchase.\\n\\nTwo and a half tons of light composite armor protect the module against the minor hazards of frontier operations. As with the tractor, the module carries no weapons from the factory. Customers on hostile worlds occasionally add turret blisters with light anti-infantry or area suppression weapons. The module's main operational limitation is its complete dependence on the tractor, as a disconnected module has no ability to reposition and becomes a fixed, vulnerable point.","deployment":"Prairie Schooner Modules operate wherever the tractor is found, predominantly on frontier and underdeveloped worlds throughout the Capellan March and beyond. Standard cargo modules are the most common configuration in day-to-day use. Refrigerated and liquid variants are especially prevalent on agricultural worlds where perishable goods supply chains are critical to local economies.\\n\\nPassenger modules serve colony transit and evacuation roles across isolated communities. In military logistics applications, modules carry ammunition, medical supplies, and general freight in rear-area support columns. On particularly hostile worlds, operators mount light weapons on module chassis for wildlife deterrence.","battleHistory":"The Prairie Schooner Module was developed simultaneously with the tractor as the essential companion component of Johnston Industries' land train concept. The design team prioritized interchangeability from the outset, ensuring each trailer would connect to any tractor and link in sequence with other modules without compatibility problems. This standardization was a deliberate commercial decision, intended to encourage operators to expand their fleets incrementally rather than commit to a single large purchase.\\n\\nProduction began at New Syrtis in 3058 alongside the tractor. The range of available configurations accelerated adoption across different markets. Refrigerated modules opened supply chain opportunities in the frontier food production sector, while passenger variants provided rural communities with a long-haul transport option that fixed-route vehicles could not deliver cost-effectively. Liquid cargo modules found ready buyers in fuel distribution and water transport roles on worlds with limited pipeline infrastructure.\\n\\nModule production followed the same disruption pattern as the tractor when the New Syrtis facility was damaged during the Jihad. Johnston's upgraded Benet III plant began producing modules alongside tractors during the recovery period. The module's simpler construction allowed Benet III to reach full module output relatively quickly, helping stabilize supply to operators during the years when the New Syrtis line was offline.\\n\\nThe variety of module types contributed to the Prairie Schooner system's long-term commercial durability. Some configurations were adapted for humanitarian logistics work during the Republic of the Sphere era, with large accessible cargo bays converted for medical supply distribution and emergency response roles. The module's unarmored cargo spaces could be reconfigured quickly with minimal tooling, a practical advantage in crisis situations where civilian equipment was pressed into aid distribution."}},"name":"Prairie Schooner Module","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":21.5,"doors":2}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":0,"armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":15,"right":15,"rear":12,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["tractor","trailer","off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"none"}},"49":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Various","primaryFactory":"Various","targeting":"Advanced Fire Control"},"components":{"engine":"Industrial Fusion","armor":"Commercial (BAR 7)"},"text":{"overview":"The Prime Mover is not a single designed vehicle but a broad classification of large wheeled logistics platforms that have supported military operations across the Inner Sphere for centuries. First recorded in Inner Sphere inventories around 2420, these six-wheeled fusion-powered trucks share common features, including a cabover cab, an integrated flatbed, and a rear cargo bay, rather than a shared blueprint. Multiple manufacturers across every major state and many Periphery worlds independently developed compatible designs, resulting in a vehicle class united by operational requirements rather than common production. The Prime Mover's ability to haul up to twenty tons of cargo over cross-country terrain without refueling stops made it indispensable to military logistics from the Age of War onward. Though designed as a civilian hauler, modest defensive armament and sturdy construction drew military operators to the platform, and prime movers have served line armies, garrison forces, and irregular units with equal reliability across every era of Inner Sphere history.","capabilities":"The standard Prime Mover is built around a cabover-style crew cab with two small lasers mounted on either side, connected to a large integrated flatbed rated for twenty tons of cargo. A rear-mounted SRM-4 provides additional defensive firepower, operated by the vehicle's cargo handler, who serves as the third crew member alongside the driver and a cab gunner. Advanced Fire Control improves weapons accuracy beyond what is typical for a logistics vehicle, giving the Prime Mover genuine defensive capability against light infantry and vehicles.\\n\\nThe fusion power plant eliminates range limitations that would otherwise constrain logistics operations, allowing prime movers to sustain forward supply chains without fuel depots. Six tons of commercial armor, rated at BAR 7, provides limited protection against small arms and light fragmentary threats, but the design was never intended to absorb sustained weapons fire. The off-road chassis and six-wheel drive allow it to follow combat forces through broken terrain that would stop conventional trucks, though ground speed is moderate. The design's three-man crew allows simultaneous cargo operations and weapons management, but the requirement to crew both cab mounts and the rear launcher demands coordination under fire. The Prime Mover's main weakness is its bulk, which reduces maneuverability and makes it a large target for heavier weapons.","deployment":"The Succession Wars variant adapted the Prime Mover for the harsher realities of that era, replacing the small lasers with machine guns and upgrading the rear launcher from an SRM-4 to a six-tube launcher. Units operating in contested territories preferred this configuration for its improved anti-infantry capability and greater weight of fire, accepting the increased ammunition burden in exchange for a more capable defensive platform. Machine gun ammunition is easier to stockpile and maintain than laser power cells in degraded industrial conditions, making this variant particularly practical for second-line and garrison units. The configuration saw widespread adoption during the Succession Wars and remained common well afterward in militia and irregular inventories.","battleHistory":"Fusion-powered heavy trucks capable of sustained cross-country operations emerged from multiple manufacturing centers during the early Age of War, driven by the need to move hazardous or heavy cargo independently of fuel supply chains. By approximately 2420, the basic configuration of a six-wheeled fusion-powered hauler with an integrated flatbed had coalesced into a broadly recognized vehicle class. No single manufacturer controlled the design, and variants proliferated across every Inner Sphere state and most Periphery territories, sharing operational concepts rather than interchangeable parts.\\n\\nDuring the Star League era, prime movers became standard equipment in SLDF logistics trains and planetary garrison inventories. Their operational independence made them essential to sustained campaigning in contested space, where fuel supply could not be guaranteed. The Succession Wars placed severe pressure on fusion-powered vehicles of all kinds, and prime movers were no exception. As fusion engines became scarce, many were stripped from logistics vehicles to keep BattleMechs operational. Units that survived the early wars were often converted to ICE power at considerable cost in range and flexibility, though the vehicles remained in service across all factions in degraded form.\\n\\nThe recovery of lostech during the late Succession Wars and the gradual return of fusion engine availability revitalized prime mover fleets across the Inner Sphere. By the Clan Invasion era, the original fusion-powered configuration had largely been restored in front-line units, while ICE variants remained common in second-line and militia service. Through the Jihad and into the Republic era, roving scavengers and irregular forces came to prize captured prime movers for their operational independence, fueling a secondary market in surplus vehicles and spare parts. The so-called RAMTech job on New Kyoto in 3115 drew public attention when thieves used two dozen prime movers as decoys during a high-profile operation, demonstrating how unremarkable and ubiquitous the vehicle had become. The incident served as a reminder to military planners that a design this common cuts both ways, offering logistics resilience while also providing cover for irregular activities."}},"name":"Prime Mover","model":"(Succession Wars)","tonnage":60,"motiveType":"wheeled","era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":25,"right":25,"rear":20,"turret":24,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~57","$v":[["cargo-container","#0",null,null],["cargo-container","#1",null,null],["machine-gun","#2","front",null],["machine-gun","#3","front",null],["srm-6","#4","turret",null],["ammo-srm-standard","#5",null,"srm-6"],"~59"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["off-road"],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"50":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Lockheed/CBM Corporation","communications":"N/A N/A","targeting":"N/A N/A"},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Quicksilver Personal Sports Craft is Lockheed/CBM Corporation's purpose-built Wing-in-Ground-Effect racing vehicle, introduced around 3058 in direct competition with Skye Pleasure Craft's Coanda on the Inner Sphere racing circuit. Designed from the outset to outperform its rival on the Skye Water Racing Championship circuit, the Quicksilver represented Lockheed/CBM's bid for the commercial prestige and sponsorship revenue that championship success carried. Larger and faster than the Coanda, it takes an aggressive design approach that prioritizes peak speed over endurance, making it a high-performance craft that demands precise handling and race team coordination to convert its mechanical advantage into victories.","capabilities":"The Quicksilver masses 4,500 kilograms, noticeably heavier than the Coanda, and is powered by an internal combustion engine tuned for higher top-end performance. Its flank speed exceeds the Coanda's, providing the raw pace advantage Lockheed/CBM intended. The defining trade-off is fuel capacity, which is insufficient to complete the roughly two-thousand-kilometer championship course without at least one refueling stop. This creates a strategic vulnerability where pit crew performance is as decisive as the craft's mechanical advantage. The Quicksilver carries BAR 4-rated armor in slightly less quantity than the Coanda. An amphibious chassis modification is standard, permitting operation across water and marginal coastal terrain. No weapons are fitted, and the craft accommodates a single crew member with no passenger provision.","deployment":"The Quicksilver is manufactured by Lockheed/CBM Corporation at a facility whose planetary location is not publicly confirmed, and is marketed to competitive racing teams and private buyers across the Inner Sphere. Lockheed/CBM's promotional partnership with former holovid personality Ricardo Hunt extended the design's commercial visibility well beyond the Skye championship circuit itself. Like the Coanda, the Quicksilver has no meaningful military application and operates exclusively in the civilian recreational and competition market. Its higher price point relative to the Coanda reflects the greater mass of the design and Lockheed/CBM's positioning of the Quicksilver as a premium performance offering.","battleHistory":"Lockheed/CBM Corporation announced development of the Quicksilver following the Coanda's debut success on the Skye championship circuit. Recognizing the commercial and publicity value the championship had attracted, Lockheed/CBM committed to a clean-sheet WiGE racing design with a clear objective: outrun the Coanda. The Quicksilver entered competition around 3058 and made an immediate impression. Carrying insufficient fuel for the full course, the design's first championship appearance saw its pilot build a commanding lead over the field, only to lose the decisive advantage during mandatory refueling stops. The near-victory demonstrated the Quicksilver's raw speed while exposing the pit operations vulnerability that would need to be addressed before the craft could claim the championship it was designed to win.\\n\\nThe 3058 result galvanized Lockheed/CBM's racing operation. The corporation's pit crew trained relentlessly through the four-year interval between championships, reducing the refueling process to a choreographed sequence of practiced movements. Their preparation paid off at the 3062 championship, when Judith Church piloted the Quicksilver to victory, ending the Coanda's run of championship titles and validating Lockheed/CBM's investment in the program.\\n\\nLockheed/CBM expanded the Quicksilver's commercial profile through an endorsement arrangement with Ricardo Hunt, a well-known figure from the popular Immortal Warrior holovid series who had taken to WiGE racing. Hunt's involvement provided Lockheed/CBM with both a credible racing ambassador and a media personality capable of extending the Quicksilver's name recognition beyond the core racing audience. The combination of championship results and celebrity promotion positioned the Quicksilver as the premier WiGE racing platform available for private purchase during the 3060s, even as Skye Pleasure Craft prepared its Coanda II response."}},"name":"Quicksilver Personal Sports Craft","tonnage":4.5,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~34","era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":12,"armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":"~82","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.36,"chassisModifications":["amphibious"],"bar":4,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"51":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Various","primaryFactory":"Various"},"text":{"overview":"The Randolph Support Vehicle is a primitive tracked utility design dating to circa 2300, one of countless logistical workhorses that sustained armies throughout the Age of War and Reunification War. Never an elite or specialized machine, its value lay in dependability: it kept combat forces supplied, maintained, and operational across a wide range of terrain and conditions. Manufactured by numerous concerns across the Inner Sphere, the Randolph was purchased in large quantities by national militaries, paramilitary organizations, and private military contractors alike. Its combination of modest armament, adequate cargo capacity, and rugged tracked chassis made it suitable for rear-echelon operations in the dangerous near-combat zone that support troops routinely occupied. Though declared obsolete by 2500, examples continued to serve in low-intensity rear-area roles for decades after that date.","capabilities":"The Randolph is a 35-ton vehicle powered by an ICE engine, providing a cruise speed of 40 km/h and a flank speed of 60 km/h. Its nine-ton enclosed cargo bay and rear-loading door are its primary assets. A tractor chassis modification allows the vehicle to tow trailers, sleds, or disabled vehicles, giving it effective logistics capacity well beyond its internal storage. The machine gun mounts use a universal design, allowing different light weapons to be fitted depending on the mission.\\n\\nFor self-defense, three machine guns cover the front, right flank, and left flank arcs. A Basic Fire Control system provides rudimentary targeting assistance. The vehicle's primitive-grade armor is well distributed across all facings, offering meaningful protection against small arms and fragmentation hazards common in rear-echelon environments.\\n\\nThe Randolph is straightforward to maintain, relying on widely available ICE components and a simple mechanical layout. The Modular Weapons configuration further simplifies field-level modification. Its ICE power plant generates no heat accumulation, appropriate given its non-energy weapon armament. The principal weaknesses are limited top speed, no electronic warfare capability, and vulnerability to any dedicated combat unit, which can bring firepower far exceeding what primitive armor can reliably absorb.","deployment":"The Randolph was deployed across the full spectrum of military logistics roles during the Age of War and Reunification War. It served with national armies of all major Inner Sphere powers, mercenary commands, and planetary defense forces. Typically assigned to supply battalions and logistics companies, it operated in cargo convoys, forward base construction teams, and mobile maintenance elements. Its towing capability made it particularly useful on long supply routes where cargo volume exceeded internal bay capacity. Field modifications as ambulances and command posts were documented across multiple theaters. By the mid-twenty-fifth century, the design was most commonly found in militia and reserve units on worlds that could not source more modern equipment.","battleHistory":"The Randolph emerged in the early twenty-fourth century as military planners across the Inner Sphere confronted a persistent operational problem: the support personnel and vehicles sustaining front-line combat units outnumbered those combat units by five-to-one or more, yet operated with little protection in environments only marginally safer than the front lines themselves. Vehicles like the Randolph were a direct response to that reality, offering armored enclosures and limited firepower to logistics crews who had no other means of self-defense.\\n\\nThe design saw wide adoption across the Inner Sphere during the Age of War. Its distributed manufacturing model, with regional armories and civilian producers building the type to meet local demand, meant that Randolphs appeared wherever armies operated. No single producer dominated the market. The design served throughout the Reunification War in the same roles that defined it, hauling artillery pieces, mobile repair equipment, and critical supplies across contested terrain. Some units were converted to improvised ambulances or field command posts, adapting the enclosed cargo bay to communications equipment.\\n\\nBy 2500, advancing technology made the Randolph's primitive construction economically unviable to upgrade. The Star League Defense Force required more capable logistics platforms, and production at most facilities wound down in favor of more modern designs. Surviving vehicles continued to serve in rear-area roles for organizations that could not afford newer equipment, particularly on colony worlds and in small mercenary units. A handful persisted into the early Succession Wars era on backwater systems, valued more for their simplicity and ease of maintenance than for any tactical capability, before finally passing out of service entirely."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter","equipmentUuid"],"$v":[["quirk-easy-to-maintain",null,null],["quirk-obsolete",2500,null],["quirk-modular-weapons",null,"#0"],["quirk-modular-weapons",null,"#1"],["quirk-modular-weapons",null,"#2"]]},"name":"Randolph Support Vehicle","tonnage":35,"hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":9,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":16,"left":16,"right":16,"rear":14,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~21","$v":["~104",["machine-gun","right","#1",null,null],["machine-gun","left","#2",null,null],"~105"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":["tractor"],"bar":5,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"52":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"Fuel Cell (Electric)","jumpJets":"N/A N/A"},"text":{"overview":"The Raptor RRV is a 15-ton tracked support vehicle developed for the Free Worlds League as a modern, cost-effective alternative for infantry transport and light patrol duties. Introduced in 3059, the vehicle was built around civilian-grade components to minimize procurement and maintenance costs while meeting the operational needs of League auxiliary forces. Lighter and faster than comparable designs fielded by other states, the Raptor has earned a reputation for dependability and useful operational range, making it a practical choice for the planetary militia and garrison formations that form the backbone of the League's distributed defense posture.","capabilities":"The Raptor RRV is built around a fuel cell electric engine that propels the vehicle to a top speed of 64 kilometers per hour with an operational range of 833 kilometers, surpassing most comparable vehicles in both speed and endurance. Its civilian-grade components keep manufacturing costs accessible and support widespread maintenance in the field without specialized tooling or rare materials. The vehicle carries 1.5 tons of BAR 8-rated armor, providing reasonable protection against small arms and light weapons, though less total coverage than the heavier Cellco Ranger it conceptually succeeds.\\n\\nThe primary weapon is a turret-mounted extended-range small laser fitted with a basic fire control system, providing accurate defensive fire at ranges exceeding typical machine gun capability. The energy weapon eliminates ammunition supply concerns for units operating on extended patrol, a useful logistical advantage in the field. The Raptor's principal operational value lies in its 3-ton infantry compartment, carrying a full squad with personal gear and heavy weapons, with personnel exiting through a single rear door. This combination of speed, range, and troop capacity makes the vehicle an effective auxiliary for infantry formations across a wide range of terrain and operational environments.","deployment":"The Raptor RRV serves Free Worlds League auxiliary and militia formations in convoy escort, infantry transport, and light patrol roles. Its fuel cell engine and extended range make it particularly suited to operations away from established supply lines. The 3-ton troop bay carries a full squad with heavy weapons and field gear, allowing the vehicle to serve as a self-contained light infantry platform in situations where dedicated combat vehicles are unavailable. The turret-mounted extended-range laser provides limited but accurate defensive fire without ammunition supply concerns, a practical advantage for units at the end of long logistical chains.","battleHistory":"The Raptor RRV emerged from Free Worlds League recognition that the Cellco Ranger and similar urban law enforcement vehicles used by neighboring states offered a practical model for affordable infantry transport. League military planners wanted a vehicle with better mobility and range than the Ranger while retaining its accessible cost and ease of maintenance. Introduced in 3059, the Raptor was deliberately engineered with off-the-shelf civilian components to hold down procurement costs and simplify supply chains, reflecting the League's characteristic preference for practical solutions that could be sustained across its decentralized military structure.\\n\\nThe fuel cell propulsion system represented a deliberate choice, offering operational advantages in environments where petroleum fuel supply might be constrained and reducing the vehicle's logistical footprint in extended operations. The adoption of an energy weapon over a machine gun similarly reduced ammunition burden. These choices made the Raptor well-suited to auxiliary formations operating at extended distances from primary supply depots, a common situation for League forces spread across numerous worlds.\\n\\nThough never a prestigious front-line platform, the Raptor built a quiet reputation for dependability among the infantry formations that relied on it. It forms part of a broader family of conceptually related tracked infantry carriers developed independently across the Inner Sphere, alongside the Cellco Ranger of the Lyran Commonwealth and Federated Suns and the ancient Chi-Ha ICV of the Draconis Combine. These vehicles share no common lineage or manufacturer, but reflect the universal military requirement for affordable, dependable infantry transport that no state in the Inner Sphere could afford to ignore."}},"name":"Raptor RRV","tonnage":15,"troopSpace":3,"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":10,"left":6,"right":6,"rear":5,"turret":6,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":[{"tag":"er-small-laser","location":"turret","uuid":"#0"}],"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":8,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"basic"}},"53":{"tro":{"production":"~12","components":{"engine":"ICE (rating unknown)","armor":"BAR 6 (3.5 tons)"},"text":{"overview":"The Reaper is a fifty-five-ton primitive self-propelled artillery vehicle introduced by the Terran Hegemony in 2321. Developed as the lighter counterpart to the heavier Apostle, the Reaper was designed to provide indirect fire support at the regimental and brigade level, filling a critical gap in the Hegemony Armed Forces' artillery capabilities. More mobile and numerous than its larger sibling, the Reaper traded raw firepower for flexibility and wider tactical deployment across the HAF's organizational structure. As one of the first generation of purpose-built Hegemony artillery platforms, the Reaper helped establish the doctrinal foundations for mobile fire support at the formation level.","capabilities":"The Reaper centers its firepower on a single Thumper artillery piece, complemented by three tons of ammunition providing sixty rounds of fire. An advanced fire control system with anti-aircraft targeting capability guides the weapon, giving the Reaper a secondary role as an impromptu air defense battery when circumstances demand. A front-mounted small laser serves as the vehicle's sole direct-fire defensive weapon.\\n\\nThe Reaper's internal combustion engine propels it at a cruise speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour with a flank speed of fifty-four, matching the Apostle in ground mobility. However, its single ton of fuel limits operational range to approximately 526 kilometers, considerably less than its heavier companion. Protection consists of 3.5 tons of BAR 6 armor, providing minimal coverage against anything beyond small arms and shrapnel. The vehicle suffers from poor environmental sealing, leaving the seven-member crew vulnerable to chemical, biological, or hazardous atmosphere conditions. Despite these limitations, the Reaper's lighter weight made it more practical for wider distribution across the HAF's regimental structure than the larger and more resource-intensive Apostle.","deployment":"The Reaper Self-Propelled Artillery served as the standard light artillery platform for Hegemony Armed Forces regiment and brigade-level formations. Its lighter weight and simpler logistics allowed wider distribution than its heavier companion, providing responsive indirect fire support closer to the front lines. The advanced fire control system's anti-aircraft targeting capability gave Reaper crews an additional air defense role, making the vehicle a versatile addition to formation-level artillery battalions.","battleHistory":"The Reaper originated alongside the Apostle as part of Field Marshal Mears Coblitz's comprehensive modernization of the Hegemony Armed Forces' artillery arm during the early years of the Terran Hegemony. While the Apostle addressed the need for heavy fire support at the division level, the Reaper filled the lighter requirement for regimental and brigade artillery battalions. Both vehicles moved from requirements to production remarkably fast, entering service in less than two years.\\n\\nThe Reaper's first operational deployment came during the later stages of the Campaign of Persuasion, where the HAF's newly modernized artillery corps proved its worth in supporting ground actions across multiple worlds. The lighter vehicle was particularly valued for its ability to be distributed more widely across the HAF's expanding force structure, providing organic fire support to formations that had previously relied on outdated Alliance-era equipment.\\n\\nOver the following decades, the Reaper and Apostle together displaced virtually all of the static, towed, and self-propelled artillery that the Hegemony had inherited from the Terran Alliance. Both platforms served for more than a century before the rapid pace of technological advancement overtook them. The Reaper was declared obsolete in 2480, slightly earlier than the Apostle, as newer designs employing what would become standard technology in later eras entered production. Few Reapers survived beyond their period of active service. By the thirty-first century, the remaining examples had been relegated to purely ceremonial roles, with a handful of relics enduring into the Dark Age as static displays on Terra and New Earth."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-poor-sealing",null],["quirk-obsolete",2480],["quirk-anti-aircraft-targeting",null]]},"name":"Reaper Self-Propelled Artillery","tonnage":55,"hasTurret":false,"armorAllocation":{"front":18,"left":18,"right":18,"rear":18,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~14",["thumper-artillery","front","#1",null],["ammo-thumper-standard",null,"#2","thumper-artillery"],["ammo-thumper-standard",null,"#3","thumper-artillery"],["ammo-thumper-standard",null,"#4","thumper-artillery"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"advanced"}},"54":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Defiance Industries"},"text":{"overview":"The Routemaster Hover Shuttle is a small civilian hover transport produced by Defiance Industries' civilian manufacturing division, introduced in 2770. Designed to provide affordable, flexible public transit in urban environments, the Routemaster operates on dedicated roadways and public thoroughfares without requiring heavy fixed infrastructure. Its hover technology allows it to cross a wide variety of terrain, including waterways, giving it flexibility that traditional wheeled or rail-based vehicles cannot match. Cheaper to build and maintain than equivalent rail systems, the Routemaster became an attractive transit solution for municipalities across the Inner Sphere. Its distinctive red livery became especially iconic in Tharkad City, where the vehicles grew into a recognized and beloved feature of the capital's urban identity across generations of continuous service.","capabilities":"The Routemaster is built on a small hover chassis, weighing only 3,800 kilograms. Its ICE power plant propels the vehicle to flank speeds exceeding 150 km/h, allowing it to keep pace with urban expressway traffic and respond efficiently across large service areas. The hover system eliminates the need for dedicated roadbed infrastructure, as the vehicle can operate on paved surfaces, unpaved terrain, and even open water, making it adaptable to a wide range of environments.\\n\\nThe Routemaster carries thirteen passengers alongside a single driver, with a modest 85-kilogram cargo capacity for luggage. Commercial BAR 3 armor covers the hull, appropriate for road debris and minor impacts but not meaningful against military weapons. The vehicle carries no armament. Its operating and maintenance costs are substantially lower than rail alternatives, which makes it competitive for budget-conscious transit operators. The primary limitation is its small passenger capacity, requiring high service frequency to achieve the throughput of larger transit systems.","deployment":"The Routemaster Hover Shuttle serves as a public transit vehicle in urban and suburban environments across the Inner Sphere. Individual units operate in large municipal fleets on fixed routes, with high service frequency compensating for the vehicle's modest per-unit capacity. The hover system allows deployment in communities with limited paved infrastructure, broadening its market beyond developed urban cores. In emergency situations the Routemaster has been pressed into civilian evacuation roles and used to shuttle personnel during urban military operations when dedicated transport assets were unavailable. Its low cost and wide distribution make it a practical fallback option whenever rapid civilian transport is needed on short notice.","battleHistory":"The Routemaster Hover Shuttle entered production in 2770, commissioned through Defiance Industries' civilian division as part of a broader push to develop affordable urban mobility solutions during the late Star League era. Rapid urban growth across the Inner Sphere had strained traditional transit networks, and the hover shuttle concept offered a low-infrastructure alternative that could be deployed quickly across a variety of metropolitan environments.\\n\\nThe vehicle spread widely through commercial export and the strength of its manufacturer's reputation. In Tharkad City, the Routemaster became a celebrated fixture of street life, its red fleet a visual signature of the capital. That attachment proved enduring. During Archon Katherine Steiner-Davion's tenure in the 3050s and 3060s, her administration moved to repaint the Tharkad City fleet in Steiner blue. The proposal was met with a significant public groundswell of opposition from city residents, and the Archon eventually abandoned the plans.\\n\\nThe Succession Wars disrupted civilian production for many vehicle types, but the Routemaster's simple ICE powertrain and near-universal maintenance familiarity helped it outlast more technologically complex competitors. Basic mechanical skills were sufficient for field maintenance, and parts were widely available throughout the Inner Sphere. The design remained in production through the Succession Wars and continued to serve municipal fleets well into the Dark Age, proof of the fundamental soundness of the original concept."}},"name":"Routemaster Hover Shuttle","tonnage":3.8,"motiveType":"hover","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.085,"doors":1}],"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":9,"armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":["~5","~6","~7","~8","~9","~10","~11","~83","~91","~92","~93","~94","~95","~96"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.08,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":3,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"55":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Veridian Dynamics","primaryFactory":"Ozawa"},"text":{"overview":"The RR-3 Recovery Vehicle was developed by Veridian Dynamics on Ozawa as a commercial utility platform designed from the outset for battlefield recovery operations. Where the Terran Hegemony had invested in a purpose-built heavy design tied directly to the Mackie program, Veridian Dynamics pursued a lighter, more economical alternative suited to recovering disabled BattleMechs up to the heavy class. The RR-3 was the culmination of several earlier Veridian recovery vehicles, distilling the practical lessons of those designs into a single capable platform. Its wheeled configuration and internal combustion powerplant kept production costs accessible, allowing wide distribution to formations that could not obtain Hegemony-restricted assets. Though a primitive design by later standards, the RR-3 established many of the core operating principles that would guide battlefield recovery vehicle development for generations to come.","capabilities":"The RR-3 is a fifty-five-ton wheeled medium vehicle powered by an internal combustion engine delivering a cruising speed of 30 km/h and a flank speed of 50 km/h. Off-Road and Tractor Chassis Modifications allow it to operate across broken terrain and to tow disabled vehicles through conditions that would defeat a simpler platform, making it viable in the forward combat zones where recovery work most often occurs. Two lift hoists mounted at the rear provide the vehicle's primary function, each rated for six-ton loads.\\n\\nThe cargo deck holds twenty tons of standard freight and can be configured to carry a secured BattleMech hull. The RR-3 carries no weapons and relies entirely on any escorting security element for protection in contested areas. Four tons of BAR 6-rated hull armor deflect light shrapnel and small arms fire but offer little resistance to dedicated anti-vehicle weapons. The Gas Hog quirk means fuel consumption is higher than the vehicle's modest speed would suggest, and the Fragile Fuel Tank requires careful attention in any combat-adjacent environment. Offsetting these drawbacks, the Easy to Maintain rating allows field technicians to service the vehicle with basic tools and common spare parts, a significant advantage for a platform expected to operate far from major depots.","deployment":"The RR-3 was distributed to unit-level logistics and support formations throughout the Terran Hegemony and early Great House militaries during the Age of War and the early Star League period. Its off-road capability and towing capacity suited it to a wide variety of terrain types, and the ICE powerplant allowed operation where sophisticated fuel infrastructure was unavailable. The design's weight class was compatible with standard DropShip loadouts common to combined-arms regiments, easing routine deployment alongside the BattleMechs it supported. Second-line and garrison formations continued operating the type past its official obsolescence date in 2550, often partnering with newer recovery vehicles when the older design's cargo capacity remained useful.","battleHistory":"The first recovery vehicles to appear on BattleMech-era battlefields were improvised affairs, standard construction cranes and heavy-transport winches adapted by logistics crews struggling to keep pace with the massive new combat machines transforming warfare across the Inner Sphere. Veridian Dynamics recognized the commercial opportunity early, developing a series of progressively more capable dedicated recovery platforms through the 2430s. Each iteration addressed shortcomings of the last, improving off-road performance, load capacity, and crew ergonomics as the operational picture became clearer.\\n\\nThe RR-3 entered production at the Veridian Dynamics facility on Ozawa in 2439, alongside the Hegemony's UTR-588, as a lighter and more economical alternative suited to unit-level recovery rather than depot-scale salvage operations. Its wheeled platform and ICE powerplant made it affordable and maintainable for formations throughout the Terran Hegemony and the surrounding realms. The design filled a clear gap, providing formations that lacked access to the Hegemony's classified heavy assets with a capable tool for keeping their BattleMechs in the fight.\\n\\nThe RR-3 served widely through the Age of War and into the early Star League era, becoming a familiar piece of equipment in the logistics trains of combined-arms regiments across the Hegemony and the Great Houses alike. Its ease of maintenance and low operating cost kept it popular with units operating far from major supply depots. The design's limitations became unavoidable as BattleMechs grew heavier and more complex, and by 2550 the RR-3 was officially declared obsolete as newer purpose-built recovery vehicles with greater lifting capacity entered service. Production wound down gradually as replacement designs accumulated in the supply chain, and surviving hulls were reassigned to second-line logistics roles before final retirement."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":["~33",["quirk-fragile-fuel-tank",null],["quirk-obsolete",2550],["quirk-gas-hog",null]]},"name":"RR-3 Recovery Vehicle","tonnage":55,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":20,"doors":1}],"armorAllocation":{"front":30,"left":30,"right":30,"rear":15,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~107","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1.5,"chassisModifications":["tractor","off-road"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"56":{"tro":{"production":"~89","text":{"overview":"The Ryu Heavy Transport is an armed variant of the Koi, developed by Wakazashi Enterprises in response to demand from planetary militia commands seeking a heavy WiGE with meaningful self-defense capability. Introduced in 3064, the Ryu retains the Koi's amphibious chassis and fusion powerplant while adding a weapons turret and lateral machine gun mounts to protect the vehicle during the vulnerable loading and unloading phases of transport operations.\\n\\nThe Ryu fills a recognized gap between unarmed civilian transports and purpose-built assault WiGEs, serving forces that need both significant payload and the ability to engage light ground threats without relying entirely on escorts. Cargo capacity is reduced to one hundred eight tons to compensate for the added crew stations, armament, and ammunition storage.","capabilities":"The Ryu mounts an AC/5 in a dorsal turret as its primary weapon, supported by four machine guns covering the forward arc and both flanks. This battery is not designed for sustained combat against armored opponents, but provides deterrent firepower sufficient to suppress light infantry and engage lightly armored vehicles at close range. A basic fire control system supports the gunners against stationary or slow-moving targets.\\n\\nThe amphibious chassis modification from the Koi carries forward intact, allowing bay doors to open while afloat without flooding. Cargo capacity is reduced but the Ryu remains one of the highest-payload armed WiGEs available to planetary militia forces. Its combination of lift capacity and defensive armament is its primary selling point.\\n\\nThe Ryu's limitations mirror those of the Koi, with additional concerns. The armament is inadequate against aerospace fighters or well-armed aircraft, the vehicle's profile makes it a priority target, and the added crew requirement stretches manpower budgets. The machine guns provide only marginal close-in defense against infantry boarding actions. Commanders relying on Ryu transports must still plan for dedicated air cover during transit and landing operations, or accept the risk that comes with a slow, visible target.","deployment":"The Ryu Heavy Transport serves primarily with Draconis Combine planetary militia and garrison forces on worlds where DropShip transport is unavailable or prohibitively expensive. Its combination of cargo capacity and light armament suits it to moving troops and equipment through territory where hostile light forces may be present. Export units serve militia and paramilitary forces across the Inner Sphere in similar roles. The Ryu is frequently deployed alongside Koi transports, providing escort coverage and suppressive fire for unarmed cargo lifts.","battleHistory":"The Ryu emerged directly from Wakazashi's experience selling the Koi to Draconis Combine militia commands. Purchasing officers consistently raised concerns about deploying an unarmed vehicle in contested airspace or near active combat zones, and Wakazashi's engineers identified a straightforward solution. Adding a dorsal turret and lateral machine gun mounts while preserving the core hull and cargo architecture produced a militarized variant at modest additional cost. The result entered service in 3064.\\n\\nThe timing aligned with growing instability across the Inner Sphere in the years before the FedCom Civil War. Garrison commands looking for transport assets that could defend themselves in low-intensity conflict environments found the Ryu attractive, and Combine militia formations adopted it in quantity for service on worlds where hostile activity was chronic but DropShip support remained unavailable or impractical.\\n\\nThe Ryu has never distinguished itself in pitched combat, and was never intended to. Its operational record consists largely of convoy escort missions, coastal patrol duties, and garrison support work where its combination of payload and light armament proved sufficient. Instances of Ryu units pressed into direct fire support roles have ended poorly, as the vehicle's size and limited maneuverability make it an easy mark for any unit carrying anti-armor weapons. Despite these limitations, the Ryu has earned a reputation for reliability in the unglamorous work of moving troops and supplies under intermittent threat, and it continues in production alongside the Koi at the Dover facility."}},"name":"Ryu Heavy Transport","tonnage":240,"motiveType":"wige","bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":108,"doors":2}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":{"front":17,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":11,"turret":17,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":15,"frontRight":15,"rearLeft":15,"rearRight":15,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":["~26",["machine-gun","front","#1",null],["machine-gun","frontRight","#2",null],["machine-gun","frontLeft","#3",null],["autocannon-standard-b","turret","#4",null],["~28",null,"#5","machine-gun"],["ammo-ac-5-standard",null,"#6","autocannon-standard-b"],["ammo-ac-5-standard",null,"#7","autocannon-standard-b"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["amphibious"],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"57":{"tro":{"production":"~100","components":"~101","text":{"overview":"The Sasayaku Control Transport is a specialized surveillance platform produced by Pesht Motors for the Draconis Combine's police forces. Introduced during the Clan Invasion era as an evolution of the ubiquitous Obuzaabaa Tactical Vehicle, the Sasayaku serves as a mobile drone control station, allowing law enforcement to quietly observe criminal elements throughout the Combine. Externally identical to the more common Obuzaabaa, the Sasayaku's true nature is invisible to casual observers, giving Combine police a significant covert intelligence advantage in populated areas.","capabilities":"Built on the same civilian chassis as the Obuzaabaa, the Sasayaku replaces much of its parent design's general-purpose capability with dedicated surveillance systems. Half of the standard communications consoles are removed in favor of more sophisticated surveillance and control equipment. The rear cargo area is converted into a launch bay for Gossamer surveillance drones, operated through a dedicated drone control system. Two tons of commercial armor provide basic protection against hostile fire from criminal elements, though the vehicle is not intended for direct military engagement.\\n\\nThe trade-off for this surveillance capability is a significant reduction in personnel capacity. The Sasayaku retains only half the infantry space of a standard Obuzaabaa, limiting its ability to transport officers. This limitation requires each Sasayaku to be paired with a nearby Obuzaabaa for personnel support when direct intervention is needed.","deployment":"The Sasayaku operates exclusively within Draconis Combine police formations. Each vehicle operates on constant rotation schedules within major population centers, maintaining persistent surveillance of areas of interest. Because of its reduced personnel capacity, each Sasayaku is paired with a nearby Obuzaabaa that can provide rapid response when surveillance identifies a situation requiring direct intervention.","battleHistory":"Pesht Motors introduced the Sasayaku in 3055 as a technological upgrade to the Combine's police surveillance capabilities. The timing coincided with broader modernization efforts following the Clan Invasion, as the Draconis Combine invested in improved internal security infrastructure. Widespread deployment followed rapidly, with the design becoming a standard element of police operations across the Combine's major population centers.\\n\\nLike the Obuzaabaa, Sasayaku production was disrupted when Word of Blake nuclear strikes destroyed the Pesht Motors facility on Pesht in 3071. The vehicle's critical role in domestic security ensured that surviving units remained in active service throughout the Jihad and into the Republic era."}},"name":"Sasayaku Control Transport","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":2,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":5,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":5,"armorAllocation":"~102","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":[["~76","#0"],["drone-carrier-control-system","#1"]]},"supportVehicle":"~103"},"58":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Earthwerks-FWL, Inc."},"components":{"chassis":"Earthwerks-FWL, Inc.","armor":"Commercial Armor BAR 4"},"text":{"overview":"The Saturn Harvester is a super-heavy agricultural support vehicle designed to deliver large-scale automated food production to planets with small populations. Introduced in 2652 by Earthwerks-FWL, Incorporated, the vehicle was built to address a fundamental challenge of the Star League's colonization programs: cultivating enough food for a growing settlement with the limited workforce available in a colony's earliest years.\\n\\nBy combining massive harvesting capacity with an integrated onboard food processing capability, the Saturn allowed a crew of three to match the output of a much larger conventional farming operation. Its longevity across subsequent centuries reflected how thoroughly it met that original mandate, remaining in active service on worlds it had helped establish long after the political context of its creation had changed.","capabilities":"At 175 tons, the Saturn Harvester is a large tracked support vehicle powered by a fusion engine providing unlimited operational range. Cruise speed of 20 km/h and top speed of 30 km/h reflect the machine's priorities: sustained output over long operating cycles rather than rapid repositioning. An amphibious chassis modification allows the vehicle to cross water obstacles without halting operations, an important capability on developing worlds where bridge and road infrastructure may be years away.\\n\\nHarvesting functions are split between a front-mounted combine for crop collection and a rear lift hoist for handling large agricultural loads and positioning equipment. An integrated automated food processing plant converts raw harvest into ready-to-eat or shelf-stable meals without requiring separate ground-based facilities. This all-in-one capability is the Saturn's defining operational advantage on colonies too new to have developed fixed infrastructure.\\n\\nCargo capacity totals 21.5 tons of standard storage alongside 12 tons of refrigerated cargo accessible through a rear loading door. Commercial armor at BAR 4 offers environmental protection suited to civilian operations, with no weapons carriage. A crew of three enlisted operators manages the vehicle's numerous systems.\\n\\nThe Saturn's practical limitation centers on its deliberate pace. In the field, its slow ground speed requires careful route planning and operational scheduling to maximize productivity across large agricultural territories. The vehicle's size also demands adequate transport infrastructure for interstellar deployment, limiting its use to well-established supply chains.","deployment":"The Saturn Harvester is deployed wherever large-scale automated agricultural production is required with minimal crew. It is most commonly found on frontier colony worlds and established agricultural planets with limited labor resources, operating in convoys or as standalone units covering territory too extensive for smaller vehicles. Its amphibious capability allows it to work across mixed terrain on developing worlds without the need for engineered crossing points. The vehicle's onboard processing capability makes it self-sufficient at the point of harvest, reducing the logistical chain required to feed colonial populations in early settlement phases.","battleHistory":"Earthwerks-FWL developed the Saturn Harvester in 2652 to support the colonization expansion that defined the mid-Star League period. New colonies typically lacked the population to staff conventional farming operations at sufficient scale, creating a persistent gap between the food supply a settlement needed and what its small labor force could produce. The Saturn addressed that gap directly, substituting automation for manpower at a scale previously unavailable in civilian agricultural equipment.\\n\\nThe vehicle first saw deployment on frontier worlds throughout the Inner Sphere, shipped alongside terraforming equipment and early colonial infrastructure packages. In operation, the Saturn often worked in convoy with smaller utility vehicles, the big machine handling primary harvesting while lighter vehicles managed staging and transport of processed output. This coordinated approach became a standard pattern in Earthwerks promotional materials, which boasted that their system could convert raw field product into packaged meals within twenty minutes of cutting.\\n\\nThe Saturn's survival through the Succession Wars owed principally to its civilian nature and universal utility. It represented no military threat and conferred no strategic advantage worth denying, passing through multiple political ownerships as interstellar borders shifted around it. On many worlds, individual vehicles outlasted the political entities that had originally fielded them, continuing to work the same ground regardless of which flag flew over the settlement they sustained.\\n\\nBy the Dark Age, the Saturn's continued service on older colonial worlds was a quiet proof of the quality of the original design. The cost-effectiveness of its automation justified maintenance costs even as newer agricultural equipment options emerged, and the planets the Saturn had helped establish rarely found reason to retire it prematurely. The Universal ownership notation across production records from the Star League through the ilClan period reflects how broadly and commercially accessible the type remained throughout the Inner Sphere's political upheavals."}},"name":"Saturn Harvester","tonnage":175,"hasTurret":false,"bays":{"$k":["tag","amount","doors"],"$v":[["cargo",21.5,1],["cargo-refrigerated",10.44,1]]},"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":2,"armorAllocation":{"front":25,"left":0,"right":0,"rear":18,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":18,"frontRight":18,"rearLeft":18,"rearRight":18,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":[["combine","front","#0"],"~108",["quarters-crew",null,"#2"],["quarters-crew",null,"#3"],["quarters-crew",null,"#4"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["amphibious"],"bar":4,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"59":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Galtor Naval Yards","primaryFactory":"New Ivaarsen"},"text":{"overview":"The Seahorse Cargo Sub is a small submersible cargo carrier designed to support underwater operations across the Inner Sphere and beyond. Produced by the Galtor Naval Yards on New Ivaarsen, the Seahorse has served as a workhorse of underwater commerce and logistics since the golden years of the First Star League. Simple to operate and widely available, the design has proven remarkably durable across centuries of use, surviving the upheavals of the Succession Wars with only modest updates to its basic configuration. The most current version of the vessel continues to be assembled on the construction slips at New Ivaarsen. Though it lacks the sophistication of more advanced submersibles, the Seahorse remains a practical and reliable choice for operators working in demanding undersea environments throughout the Inner Sphere.","capabilities":"The Seahorse is driven by an internal combustion engine, giving it a cruising speed adequate for its role as a cargo carrier. Its chief limitation is dive endurance. The vessel can sustain submerged operations for approximately twelve hours before it must surface to run its engines and replenish fuel. This is typically sufficient for transit between submerged habitats, underwater farms, and the factories and depots that many planets maintain on their ocean floors. Captains operating in coastal waters with elevated threat exposure will often surface to recharge in deeper water before making the final approach, accepting the operational delay to reduce risk.\\n\\nProtection consists of a moderate layer of standard armor rated to BAR 7, sufficient to resist small arms fire and light weapons, but not designed to withstand sustained military attack. The Seahorse is not a combat vessel, and its armor reflects that reality.\\n\\nFactory specifications include a pair of torpedo launcher mounts, one to each side, and a basic fire control package. In practice the vast majority of operating examples carry no torpedoes at all. The fire control equipment proved inadequate for reliable targeting, and most operators concluded the launchers consumed space and crew attention better spent elsewhere. The empty ports are left visible rather than plated over, functioning as a passive deterrent. A Seahorse running without weapons is the norm across the Inner Sphere; a fully armed example is the exception.","deployment":"The Seahorse is found most commonly in the hands of independent owner-operators and small planetary freight companies, typically serving routes between surface ports and submerged habitats, mining stations, or underwater industrial facilities. Larger corporations with extensive undersea extraction operations maintain fleets of the type to support logistical needs that surface vessels cannot reach. The design's modest cost and simple maintenance profile make it accessible to operators who cannot support more capable submersibles. The empty torpedo ports visible on most examples serve as the vessel's primary security measure, relying on the appearance of a threat-conscious crew rather than actual firepower.","battleHistory":"The Seahorse traces its origins to the early Star League period, when the Galtor Naval Yards on New Ivaarsen developed it to meet growing demand for reliable underwater freight transport. From the outset the design prioritized simplicity and low cost over raw performance, making it accessible to independent operators and smaller planetary corporations that could not support more capable or specialized vessels. Production expanded steadily through the golden years of the Star League as planetary development pushed economic activity deeper underwater.\\n\\nThe collapse of the Star League and the onset of the Succession Wars changed the operating environment sharply. As piracy and freebooter raids spread across the Inner Sphere, even mundane cargo vessels became targets of opportunity. Responding to the threat, Galtor Naval Yards introduced torpedo launcher mounts on later production runs, fitting the Seahorse with paired torpedo installations and a basic fire control package. The intent was reasonable. The result was disappointing. Fire control proved inadequate for reliable targeting under operational conditions, and many captains reached the same conclusion independently. The launchers were more trouble than they were worth.\\n\\nA practical norm developed among Seahorse operators over the following decades. Skippers stripped the weapons from their vessels, recovering space for additional cargo and fuel and reducing crew requirements at the same time. The empty weapon ports were left open and visible rather than plated over. A vessel with bare torpedo bays signals to potential raiders that someone installed weapons here once, and that the crew knows how to think about threats. Whether the deterrent effect holds up under scrutiny is debated, but the practice became so widespread that a fully armed Seahorse is now a curiosity. The unarmed version has become the de facto standard.\\n\\nThe most recent iteration of the design was produced through the Jihad period, incorporating accumulated operator preferences without formally abandoning the weapons capability. The Galtor Naval Yards' construction slips on New Ivaarsen remained active through multiple eras of political ownership. New Ivaarsen passed from Federated Suns control to Federated Commonwealth administration during the Renaissance period, returned briefly to Federated Suns jurisdiction during the Civil War and Jihad, and eventually came under Draconis Combine administration in the Dark Age, carrying the Seahorse production line with it."}},"name":"Seahorse Cargo Sub","tonnage":250,"motiveType":"naval-sub","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":84.55,"doors":3}],"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":25,"left":21,"right":21,"rear":20,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~57","$v":[["lifeboat-maritime","#0",null,null],["lifeboat-maritime","#1",null,null],["lrt-10","#2","right",null],["lrt-10","#3","left",null],["ammo-lrt-standard","#4",null,"lrt-10"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#5",null,"lrt-10"],["seating-standard","#6",null,null],"~60",["seating-standard","#8",null,null],["seating-standard","#9",null,null],["seating-standard","#10",null,null],["seating-standard","#11",null,null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":26.5,"chassisModifications":["submersible"],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"basic"}},"60":{"tro":{"production":"~109","text":{"overview":"~110","capabilities":"~111","deployment":"The base Sherpa serves as a standard military cargo transport, deployed behind the front lines to move ammunition, rations, spare parts, and equipment to forward positions. Its rear bay door allows rapid loading and unloading in the field, and its light armor gives crews reasonable protection against stray fire during supply runs. The vehicle is found across the Inner Sphere and Periphery in militia, paramilitary, and regular rear-echelon formations that require affordable armored transport.","battleHistory":"~112"}},"quirks":"~113","name":"Sherpa Armored Truck","tonnage":35,"bays":"~29","cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~114","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["machine-gun","turret","#0",null],["~28",null,"#1","machine-gun"]]},"supportVehicle":"~115"},"61":{"tro":{"production":"~109","text":{"overview":"~110","capabilities":"~111","deployment":"The MASH variant converts the Sherpa's cargo space into a mobile surgery suite, allowing medical teams to establish a functioning field hospital close to the front lines. Both the Capellan Confederation and the Taurian Concordat employ this variant in their planetary militia structures, as do various Periphery states seeking affordable medical mobility. Its armored cab protects surgical staff from small arms and shrapnel, and its tracked mobility allows it to reposition when a front shifts.","battleHistory":"~112"}},"quirks":"~113","name":"Sherpa Armored Truck","model":"(MASH)","tonnage":35,"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~114","equipment":{"$k":"~57","$v":[["mash-core","#0",null,null],["machine-gun","#1","turret",null],["~28","#2",null,"machine-gun"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1.5,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"62":{"tro":{"production":"~109","text":{"overview":"~110","capabilities":"~111","deployment":"The Mobile Canteen replaces the standard cargo bay with a field kitchen capable of preparing and serving hot meals to infantry in the field. Both Capellan and Taurian militia formations use this variant to maintain troop morale and nutrition during prolonged operations, particularly where supply lines are stretched and field rations would otherwise be the only option. It is a common sight at forward logistics areas and base camps.","battleHistory":"~112"}},"quirks":"~113","name":"Sherpa Armored Truck","model":"(Mobile Canteen)","tonnage":35,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":1,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":"~114","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["field-kitchen","right","#0",null],["field-kitchen","left","#1",null],["trailer-hitch","rear","#2",null],["machine-gun","turret","#3",null],["~28",null,"#4","machine-gun"]]},"supportVehicle":"~115"},"63":{"tro":{"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Simca ambulance is a lightweight wheeled support vehicle introduced during the early Star League period, designed to provide rapid pre-hospital emergency medical care and patient transport for communities throughout the Inner Sphere. The vehicle's design philosophy emphasizes speed and patient capacity over protection, reflecting the expectation that ambulances operate in relatively secure environments. At 2.5 tons, it is one of the smaller wheeled support vehicles in common use, optimized for maneuvering through the streets and corridors of inhabited areas. The Simca has been a fixture of Inner Sphere civilian infrastructure since its introduction in 2607, and vehicles of its type are encountered on virtually every inhabited world regardless of faction or political status.","capabilities":"The Simca is a small-class wheeled vehicle propelled by an internal combustion engine to a cruise speed of 75 km/h, achieving a top speed of approximately 118 km/h. It mounts no weapons and carries only minimal barrier armor rated at BAR 2, providing negligible protection against anything beyond environmental hazards. Its medical capability is carried in four sets of paramedic equipment providing life support, diagnostic, and stabilization capability for patients in transit. Six passenger seats allow the vehicle to carry a driver alongside two paramedics and up to five patients, though the practical patient capacity in a trauma scenario is typically limited by the need for stretcher space.\\n\\nA siren and strobe lighting package is standard, allowing the vehicle to signal priority transit in populated areas. Cargo capacity of 136 kg accommodates supplemental medical supplies, specialized tools, and consumables. Rear bay doors facilitate rapid loading and unloading of patients. A petrochemical fuel supply provides 845 km of operating range, sufficient to cover regional response areas on most inhabited worlds. The vehicle's key limitation is its near-total lack of protection, making it entirely dependent on operating in environments where emergency vehicles are recognized and respected.","deployment":"The Simca ambulance is deployed universally in civilian emergency medical services across the Inner Sphere. It serves in urban emergency response networks, rural community medical programs, industrial facility first-response teams, and military garrison medical support roles where full field hospital equipment is not required. Its compact size and high top speed make it well-suited to initial response and patient transport in densely populated areas. The Simca is not equipped for frontline medical operations and is not expected to function in active combat environments. Military organizations typically employ this vehicle type in rear-area support and base medical functions rather than in forward combat zones.","battleHistory":"The Simca ambulance was introduced in 2607, near the midpoint of the Star League era. Emergency medical transport had always been a necessity on inhabited worlds, but the Star League period brought increased investment in civilian infrastructure that included standardization of emergency vehicle types. The Simca emerged from this environment as a straightforward, utilitarian design intended to meet the needs of small communities and secondary urban areas that could not support a more complex medical transport fleet.\\n\\nLike the Saurer-Bucher Fire Engine with which it is catalogued in the Vehicle Annex, the Simca did not carry the strategic significance that made military vehicles prime targets during the Succession Wars. Civilian ambulances continued to be manufactured and operated throughout the most destructive periods of Inner Sphere history, though manufacturing quality and maintenance standards varied considerably as industrial capacity declined. Many planets produced local variants or substitutes using the Simca specifications as a starting point, resulting in a wide range of vehicles that shared the design's basic configuration while differing in construction quality and component sourcing.\\n\\nThe Star League period saw relatively standardized production with consistent equipment quality. After the Exodus and the beginning of the Succession Wars, production fragmented among regional and local manufacturers. The vehicle increasingly became a class designation rather than a single manufacturer's product. Successive generations of emergency medical personnel trained on vehicles using the Simca's configuration regardless of the specific planet of manufacture, and its specifications became a de facto standard for light wheeled ambulances across the Inner Sphere.\\n\\nThe vehicle's basic form remained largely unchanged across the centuries, as the technology involved, an ICE-powered lightweight wheeled platform with paramedic equipment, was simple enough to manufacture without advanced industrial infrastructure. This ensured production continuity even during the worst periods of technological decline in the Succession Wars era, and the Simca remained available to communities that could no longer afford or produce more sophisticated medical equipment."}},"name":"Simca Ambulance","tonnage":2.5,"motiveType":"wheeled","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.136,"doors":1}],"era":"star-league","cruiseMP":7,"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":[["paramedic-equipment","#0",null],["paramedic-equipment","#1","right"],["paramedic-equipment","#2","left"],["paramedic-equipment","#3","rear"],"~116","~117","~118",["seating-standard","#7",null],["seating-standard","#8",null],["seating-standard","#9",null],["seating-standard","#10",null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.042,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"64":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~119","primaryFactory":"Belladonna"},"components":{"chassis":"~119"},"text":{"overview":"The Sky Eye News Helicopter is a compact civilian VTOL designed and produced by Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion on Belladonna for news-gathering and broadcast operations. Introduced in 3061, the design entered a Federated Suns market already sliding toward civil war, and initial sales were modest at best. The outbreak of the FedCom Civil War proved unexpectedly beneficial, as media organizations scrambled to acquire capable broadcast platforms for front-line coverage. Nine years after its introduction, examples of the Sky Eye could be found operating in every Successor State, a sign of the universal appeal of a design built not to fight but to document the conflicts of others. The helicopter's combination of high-quality imaging and HPG uplink capability made it uniquely valuable for real-time off-world transmission of conflict coverage.","capabilities":"The Sky Eye carries no weapons and relies entirely on its civilian status for protection in contested areas. Its real value lies in the sophisticated broadcast suite that has earned it a reputation as a miniature flying studio. The camera array combines a high-resolution imager and an infrared imager, both gyrostabilized with automatic tracking to remain focused during tight maneuvering. An onboard communications array handles mixing, pre-production, and data protocol conversion needed to deliver broadcast-quality content in any format. One ton of dedicated communications equipment enables transmission to ground stations, and a licensing agreement with INN allows direct uplink to ComStar's HPG network for near-seamless off-world broadcast of captured footage.\\n\\nThe aircraft can be flown solo, though duplicated flight controls allow a co-pilot when conditions require it. The soundproofed rear compartment provides four passenger seats for production personnel, with a standard news team typically consisting of a camera operator, a sound technician/narrator, and a production engineer. The co-axial rotor arrangement provides enhanced stability, particularly during the tight maneuvering common in urban coverage operations. A 1.2-ton cargo bay accommodates additional supplies and equipment. The ICE powerplant and limited half-ton of commercial-grade BAR 5 armor reflect its non-combatant design philosophy, prioritizing payload and range over survivability in any hostile engagement.","deployment":"The Sky Eye is produced exclusively at Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion's facility on Belladonna in the Federated Suns. No model variants have been developed or fielded. Primary operators are media agencies, commercial broadcast networks, and news organizations throughout the Inner Sphere, with distribution reaching across factional lines through commercial channels. Military and intelligence organizations have acquired limited examples drawn by the design's advanced imaging and HPG transmission capabilities. The design's non-combatant role and widespread commercial presence have allowed it to operate in territories that would normally complicate procurement of Federated Suns-manufactured equipment.","battleHistory":"Cal-Boeing of Dorwinion introduced the Sky Eye at an inopportune moment. The Federated Suns of 3061 was a realm on the verge of consuming itself, and military demands dominated procurement budgets across the Federated Commonwealth. Media agencies were cautious with capital expenditure, and initial sales proved sluggish. The design's commercial prospects appeared genuinely uncertain.\\n\\nThe outbreak of the FedCom Civil War changed the calculus entirely. News organizations across the Federated Commonwealth suddenly competed for quality footage of the conflict, and the Sky Eye's combination of onboard production capability and HPG uplink access made it the preferred platform. Domestic sales recovered quickly, and foreign agencies followed. Irian News was among the early adopters, and ComStar's Interstellar News Network later acquired the type to document the Jihad in the years that followed.\\n\\nThe Renard Reportage became one of the more prominent early users, deploying Sky Eyes to cover the fighting on New Avalon during the civil war's climax. Crews often flew with reduced personnel to get airborne faster, sacrificing broadcast quality for access. The resulting footage drew criticism from professional news organizations for its technical shortcomings, but it produced some of the war's most widely distributed imagery. The incident highlighted both the Sky Eye's flexibility and the compromises inherent in solo or under-crewed operation.\\n\\nIntelligence agencies took notice. MIIO reportedly evaluated the design's imaging systems for surveillance applications, drawn by the advanced optics and the helicopter's ability to transmit data directly through HPG infrastructure. Whether formal procurement followed has not been confirmed in open sources.\\n\\nBy 3070, nine years after its introduction, examples operated in every Successor State. The Sky Eye had transcended its Federated Suns origins to become the Inner Sphere's de facto standard for conflict journalism, a commercial success built on the back of the very war that had threatened to doom it."}},"quirks":[{"tag":"~90","parameter":"co-axial"}],"name":"Sky Eye News Helicopter","motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":1.2,"doors":1}],"era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":{"front":4,"left":3,"right":3,"rear":2,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~16","$v":["~17","~78","~19","~79","~116","~117","~118"]},"supportVehicle":"~20"},"65":{"tro":{"text":{"overview":"The small, steam-powered riverboat described here is not a military craft per se, but an example of an ultra low-tech vehicle that occasionally appears on many worlds either as a tour boat, light transport, or pleasure craft. Suitable for shallow waters, steamers have been pressed into military use over the centuries, often by scouts to covertly transport infantry troops and light gear through hostile waterways."}},"name":"Small Steamer","tonnage":2.5,"motiveType":"naval-surface","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.7,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":2,"armorAllocation":"~31","equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":["~5",["seating-pillion","#1"],["seating-pillion","#2"],["seating-pillion","#3"],["seating-pillion","#4"],["seating-pillion","#5"],["seating-pillion","#6"],["seating-pillion","#7"],["seating-pillion","#8"],["seating-pillion","#9"],["seating-pillion","#10"],["seating-pillion","#11"],["seating-pillion","#12"],["seating-pillion","#13"],["seating-pillion","#14"],["seating-pillion","#15"],["seating-pillion","#16"],["seating-pillion","#17"],["seating-pillion","#18"],["seating-pillion","#19"],["seating-pillion","#20"],["seating-pillion","#21"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"b","engine":"steam","engineTechRating":"b","fuelTons":0.21,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"b","fireControl":"none"}},"66":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Yan Manufacturing","primaryFactory":"New Samarkand"},"text":{"overview":"The SOAR (Search-Over And Rescue) VTOL was developed by Yan Manufacturing on New Samarkand and introduced in 3018 under the direction of Luthien Armor Works. The design fills a niche role in civilian emergency services and light logistics, offering an affordable and mechanically simple airframe suited to search-and-rescue operations, medical evacuation, and passenger transport in difficult terrain. Though not a combat platform, the SOAR attracted attention beyond its intended market when security and paramilitary organizations recognized its quiet propulsion system and extended range as valuable assets. Its compact construction and fuel cell powerplant make it inexpensive to operate, and its open-architecture cargo bays support a variety of mission-specific equipment configurations.","capabilities":"The SOAR is powered by an electric fuel cell engine, giving it a especially quiet operational signature compared to conventional combustion or fusion-powered airframes. This low acoustic profile is one of the design's most prized features and a key factor in its adoption by paramilitary users. The airframe uses pre-molded pocket compartments along the fuselage to accommodate extra fuel cells, extending effective range to nearly 1,000 kilometers, a substantial improvement over most civilian-tier VTOLs.\\n\\nThe vehicle can seat up to six passengers or paramedics and is equipped with a myomer tether assembly along the doorframe, allowing vertical pickups without a prepared landing zone. Active vibration control works in concert with the rotors to further reduce noise output. The SOAR cruises at a moderate pace and can sustain over 100 kilometers per hour for extended periods without straining the powerplant.\\n\\nThe design's weaknesses are closely tied to its civilian mandate. Armor protection is minimal, and any sustained hostile fire poses a serious threat. The SOAR carries no weapon systems. It is entirely dependent on escort or distance from threat for survivability in contested environments, and its open-bay configuration offers no protection to passengers against weapons effects.","deployment":"The SOAR sees its heaviest use in civilian emergency services, with search-and-rescue teams, medical evacuation units, and mountain rescue organizations operating it throughout the Draconis Combine and beyond. The Free Rasalhague Republic and Lyran Alliance both field significant numbers. In paramilitary roles, select ISF DEST teams employ the SOAR as an air cover and insertion support asset, relying on its low acoustic signature and extended range for operations where a louder or larger airframe would compromise mission security.","battleHistory":"Kenneth and Bella Yan established Yan Manufacturing in 3009 on New Samarkand, drawing on Kenneth's decades of experience as a firefighter with the New Samarkand Fire Department. The couple had long been enthusiasts of personal aviation, and they entered the chopper kit market with designs derived from the Wakazashi Airframe Ruckus-class acrobatic helicopter. Sales were modest in the early years, and the company nearly folded before an unexpected moment of publicity changed its trajectory.\\n\\nDuring the 3013 Galedon Acrobatic Tour, pilot Michael Hulis scored a near-perfect 97.23 using a Yan Stutter-class single-seat chopper, drawing widespread attention to the small firm. Demand surged in the aftermath, and the Yans expanded their workforce and production capacity. Kenneth died in 3017, and Bella subsequently sold a controlling interest to Luthien Armor Works, stipulating that LAW retain the Yan name and continue producing compact VTOL airframes. LAW honored the arrangement and immediately tasked engineers with developing a dedicated utility design to compete with Wakazashi Airframe's AirRescue model.\\n\\nThe SOAR entered service in 3018 and proved popular across multiple markets. It spread quickly in the Free Rasalhague Republic and attracted interest from the Lyran Alliance, where mountain rescue units adopted it in significant numbers. The Royal Tharkad Mountain Patrol maintains at least six SOARs in regular rotation. Some ISF DEST teams also acquired the SOAR for insertion support, using its quiet propulsion and extended range to provide overhead cover during covert operations."}},"name":"SOAR VTOL","tonnage":15,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~80","era":"late-sw-lt","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":5,"left":3,"right":3,"rear":2,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":["~120",["paramedic-equipment","left","#1"],["paramedic-equipment","rear","#2"],["paramedic-equipment","rear","#3"],"~86","~87","~88","~121","~122","~123"]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":"~81","bar":4,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"67":{"tro":{"production":"~124","text":{"overview":"~125","capabilities":"~126","deployment":"The C1 configuration is the militarized variant of the St. Christopher, featuring an armored chassis modification and heavier armor protection suitable for operations in contested airspace or near active combat zones. Intended to compete with the Karnov UR for military logistics roles, the C1 sacrifices cargo capacity for improved survivability. The Lyran Alliance Armed Forces acquired the initial production run for evaluation by the Quartermaster Corps, with plans for broader distribution to frontline formations pending operational testing results.","battleHistory":"~127"}},"quirks":"~128","name":"~129","model":"C1","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":8.55,"doors":3}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":7,"left":5,"right":5,"rear":4,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~130","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"68":{"tro":{"production":"~124","text":{"overview":"~125","capabilities":"~126","deployment":"The C2 is the civilian transport configuration, optimized for maximum cargo volume through a lighter chassis frame and reduced armor protection. Its modular cargo bay accepts factory-built modifications ranging from refrigerated and pressurized compartments to expanded passenger seating and electronic warfare suites, making it adaptable to a wide variety of non-combat roles. The Lyran Diplomatic Corps adopted the C2 for VIP shuttle services on Tharkad, and the variant attracted interest from regional leaders seeking affordable transport solutions for post-war reconstruction.","battleHistory":"~127"}},"quirks":"~128","name":"~129","model":"C2","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":12.55,"doors":3}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":{"front":6,"left":4,"right":4,"rear":3,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~130","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.5,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":4,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"69":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Grand Union Battle Works","primaryFactory":"Tikonov"},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"The Strike Falcon Attack VTOL was developed by Grand Union Battle Works on Tikonov, entering service in 2371 as the Capellan Confederation's answer to the aging OT-22 Bird of Prey rotary-wing platform. Designed to fill a demanding dual role, the Strike Falcon combined heavy infantry transport capacity with substantial fire support capability, making it one of the more versatile VTOL designs of the Age of War. Its introduction was enthusiastically received by Capellan commanders, who found its combination of speed, range, and cargo capacity well suited to the mobile warfare of the era.\\n\\nThe design's reputation spread quickly beyond Capellan borders, and multiple powers attempted to replicate its performance with varying degrees of success. Despite its eventual obsolescence, the Strike Falcon remained in frontline service for over a century before giving way to lighter, faster, and better-protected successors.","capabilities":"The Strike Falcon is a 30-ton medium VTOL built around an internal combustion engine, delivering a cruise speed of approximately 119 km/h and a flank speed of 186 km/h. Its primary offensive systems are a pair of SRM 4 launchers mounted in the nose, providing effective short-range fire against armored targets. Three machine guns, positioned in the chin and on both flanks, offer broad-arc anti-infantry suppression. An Advanced Fire Control system improves crew accuracy, partially compensating for the vehicle's relatively modest armament compared to heavier dedicated gunships.\\n\\nThe aircraft's most operationally significant feature is its large troop bay, designed to carry one fully equipped heavy infantry platoon, or two light platoons in cramped conditions, through three access doors on the right, left, and rear. The wings serve a secondary aerodynamic function, contributing significant lift in forward flight and extending the operational radius to well over 1,500 kilometers. BAR 7 armor provides adequate protection against small arms and light weapons common to the period, but leaves the vehicle dangerously exposed to any concentrated anti-armor fire. The Easy to Pilot design quirk made the Strike Falcon accessible to a broad pool of trained crews, an important advantage when qualified rotary-wing pilots were in short supply across the Confederation's expanding military.","deployment":"The Strike Falcon served across Capellan Confederation ground forces as an assault transport and fire support asset throughout the Age of War. Commanders typically deployed pairs or flights of three to insert a company-sized infantry element, with the aircraft transitioning to suppression and covering fire after troops cleared the landing zone. The design saw action along multiple contested borders, operating in coordination with ground mechanized units to exploit breakthrough opportunities and secure objectives ahead of slower-moving armor.\\n\\nAs the design aged into the mid-twenty-fourth century, Strike Falcons increasingly appeared in reserve and militia formations, replaced in frontline roles by more capable platforms. Following the 2510 obsolescence declaration, units transferred to civilian operators retained their transport capacity but were stripped of weapons and combat sensors.","battleHistory":"The helicopter's place on the battlefield was never seriously in doubt during the Age of War. When the Capellan Confederation coalesced from a collection of smaller states in 2367, Tikonov contributed not just territory but a robust defense industrial base centered on Grand Union Battle Works. The OT-22 Bird of Prey had been the backbone of Tikonov's rotary-wing forces, but it was approaching the end of its service life. Grand Union Battle Works received the commission to develop a capable successor that could meet the Confederation's demands for combined-arms flexibility.\\n\\nThe Strike Falcon entered service in 2371 and quickly earned a strong operational reputation in Capellan formations. Its ability to insert infantry directly into contested areas, then remain on station to provide immediate fire support, made it a preferred tool for battalion and regiment commanders throughout the Age of War. Few opponents could take one down with a single hit in its heyday, and the design's durability drew attention across the Inner Sphere. Several rival powers and independent manufacturers produced copies or near-copies of the Strike Falcon during the mid-twenty-fourth century, though results were mixed.\\n\\nThe aircraft's shortcomings grew more pronounced as the era progressed. Advances in weapons and armor technology increasingly outpaced the Strike Falcon's protection levels, and newer platforms offered better performance across every mission parameter. By 2510, Grand Union Battle Works officially declared the design obsolete. Surviving airframes were stripped of their combat systems and transitioned to civilian cargo and passenger roles, where some remained in operation for decades after military retirement.\\n\\nThe Grand Union Battle Works facility on Tikonov continued operating through subsequent centuries under shifting ownership. Tikonov's contested position in Inner Sphere politics brought the world through Capellan Confederation, Tikonov Free Republic, and Federated Commonwealth control during the Succession Wars and Clan Invasion eras, before returning to Capellan hands during the Civil War period. The planet later passed through independent administration, Republic of the Sphere stewardship, a brief Federated Suns interval, and ultimately back to the Confederation during the ilClan era. The factory itself, long since repurposed from Strike Falcon production, reflects that turbulent history of planetary control."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-easy-to-pilot",null],["quirk-obsolete",2510]]},"name":"Strike Falcon Attack VTOL","tonnage":30,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"troopSpace":6,"cruiseMP":7,"armorAllocation":{"front":10,"left":8,"right":8,"rear":5,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":["tag","location","uuid","feedsWeaponTag","halfTon"],"$v":[["srm-4","front","#0",null,null],["srm-4","front","#1",null,null],["machine-gun","front","#2",null,null],["machine-gun","right","#3",null,null],["machine-gun","left","#4",null,null],["ammo-srm-standard",null,"#5","srm-4",null],["~28",null,"#6","machine-gun",true]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":7,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"advanced"}},"70":{"tro":{"production":"~131","components":"~132","text":{"overview":"The Sturmvogel Gunship is the original military prototype that preceded the more widely known Maritime Patrol variant. Developed by Nashan Diversified on Furillo in 3059, the design emerged from an attempt to build a Lyran combat WiGE with meaningful offensive reach. Though rejected by Lyran Alliance Armed Forces evaluators for insufficient armor protection, the Gunship established the speed, power plant, and modular foundation that the Maritime Patrol design built upon. Small numbers entered service with Lyran militia and planetary defense forces who valued its firepower over its survivability, and the design has remained available from Nashan's Furillo facility despite never achieving the widespread adoption of its better-known sibling.","capabilities":"The Sturmvogel Gunship shares its power plant with the Maritime Patrol variant, reaching a maximum speed above 120 kilometers per hour with an unlimited patrol range. The primary difference lies in its armament. Rather than the rotary autocannon of the standard design, the Gunship mounts a light Gauss rifle, providing greater reach and accuracy at the cost of the burst-fire volume that makes the Maritime Patrol effective at close engagement ranges. Three mine dispensers remain for area-denial, though one fewer than the standard model reduces flexibility when laying barrier fields.\\n\\nThe Gunship's most pronounced weakness is its armor, which falls short even of the Maritime Patrol's already thin protection. This deficiency was the central reason LAAF evaluators rejected the design for front-line combat service. Crews compensate through the same high-speed, terrain-exploiting tactics used by Maritime Patrol crews, keeping the craft at stand-off range and avoiding direct engagement with armored opponents. For operators willing to accept the survivability trade-off, the light Gauss rifle provides a genuine long-range capability that the rotary autocannon cannot duplicate, giving the Gunship a distinct role when used in conjunction with better-protected patrol craft.","deployment":"The Sturmvogel Gunship is deployed as a fire support and interdiction asset in units lacking access to heavier WiGE combat vehicles. The light Gauss rifle gives it a stand-off advantage over the standard Maritime Patrol model, making it useful for harassing incoming waterborne forces and supporting other patrol craft from a distance. Mine dispensers provide secondary area-denial capability. Commanders typically hold the Gunship behind the engagement line, using faster and better-armed surface assets to screen it while it engages at range. Independent operations against armored opposition without support are strongly discouraged given the design's vulnerability to return fire.","battleHistory":"Nashan Diversified began development of what would become the Sturmvogel line in the late 3050s, following the Lyran Alliance's secession from the Federated Commonwealth in 3058. With access to Federated Suns-built patrol WiGEs growing uncertain, Nashan's designers set out to produce a Lyran-built replacement with genuine combat capability. The company's Furillo facility, already experienced in vehicle construction, was tasked with delivering a WiGE gunship that could compete with existing patrol craft in both speed and firepower.\\n\\nThe resulting prototype, completed in 3059, carried a light Gauss rifle and mine dispensers on a high-speed WiGE hull. LAAF evaluators were impressed by the weapon fit but concluded the armor protection was inadequate for any role involving direct engagement with enemy combat vehicles. The rejection pushed Nashan to pivot the design toward coast guard and militia markets, producing the Maritime Patrol variant with a rotary autocannon and search-and-rescue equipment in its place. The Maritime Patrol eclipsed the Gunship commercially, but Nashan continued to offer the Gunship configuration for customers who prioritized stand-off firepower over versatility.\\n\\nThe Gunship found a modest market among militia commanders defending contested coastlines who needed reach that the standard model's rotary autocannon could not provide. Production at Furillo continued alongside the Maritime Patrol line through the Civil War, the Jihad, and into the Dark Age, though the Gunship always remained the rarer of the two designs."}},"name":"Sturmvogel Gunship","model":"WiGE","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~133","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["gauss-rifle-light","front","#0",null],"~134","~135","~136",["~137",null,"#4","gauss-rifle-light"],["~137",null,"#5","gauss-rifle-light"],["~137",null,"#6","gauss-rifle-light"]]},"supportVehicle":"~138"},"71":{"tro":{"production":"~131","components":"~132","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~140","deployment":"The standard Sturmvogel is deployed as a coastal patrol and fast interception craft, most commonly with coast guard units and planetary militias. Crews use the mine dispensers to pre-deploy barrier fields in predictable pirate lanes, or release mines directly in a pursuer's path when being chased. The remote sensor dispenser gives the craft a strong search and rescue capability, and the cargo bay is ordinarily stocked with rescue equipment rather than weapon stores. The craft operates equally well over open ocean, coastal wetlands, and frozen tundra, making it effective wherever conventional watercraft cannot follow.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"~142","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":3.5,"doors":0}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~133","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["rotary-ac-2","front","#0",null],"~134","~135","~136","~143",["~18","rear","#5",null],["~144",null,"#6","rotary-ac-2"],["~144",null,"#7","rotary-ac-2"],["~144",null,"#8","rotary-ac-2"]]},"supportVehicle":"~138"},"72":{"tro":{"production":"~131","components":"~132","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~140","deployment":"The LB-10X represents the most heavily armed Sturmvogel variant. Removing the remote sensor dispenser, the cargo hold, and one mine dispenser freed enough tonnage to mount an LB 10-X autocannon in place of the lighter rotary weapon. The result is a patrol craft capable of seriously threatening even medium combat vehicles at range. This variant sacrifices nearly all of the versatility that defines the base design, making it poorly suited for rescue or interdiction work but valuable where pure firepower is the priority. It is most often found with front-line militia units facing credible armored threats along contested coastlines.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"~142","model":"(LB-10X)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~133","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["autocannon-lbx-10","front","#0",null],"~134","~135","~136",["~145",null,"#4","autocannon-lbx-10"],["~145",null,"#5","autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster",null,"#6","autocannon-lbx-10"],["ammo-is-lb-10x-cluster",null,"#7","autocannon-lbx-10"]]},"supportVehicle":"~138"},"73":{"tro":{"production":"~131","components":"~132","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~140","deployment":"The LB-2X refit trades the rotary autocannon and remote sensor dispenser for an LB 2-X autocannon, prioritizing accuracy and engagement range over burst-fire capability. This variant is more common in paramilitary and light military service where precise engagement at longer distances matters more than mine-laying support. Without the remote sensor dispenser, the LB-2X is less suited to search and rescue work, limiting its appeal for pure coast guard operations. Units that need a patrol craft with harder reach at range tend to prefer it over the standard model.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"~142","model":"(LB-2X)","tonnage":50,"motiveType":"wige","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":6,"doors":0}],"era":"civil-war","cruiseMP":8,"armorAllocation":"~133","equipment":{"$k":"~3","$v":[["autocannon-lbx-2","front","#0",null],"~134","~135","~136","~143",["ammo-is-lb-2x-standard",null,"#5","autocannon-lbx-2"],["ammo-is-lb-2x-standard",null,"#6","autocannon-lbx-2"],["ammo-is-lb-2x-cluster",null,"#7","autocannon-lbx-2"]]},"supportVehicle":"~138"},"74":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"~119"},"components":{"engine":"ICE"},"text":{"overview":"Cal-Boeing developed the Swiftran to fill a critical gap in battlefield logistics: fast, flexible transport for casualties and supplies in modern high-intensity combat. The tilt-rotor design allows vertical takeoff and landing while sustaining transit speeds suited to rapid movement between forward positions and field hospitals. The medivac variant, designated the RTC-215M, proved the most compelling configuration and secured acceptance by the Federated Commonwealth's military in 3053. Cal-Boeing subsequently licensed production to manufacturers across the Inner Sphere, excluding the Capellan Confederation, establishing the Swiftran in most major militaries and numerous civilian relief and emergency services organizations.","capabilities":"The Swiftran RTC-215M is a 25-ton VTOL built around a tilt-rotor configuration that provides both vertical takeoff capability and efficient cruise flight. The ICE powerplant delivers a practical range of approximately 950 kilometers, adequate for extended evacuation runs from forward positions to rear-area medical facilities. A rear-access ramp enables rapid loading and unloading under field conditions, even with a reduced crew.\\n\\nThe medivac fit accommodates six fully equipped stretcher positions, each with monitoring equipment, life support hardware, and medication dispensers. Ten medical personnel can work the litters in flight, and the design supports up to ten additional wounded or ambulatory passengers alongside a half-ton of cargo. The dual VTOL rotor arrangement, while responsible for the design's landing flexibility, introduces significant maintenance burden and requires precise synchronization of four engines across two separate rotating wing pairs, a system that has never been entirely trouble-free.\\n\\nProtection consists of a BAR-10 rated armored chassis carrying twenty-nine points of composite armor, distributed across the front, flanks, rear, and rotor assembly. The Swiftran carries no weapons and depends on speed, dedicated escort, and the protected status of marked medevac craft for survivability. The design's Bad Reputation and Difficult to Maintain quirks reflect a service record that has never fully escaped the shadow of early production problems.","deployment":"The RTC-215M serves primarily in dedicated casualty evacuation roles with combined-arms ground forces, positioned close enough to the forward line to respond quickly to mass casualty events while remaining outside direct fire ranges. Its rear-ramp configuration and litter stations allow a two-person medical team to load patients without assistance from the flight crew, a practical advantage during hot extractions. The design has also been deployed in disaster relief operations, urban search-and-rescue missions, and as a liaison transport for senior officers requiring rapid movement across a theater without fixed-wing infrastructure. Civilian operators use the type extensively in major population centers and in humanitarian corridors during active conflicts.","battleHistory":"Cal-Boeing's development of the Swiftran arose from a clear-eyed reading of the battlefield following the Fourth Succession War. The pace and lethality of modern combined-arms operations had made casualty evacuation a persistent crisis for every Inner Sphere power, and existing rotary-wing designs lacked either the range, capacity, or speed to meet demand. Cal-Boeing engineers proposed a tilt-rotor platform that could operate without prepared landing zones while maintaining flight profiles comparable to fixed-wing transports on long-haul runs.\\n\\nThe medivac variant, the RTC-215M, became the design's commercial and military breakthrough. Its acceptance by the AFFC in 3053 opened procurement channels across most of the Inner Sphere, and Cal-Boeing moved quickly to license production rights to regional manufacturers in every major state except the Capellan Confederation. Civilian hospitals, urban paramedic services, and disaster relief agencies adopted the type alongside military customers, and Cal-Boeing introduced passenger and cargo variants to capture additional market share.\\n\\nThe Swiftran's early service history was marked by serious reliability concerns. Before military acceptance, Cal-Boeing had struggled to resolve problems with the vertical-to-horizontal flight transition, which required four engines on two independent wing pairs to rotate in synchronized pairs. Engineers declared the problem solved before production began, but similar failures surfaced on early production airframes and contributed to three confirmed fatal accidents during the first fifteen years of service. Operators of older airframes continued to report unexplained severe vibrations, prompting mandatory airframe directives requiring early replacement of specified engine components well ahead of standard maintenance intervals. These recurring issues earned the design a lasting bad reputation among maintenance crews and a degree of caution among aircrews assigned to older examples.\\n\\nDespite these difficulties, operational demand has kept the RTC-215M in continuous production. Its combination of vertical lift, long range, and purpose-built medevac fit has proven difficult to replace, and no successor design has displaced it across the breadth of operators the Swiftran accumulated during the 3050s and 3060s. The type has served in every major conflict since its introduction, from the early Clan Invasion engagements through the Jihad, though maintenance demands escalate sharply on airframes approaching ten years of operational use."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-bad-reputation",null],"~97",["~84",null]]},"name":"Swiftran RTC-215M","tonnage":25,"motiveType":"vtol","hasTurret":false,"bays":"~15","era":"clan-inv","cruiseMP":11,"armorAllocation":{"front":8,"left":7,"right":7,"rear":5,"turret":0,"rotor":2,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~32","$v":["~120",["paramedic-equipment","right","#1"],["paramedic-equipment","left","#2"],["paramedic-equipment","left","#3"],["paramedic-equipment","rear","#4"],["paramedic-equipment","rear","#5"],"~88","~121","~122","~123",["seating-standard",null,"#10"],["seating-standard",null,"#11"],["seating-standard",null,"#12"],["seating-standard",null,"#13"],["seating-standard",null,"#14"],["seating-standard",null,"#15"],["seating-standard",null,"#16"],["seating-standard",null,"#17"],["seating-standard",null,"#18"],["seating-standard",null,"#19"],["seating-standard",null,"#20"],["seating-standard",null,"#21"],["seating-standard",null,"#22"],["seating-standard",null,"#23"],["seating-standard",null,"#24"],["seating-standard",null,"#25"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"fusion","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":1,"chassisModifications":["armored"],"bar":10,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"75":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Hegemony Research and Development Department (Weapons Division)","primaryFactory":"New Earth"},"text":{"overview":"The UTR-588 Recovery Vehicle was one of the earliest vehicles designed from the ground up for BattleMech recovery, developed by the Terran Hegemony's Research and Development Department at New Earth in direct parallel with the Mackie program itself. Where earlier recovery equipment had been adapted from commercial construction machinery, the UTR-588 was engineered specifically to handle the hundred-ton assault machines the Hegemony was bringing to the battlefield. Its ninety-ton tracked hull, fusion powerplant, and purpose-built cargo bed represented a significant investment in the support infrastructure required to keep BattleMechs operational under field conditions. The design served as a template for the category of purpose-built BattleMech recovery vehicles that would become a fixture of every major military's logistical apparatus for generations.","capabilities":"The UTR-588 is a ninety-ton tracked vehicle powered by a fusion engine, giving it the sustained power output and raw mass necessary to handle the largest BattleMechs of its era. Nominal cruise speed is 40 km/h and flank speed 60 km/h, though the Poor Performance design quirk means actual operational speed falls below those figures in practice, a characteristic attributed to primitive powerplant calibration tolerances. Ten heat sinks manage the fusion plant's waste heat, an early engineering lesson in the thermal demands of fusion-powered ground vehicles.\\n\\nHeavy tracked suspension provides the stability needed to drag disabled BattleMechs from swamps, rubble, and other difficult terrain, and the vehicle's own mass serves as counter-balance during recovery operations. Two rear-mounted lift hoists, each rated for six tons, can recover both BattleMechs and disabled vehicles. The forty-ton cargo bed features purpose-built mounting points and restraint hardware sized for a strapped-down BattleMech hull. The UTR-588 carries no weapons and is entirely reliant on escort for protection. Seven tons of BAR 6-rated armor provide reasonable resistance against incidental battle damage. The Obsolete (2530) quirk reflects the rapid advancement of recovery vehicle technology that followed the BattleMech's proliferation, as each realm developed its own more capable analogs.","deployment":"The UTR-588 was deployed exclusively with Terran Hegemony heavy assault formations during the Age of War, assigned at the regiment or battalion level to accompany assault-class BattleMech units. Its tracked configuration and heavy mass suited it to difficult terrain operations where wheeled recovery vehicles could not function effectively. The design was not exported to the Great House militaries, remaining a Hegemony-specific asset throughout its operational service. As SLDF formations absorbed surviving Hegemony assets following the Star League's founding, some UTR-588 hulls entered service with SLDF depot and training commands before the type was fully superseded by more capable recovery vehicles.","battleHistory":"The development of the UTR-588 was inseparable from the development of the Mackie. As Hegemony engineers worked on the first true BattleMech at the Weapons Division facility on New Earth, the logistical support staff recognized that no existing recovery vehicle was capable of pulling a hundred-ton combat machine out of the field conditions where it would inevitably be disabled. The decision to develop a dedicated recovery platform in parallel with the Mackie itself reflected the Hegemony's systematic approach to warfare, building the support infrastructure alongside the primary weapon system from the beginning rather than improvising solutions after the fact.\\n\\nThe UTR-588 entered service in 2439 alongside the first Mackie production examples and proved its value almost immediately. The BattleMech's combination of extreme mass and the difficult terrain it was designed to operate in made recovery operations a routine part of every major engagement. The UTR-588's tracks, counter-balancing weight, and dedicated mounting hardware made it far more effective than the improvised solutions that other realms were still relying upon, and the design gained a reputation as an essential companion to any assault formation deploying the new Mackies.\\n\\nThe rapid proliferation of BattleMechs through the Age of War created demand for newer and more capable recovery designs, and by 2530 the UTR-588 was declared obsolete as follow-on vehicles with improved performance entered production. Surviving hulls were redistributed to training commands and second-line depot roles, where many remained in service for years after the design officially left the front line. The New Earth facility continued producing spare parts until production was fully wound down. The UTR-588's legacy was the concept it proved, that a purpose-built recovery vehicle was an essential component of any force built around assault-class BattleMechs, a lesson that every major military in the Inner Sphere would eventually internalize."}},"quirks":{"$k":["tag","parameter"],"$v":[["quirk-obsolete",2530],"~146"]},"name":"UTR-588 Recovery Vehicle","tonnage":90,"hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":40,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":4,"armorAllocation":{"front":50,"left":50,"right":50,"rear":34,"turret":0,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":"~107","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":6,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}},"76":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Bigoti Seacraft","primaryFactory":"Keid","targeting":"Advanced Fire Control"},"components":{"chassis":"Bigoti Seacraft"},"text":{"overview":"The White Tip command submarine was developed by Bigoti Seacraft on Keid in 3074, serving the Word of Blake Protectorate as a mobile command platform for aquatic operations. Rather than building to standard military specifications, Bigoti Seacraft drew on its experience in the civilian shipbuilding market, producing a vessel that met uprated civilian standards at a fraction of the cost of a true military design. This practical approach delivered a platform that could function both as a mobile headquarters and as an auxiliary combatant for coastal patrol work. The White Tip relied on its submersible capability to evade hostile forces rather than on heavy armor protection, a design philosophy that made it well-suited for contested aquatic environments. Despite its modest origins, the design proved resilient enough to outlast its original patron and eventually attract commercial interest well beyond the Protectorate.","capabilities":"The White Tip is a 250-ton naval vessel designed around dual roles as a command platform and coastal combat asset. Its primary offensive capability comes from two Long Range Torpedo-15 launchers, one positioned forward and one aft, giving the vessel simultaneous coverage across both arcs. A Long Range Torpedo-10 rounds out the long-range battery, mounted in the turret alongside two Short Range Torpedo-4 launchers for close-range defense. An Extended Range Medium Laser in the bow provides a directed-energy option effective against surface targets and watercraft that may be resistant to torpedo fire.\\n\\nThe White Tip's armor is minimal by any military standard, relying on just four tons of civilian-grade material rated to BAR 8. This thin protection reflects the core design philosophy of avoidance over absorption. The vessel is intended to remain submerged and difficult to locate, not to absorb fire from dedicated combat units. A cruise speed of 3 and flank speed of 5 are unimpressive for its displacement, but underwater concealment compensates for what it lacks in straight-line performance.\\n\\nCommand facilities are substantial relative to the vessel's tonnage. Seven tons of communications equipment support a wide-spectrum communications capability, and the ship carries a MASH unit, medical quarters, and berthing for officers and support personnel. Advanced Fire Control guides the weapons suite. A 30-ton cargo hold allows extended independent deployment, reducing reliance on shore support during prolonged operations.","deployment":"The White Tip is deployed on worlds with significant aquatic terrain, where its submersible capability provides both a tactical and operational advantage. Operating as a mobile command platform, it is best suited to coordinating coastal and riverine operations from submerged positions that deny enemy forces an easy targeting solution. Long-range torpedo batteries allow engagement of surface and underwater targets at standoff distances, while the command facilities give embarked commanders the communications reach to direct dispersed forces across wide areas. The design's light protection means White Tip commanders favor submerged standoff tactics over surface engagements. Medical and personnel support facilities make extended independent deployments practical, allowing the vessel to serve as a self-sufficient mobile headquarters during prolonged coastal operations.","battleHistory":"Bigoti Seacraft was a Keid-based company with deep roots in civilian maritime construction when the Word of Blake commissioned the White Tip in 3074. The Protectorate sought an affordable command platform for aquatic environments, and Bigoti's expertise in commercial vessel construction made them a natural choice. Rather than demanding military-specification systems throughout, the Blakists accepted a design built to enhanced civilian standards. This kept unit costs manageable while delivering the capabilities the Protectorate required: submersible operations, torpedo armament, and extensive command and communications facilities.\\n\\nWhen Devlin Stone's Coalition forces arrived to liberate Keid, all six operational White Tips were deployed against the invaders. The submarines proved capable coastal defenders, using their submersible nature to strike and withdraw before Coalition forces could effectively respond. Despite the Protectorate's ultimate defeat, the performance of the White Tips was noted by Coalition commanders. The vessels demonstrated real utility as mobile command posts and coastal patrol assets, and Stone's forces committed resources to recovering and rebuilding four of the six submarines. The two destroyed in combat were beyond recovery.\\n\\nThe surviving White Tips were pressed into Coalition service, effectively transferring the design's operational legacy from the Word of Blake to what would become the Republic of the Sphere. Bigoti Seacraft, now operating under Republic governance on Keid, formalized this transition by beginning to market the White Tip to off-world customers by 3079. The design's low unit cost and proven submersible capability made it an attractive option for planetary militias and corporate security forces on aquatically active worlds.\\n\\nIn the ilClan era, Keid's ownership passed to the Capellan Confederation, bringing Bigoti Seacraft's production line under Capellan authority."}},"name":"White Tip Submarine","tonnage":250,"motiveType":"naval-sub","bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":30,"doors":1}],"era":"jihad","armorAllocation":{"front":22,"left":14,"right":14,"rear":17,"turret":20,"rotor":0,"frontLeft":0,"frontRight":0,"rearLeft":0,"rearRight":0,"turret2":0},"equipment":{"$k":"~57","$v":["~58",["field-kitchen","#1",null,null],["mash-core","#2",null,null],["srt-4","#3","front",null],["lrt-15","#4","front",null],["lrt-15","#5","rear",null],["srt-4","#6","turret",null],["lrt-10","#7","turret",null],["er-medium-laser","#8","turret",null],["ammo-srt-standard","#9",null,"srt-4"],["ammo-srt-standard","#10",null,"srt-4"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#11",null,"lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#12",null,"lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#13",null,"lrt-15"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#14",null,"lrt-10"],["ammo-lrt-standard","#15",null,"lrt-10"],["quarters-officer","#16",null,null],["quarters-officer","#17",null,null],["quarters-officer","#18",null,null],["quarters-crew","#19",null,null],["quarters-crew","#20",null,null],["quarters-crew","#21",null,null],["quarters-crew","#22",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#23",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#24",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#25",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#26",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#27",null,null],["quarters-steerage","#28",null,null]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"e","engine":"ice","engineTechRating":"e","fuelTons":0,"chassisModifications":["submersible"],"bar":8,"armorTechRating":"e","fireControl":"advanced"}},"77":{"tro":{"production":"~147","components":"~148","text":{"overview":"The Whitestreak JetSki is a lightweight personal watercraft produced by Whitestreak, Inc. on Marantha in the Magistracy of Canopus. Introduced in 2320, it predates the company's better-known Speedboat by two years and represents Whitestreak's original and most accessible product. At 100 kilograms, the JetSki is designed for a broader consumer market than the racing-focused Speedboat, prioritizing ease of use, two-person capacity, and range over outright speed. It has sold in large numbers across the Magistracy, introducing tens of thousands of Canopian citizens to high-speed water recreation. Comparable personal watercraft are available from other manufacturers throughout the Inner Sphere, but the Whitestreak JetSki remains the best-known product of its type within the Magistracy of Canopus.","capabilities":"The Whitestreak JetSki achieves a cruise speed of 70 kilometers per hour and a flank speed of 110 kilometers per hour. These figures are modest compared to the Speedboat, but they are more than sufficient for recreational use and represent genuine performance for a craft of the JetSki's scale. The electric fuel cell drive system provides 256 kilometers of range, particularly more than the heavier Speedboat, a result of the compact engine's lower power draw and the JetSki's substantially reduced mass.\\n\\nThe design seats two people, a pilot and one pillion passenger, with a combined load allowance of 50 kilograms for seated occupants beyond the pilot. A rear cargo compartment provides nine kilograms of storage, making the JetSki practical for camping and river travel where small amounts of gear need to be carried. Armor protection matches the Speedboat at BAR 2, sufficient to resist water spray and minor impacts but nothing more. Like its sibling design, the JetSki carries no weapons and has no meaningful defensive capability. Its lighter weight and tighter turning radius give it better maneuverability than the Speedboat in confined waterways, which partially compensates for the speed disadvantage in situations where open-water escape is not an option.","deployment":"The standard Whitestreak JetSki is the most widely distributed of Whitestreak's product line, found on waterways and coastlines throughout the Magistracy of Canopus. Private ownership is widespread on Canopian worlds with significant water coverage, with the design equally common in urban recreational settings and remote river environments. The rear cargo capacity drives much of the latter use, as campers and river travelers rely on the craft for transporting gear to otherwise inaccessible locations. Limited export examples have reached non-Canopian worlds where comparable products from other manufacturers are unavailable or too expensive for general civilian use.","battleHistory":"Whitestreak, Inc. launched the JetSki in 2320 as its founding product, establishing the company's identity as a manufacturer of high-speed Canopian pleasure craft before the larger Speedboat followed two years later. The JetSki was designed to be affordable and broadly accessible, aimed at ordinary Canopian citizens rather than the competitive racing community that would later embrace the Speedboat. Its commercial success helped fund the development and production of the more expensive Speedboat and cemented Whitestreak's position in the Magistracy's recreational watercraft market.\\n\\nThe JetSki found a strong following among Marantha's adventure tourism sector, particularly with campers and river enthusiasts who valued the rear cargo compartment for carrying gear upriver. The craft became a common sight on the planet's rivers and along its coastlines, and its reputation spread to other Canopian worlds with comparable water environments. The design's extended range relative to its size made it especially valued on frontier worlds where refueling infrastructure was limited.\\n\\nUnlike its sibling the Speedboat, the JetSki never developed a military or intelligence association. Its two-person capacity and minimal cargo space made it unsuitable for the kind of special operations use attributed to the Speedboat, and it remained a civilian product throughout its production history. It was occasionally pressed into service for emergency transportation and light logistics on water-rich worlds where better options were unavailable, but these uses were incidental rather than intentional. The JetSki's legacy is as a consumer product, one of the most widely distributed pleasure craft the Magistracy of Canopus has ever produced."}},"name":"Whitestreak Jetski","tonnage":0.1,"motiveType":"naval-surface","hasTurret":false,"bays":[{"tag":"cargo","amount":0.009,"doors":1}],"cruiseMP":7,"equipment":{"$k":["tag","uuid"],"$v":[["seating-pillion","#0"],["seating-pillion","#1"]]},"supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"c","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"c","fuelTons":0.001,"chassisModifications":[],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"c","fireControl":"none"}},"78":{"tro":{"production":"~147","components":"~148","text":{"overview":"The Whitestreak Speedboat is a high-speed pleasure craft produced by Whitestreak, Inc., based on Marantha in the Magistracy of Canopus. Introduced in 2322 as a companion to the company's earlier JetSki design, the Speedboat represents the flagship offering for the performance end of the recreational marine market. At 230 kilograms, it is the heavier and faster of Whitestreak's two-product line, built for thrill-seekers willing to trade comfort and cargo capacity for raw speed. The Speedboat has built a strong reputation across the Magistracy as a purpose-built racing machine, and persistent if unconfirmed rumors of its use by Magistracy Intelligence Ministry operatives have added a certain mystique to the brand. Comparable high-speed pleasure craft are produced by other manufacturers throughout the Inner Sphere, but Whitestreak's design remains the best-known of its type within Canopian space.","capabilities":"The Whitestreak Speedboat achieves a cruise speed of 120 kilometers per hour and a flank speed of 180 kilometers per hour, placing it at the top of the small civilian watercraft performance envelope. Power comes from an electric fuel cell drive system that provides 194 kilometers of range before requiring refueling, a figure adequate for racing circuits and coastal runs but limiting for extended open-water travel. The Ultra-Light Chassis Modification keeps total mass to 230 kilograms, a design choice that contributes directly to the craft's performance but leaves very little room for structural reinforcement.\\n\\nThe Speedboat carries a single crew member in a forward seat rated for up to 75 kilograms. It has no passenger accommodation and no cargo capacity. Armor protection consists of a thin barrier layer rated at BAR 2, sufficient to resist spray and minor collision damage but entirely inadequate against deliberate attack. This near-total lack of defensive capability is the design's most significant shortcoming. Any Speedboat caught in a hostile situation depends entirely on its speed to disengage, and its fuel range constrains how far that escape can carry it before resupply becomes necessary.","deployment":"The standard Whitestreak Speedboat serves as a recreational racing and high-speed leisure craft throughout the Magistracy of Canopus. It is primarily found on Marantha and other Canopian worlds with significant coastal and open-water environments, where it participates in organized racing circuits and is owned by private enthusiasts. The design's relatively simple fuel cell construction requires minimal manufacturing sophistication, and production has continued largely uninterrupted since its introduction. The craft is occasionally encountered in private hands outside the Magistracy, though it remains most common within Canopian space.","battleHistory":"Whitestreak, Inc. established itself on Marantha in the early twenty-fourth century, growing from a small recreational watercraft producer into a fixture of Canopian leisure culture. The Speedboat, introduced two years after the company's debut JetSki model, targeted a different customer entirely, aimed at racing enthusiasts and adrenaline seekers willing to pay for a purpose-built performance machine. Annual races held on Marantha's Oblivion Sea became a cultural institution, drawing competitors from across the Magistracy and providing Whitestreak with free advertising through spectacle and, on occasion, tragedy. The high accident rate at these events was an accepted cost of designing for maximum performance rather than safety.\\n\\nThe Speedboat gained a second reputation beyond recreational use in the decades following its introduction. Persistent but unsubstantiated reports emerged of Magistracy Intelligence Ministry Special Forces using Whitestreak-style speedboats to rapidly approach coastal targets on water-rich worlds. The MIM denied all such reports consistently. Regardless of official denials, the craft's combination of high speed, minimal electronic profile, and low visual signature made it theoretically well-suited for covert insertion operations. This association eventually led to the development of a dedicated intelligence ministry variant, though the details of its configuration remained officially classified.\\n\\nThe Speedboat competes in a market alongside other Inner Sphere pleasure craft. Corean Enterprises' Poseidon hydrofoil carries several passengers at comparable speeds, though at substantially higher cost. StarCorps Industries' Sundiver offers the unique capability to travel briefly underwater at reduced speed. Against these alternatives, the Whitestreak holds its position through competitive pricing, strong brand recognition within the Magistracy, and a top-end performance figure that neither competitor matches in a straight race."}},"name":"Whitestreak Speedboat","tonnage":0.23,"motiveType":"naval-surface","hasTurret":false,"cruiseMP":12,"equipment":"~82","supportVehicle":{"structuralTechRating":"d","engine":"fuel-cell","engineTechRating":"d","fuelTons":0.004,"chassisModifications":["ultra-light"],"bar":2,"armorTechRating":"d","fireControl":"none"}}}`)};export{e as default};